NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000046 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000047 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000048 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000049 "=head1 NAME"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000050 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000051 "Excel::Writer::XLSX - Create a new file in the Excel 2007+ XLSX format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000052 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000053 "=head1 SYNOPSIS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000054 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000055 "To write a string, a formatted string, a number and a formula to the first worksheet in an Excel workbook called perl.xlsx:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000056 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000057 "    use Excel::Writer::XLSX;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000058 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000059 "    # Create a new Excel workbook"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000060 "    my $workbook = Excel::Writer::XLSX->new( 'perl.xlsx' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000061 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000062 "    # Add a worksheet"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000063 "    $worksheet = $workbook->add_worksheet();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000064 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000065 "    #  Add and define a format"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000066 "    $format = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000067 "    $format->set_bold();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000068 "    $format->set_color( 'red' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000069 "    $format->set_align( 'center' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000070 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000071 "    # Write a formatted and unformatted string, row and column notation."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000072 "    $col = $row = 0;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000073 "    $worksheet->write( $row, $col, 'Hi Excel!', $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000074 "    $worksheet->write( 1, $col, 'Hi Excel!' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000075 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000076 "    # Write a number and a formula using A1 notation"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000077 "    $worksheet->write( 'A3', 1.2345 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000078 "    $worksheet->write( 'A4', '=SIN(PI()/4)' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000079 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000080 "    $workbook->close();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000081 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000082 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000083 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000084 "=head1 DESCRIPTION"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000085 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000086 "The C<Excel::Writer::XLSX> module can be used to create an Excel file in the 2007+ XLSX format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000087 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000088 "Multiple worksheets can be added to a workbook and formatting can be applied to cells. Text, numbers, and formulas can be written to the cells."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000089 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000090 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000091 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000092 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000093 "=head1 Excel::Writer::XLSX and Spreadsheet::WriteExcel"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000094 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000095 "C<Excel::Writer::XLSX> uses the same interface as the L<Spreadsheet::WriteExcel> module which produces an Excel file in binary XLS format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000096 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000097 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000098 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000099 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000100 "=head1 QUICK START"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000101 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000102 "Excel::Writer::XLSX tries to provide an interface to as many of Excel's features as possible. As a result there is a lot of documentation to accompany the interface and it can be difficult at first glance to see what it important and what is not. So for those of you who prefer to assemble Ikea furniture first and then read the instructions, here are four easy steps:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000103 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000104 "1. Create a new Excel I<workbook> (i.e. file) using C<new()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000105 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000106 "2. Add a worksheet to the new workbook using C<add_worksheet()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000107 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000108 "3. Write to the worksheet using C<write()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000109 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000110 "4. C<close()> the file."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000111 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000112 "Like this:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000113 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000114 "    use Excel::Writer::XLSX;                                   # Step 0"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000115 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000116 "    my $workbook = Excel::Writer::XLSX->new( 'perl.xlsx' );    # Step 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000117 "    $worksheet = $workbook->add_worksheet();                   # Step 2"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000118 "    $worksheet->write( 'A1', 'Hi Excel!' );                    # Step 3"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000119 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000120 "    $workbook->close();                                        # Step 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000121 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000122 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000123 "This will create an Excel file called C<perl.xlsx> with a single worksheet and the text C<'Hi Excel!'> in the relevant cell. And that's it. Okay, so there is actually a zeroth step as well, but C<use module> goes without saying. There are many examples that come with the distribution and which you can use to get you started. See L</EXAMPLES>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000124 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000125 "Those of you who read the instructions first and assemble the furniture afterwards will know how to proceed. ;-)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000126 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000127 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000128 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000129 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000130 "=head1 WORKBOOK METHODS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000131 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000132 "The Excel::Writer::XLSX module provides an object oriented interface to a new Excel workbook. The following methods are available through a new workbook."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000133 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000134 "    new()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000135 "    add_worksheet()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000136 "    add_format()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000137 "    add_chart()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000138 "    add_shape()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000139 "    add_vba_project()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000140 "    set_vba_name()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000141 "    close()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000142 "    set_properties()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000143 "    set_custom_property()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000144 "    define_name()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000145 "    set_tempdir()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000146 "    set_custom_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000147 "    sheets()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000148 "    get_worksheet_by_name()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000149 "    set_1904()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000150 "    set_optimization()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000151 "    set_calc_mode()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000152 "    get_default_url_format()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000153 "    read_only_recommended()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000154 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000155 "If you are unfamiliar with object oriented interfaces or the way that they are implemented in Perl have a look at C<perlobj> and C<perltoot> in the main Perl documentation."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000156 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000157 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000158 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000159 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000160 "=head2 new()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000161 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000162 "A new Excel workbook is created using the C<new()> constructor which accepts either a filename or a filehandle as a parameter. The following example creates a new Excel file based on a filename:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000163 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000164 "    my $workbook  = Excel::Writer::XLSX->new( 'filename.xlsx' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000165 "    my $worksheet = $workbook->add_worksheet();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000166 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 0, 'Hi Excel!' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000167 "    $workbook->close();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000168 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000169 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000170 "Here are some other examples of using C<new()> with filenames:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000171 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000172 "    my $workbook1 = Excel::Writer::XLSX->new( $filename );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000173 "    my $workbook2 = Excel::Writer::XLSX->new( '/tmp/filename.xlsx' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000174 "    my $workbook3 = Excel::Writer::XLSX->new( \"c:\\\\tmp\\\\filename.xlsx\" );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000175 "    my $workbook4 = Excel::Writer::XLSX->new( 'c:\\tmp\\filename.xlsx' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000176 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000177 "The last two examples demonstrates how to create a file on DOS or Windows where it is necessary to either escape the directory separator C<\\> or to use single quotes to ensure that it isn't interpolated. For more information see C<perlfaq5: Why can't I use \"C:\\temp\\foo\" in DOS paths?>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000178 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000179 "It is recommended that the filename uses the extension C<.xlsx> rather than C<.xls> since the latter causes an Excel warning when used with the XLSX format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000180 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000181 "The C<new()> constructor returns a Excel::Writer::XLSX object that you can use to add worksheets and store data. It should be noted that although C<my> is not specifically required it defines the scope of the new workbook variable and, in the majority of cases, ensures that the workbook is closed properly without explicitly calling the C<close()> method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000182 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000183 "If the file cannot be created, due to file permissions or some other reason,  C<new> will return C<undef>. Therefore, it is good practice to check the return value of C<new> before proceeding. As usual the Perl variable C<$!> will be set if there is a file creation error. You will also see one of the warning messages detailed in L</DIAGNOSTICS>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000184 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000185 "    my $workbook = Excel::Writer::XLSX->new( 'protected.xlsx' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000186 "    die \"Problems creating new Excel file: $!\" unless defined $workbook;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000187 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000188 "You can also pass a valid filehandle to the C<new()> constructor. For example in a CGI program you could do something like this:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000189 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000190 "    binmode( STDOUT );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000191 "    my $workbook = Excel::Writer::XLSX->new( \\*STDOUT );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000192 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000193 "The requirement for C<binmode()> is explained below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000194 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000195 "See also, the C<cgi.pl> program in the C<examples> directory of the distro."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000196 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000197 "In C<mod_perl> programs where you will have to do something like the following:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000198 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000199 "    # mod_perl 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000200 "    ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000201 "    tie *XLSX, 'Apache';"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000202 "    binmode( XLSX );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000203 "    my $workbook = Excel::Writer::XLSX->new( \\*XLSX );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000204 "    ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000205 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000206 "    # mod_perl 2"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000207 "    ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000208 "    tie *XLSX => $r;    # Tie to the Apache::RequestRec object"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000209 "    binmode( *XLSX );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000210 "    my $workbook = Excel::Writer::XLSX->new( \\*XLSX );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000211 "    ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000212 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000213 "See also, the C<mod_perl1.pl> and C<mod_perl2.pl> programs in the C<examples> directory of the distro."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000214 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000215 "Filehandles can also be useful if you want to stream an Excel file over a socket or if you want to store an Excel file in a scalar."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000216 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000217 "For example here is a way to write an Excel file to a scalar:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000218 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000219 "    #!/usr/bin/perl -w"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000220 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000221 "    use strict;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000222 "    use Excel::Writer::XLSX;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000223 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000224 "    open my $fh, '>', \\my $str or die \"Failed to open filehandle: $!\";"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000225 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000226 "    my $workbook  = Excel::Writer::XLSX->new( $fh );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000227 "    my $worksheet = $workbook->add_worksheet();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000228 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000229 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 0, 'Hi Excel!' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000230 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000231 "    $workbook->close();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000232 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000233 "    # The Excel file in now in $str. Remember to binmode() the output"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000234 "    # filehandle before printing it."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000235 "    binmode STDOUT;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000236 "    print $str;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000237 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000238 "See also the C<write_to_scalar.pl> and C<filehandle.pl> programs in the C<examples> directory of the distro."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000239 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000240 "B<Note about the requirement for> C<binmode()>. An Excel file is comprised of binary data. Therefore, if you are using a filehandle you should ensure that you C<binmode()> it prior to passing it to C<new()>.You should do this regardless of whether you are on a Windows platform or not."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000241 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000242 "You don't have to worry about C<binmode()> if you are using filenames instead of filehandles. Excel::Writer::XLSX performs the C<binmode()> internally when it converts the filename to a filehandle. For more information about C<binmode()> see C<perlfunc> and C<perlopentut> in the main Perl documentation."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000243 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000244 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000245 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000246 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000247 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000248 "=head2 add_worksheet( $sheetname )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000249 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000250 "At least one worksheet should be added to a new workbook. A worksheet is used to write data into cells:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000251 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000252 "    $worksheet1 = $workbook->add_worksheet();               # Sheet1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000253 "    $worksheet2 = $workbook->add_worksheet( 'Foglio2' );    # Foglio2"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000254 "    $worksheet3 = $workbook->add_worksheet( 'Data' );       # Data"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000255 "    $worksheet4 = $workbook->add_worksheet();               # Sheet4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000256 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000257 "If C<$sheetname> is not specified the default Excel convention will be followed, i.e. Sheet1, Sheet2, etc."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000258 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000259 "The worksheet name must be a valid Excel worksheet name, i.e:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000260 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000261 "=over"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000262 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000263 "=item * It must be less than 32 characters."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000264 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000265 "=item * It cannot contain any of the following characters: C<[ ] : * ? / \\>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000266 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000267 "=item * It cannot start or end with an apostrophe."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000268 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000269 "=item * It cannot be the same as an existing worksheet name (or a case insensitive variant)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000270 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000271 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000272 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000273 "Note, the sheetname should not be \"History\" (case insensitive) which is reserved in English language versions of Excel. Non-English versions may have restrictions on the equivalent word."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000274 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000275 "See the Excel worksheet naming rules at L<https://support.office.com/en-ie/article/rename-a-worksheet-3f1f7148-ee83-404d-8ef0-9ff99fbad1f9>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000276 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000277 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000278 "=head2 add_format( %properties )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000279 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000280 "The C<add_format()> method can be used to create new Format objects which are used to apply formatting to a cell. You can either define the properties at creation time via a hash of property values or later via method calls."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000281 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000282 "    $format1 = $workbook->add_format( %props );    # Set properties at creation"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000283 "    $format2 = $workbook->add_format();            # Set properties later"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000284 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000285 "See the L</CELL FORMATTING> section for more details about Format properties and how to set them."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000286 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000287 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000288 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000289 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000290 "=head2 add_chart( %properties )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000291 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000292 "This method is use to create a new chart either as a standalone worksheet (the default) or as an embeddable object that can be inserted into a worksheet via the C<insert_chart()> Worksheet method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000293 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000294 "    my $chart = $workbook->add_chart( type => 'column' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000295 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000296 "The properties that can be set are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000297 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000298 "    type     (required)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000299 "    subtype  (optional)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000300 "    name     (optional)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000301 "    embedded (optional)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000302 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000303 "=over"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000304 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000305 "=item * C<type>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000306 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000307 "This is a required parameter. It defines the type of chart that will be created."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000308 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000309 "    my $chart = $workbook->add_chart( type => 'line' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000310 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000311 "The available types are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000312 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000313 "    area"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000314 "    bar"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000315 "    column"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000316 "    line"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000317 "    pie"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000318 "    doughnut"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000319 "    scatter"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000320 "    stock"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000321 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000322 "=item * C<subtype>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000323 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000324 "Used to define a chart subtype where available."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000325 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000326 "    my $chart = $workbook->add_chart( type => 'bar', subtype => 'stacked' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000327 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000328 "See the L<Excel::Writer::XLSX::Chart> documentation for a list of available chart subtypes."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000329 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000330 "=item * C<name>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000331 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000332 "Set the name for the chart sheet. The name property is optional and if it isn't supplied will default to C<Chart1 .. n>. The name must be a valid Excel worksheet name. See C<add_worksheet()> for more details on valid sheet names. The C<name> property can be omitted for embedded charts."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000333 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000334 "    my $chart = $workbook->add_chart( type => 'line', name => 'Results Chart' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000335 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000336 "=item * C<embedded>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000337 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000338 "Specifies that the Chart object will be inserted in a worksheet via the C<insert_chart()> Worksheet method. It is an error to try insert a Chart that doesn't have this flag set."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000339 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000340 "    my $chart = $workbook->add_chart( type => 'line', embedded => 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000341 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000342 "    # Configure the chart."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000343 "    ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000344 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000345 "    # Insert the chart into the a worksheet."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000346 "    $worksheet->insert_chart( 'E2', $chart );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000347 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000348 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000349 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000350 "See Excel::Writer::XLSX::Chart for details on how to configure the chart object once it is created. See also the C<chart_*.pl> programs in the examples directory of the distro."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000351 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000352 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000353 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000354 "=head2 add_shape( %properties )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000355 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000356 "The C<add_shape()> method can be used to create new shapes that may be inserted into a worksheet."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000357 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000358 "You can either define the properties at creation time via a hash of property values or later via method calls."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000359 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000360 "    # Set properties at creation."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000361 "    $plus = $workbook->add_shape("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000362 "        type   => 'plus',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000363 "        id     => 3,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000364 "        width  => $pw,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000365 "        height => $ph"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000366 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000367 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000368 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000369 "    # Default rectangle shape. Set properties later."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000370 "    $rect =  $workbook->add_shape();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000371 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000372 "See L<Excel::Writer::XLSX::Shape> for details on how to configure the shape object once it is created."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000373 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000374 "See also the C<shape*.pl> programs in the examples directory of the distro."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000375 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000376 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000377 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000378 "=head2 add_vba_project( 'vbaProject.bin' )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000379 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000380 "The C<add_vba_project()> method can be used to add macros or functions to an Excel::Writer::XLSX file using a binary VBA project file that has been extracted from an existing Excel C<xlsm> file."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000381 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000382 "    my $workbook  = Excel::Writer::XLSX->new( 'file.xlsm' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000383 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000384 "    $workbook->add_vba_project( './vbaProject.bin' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000385 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000386 "The supplied C<extract_vba> utility can be used to extract the required C<vbaProject.bin> file from an existing Excel file:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000387 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000388 "    $ extract_vba file.xlsm"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000389 "    Extracted 'vbaProject.bin' successfully"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000390 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000391 "Macros can be tied to buttons using the worksheet C<insert_button()> method (see the L</WORKSHEET METHODS> section for details):"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000392 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000393 "    $worksheet->insert_button( 'C2', { macro => 'my_macro' } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000394 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000395 "Note, Excel uses the file extension C<xlsm> instead of C<xlsx> for files that contain macros. It is advisable to follow the same convention."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000396 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000397 "See also the C<macros.pl> example file and the L<WORKING WITH VBA MACROS>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000398 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000399 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000400 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000401 "=head2 set_vba_name()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000402 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000403 "The C<set_vba_name()> method can be used to set the VBA codename for the workbook. This is sometimes required when a C<vbaProject macro> included via C<add_vba_project()> refers to the workbook. The default Excel VBA name of C<ThisWorkbook> is used if a user defined name isn't specified. See also L<WORKING WITH VBA MACROS>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000404 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000405 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000406 "=head2 close()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000407 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000408 "In general your Excel file will be closed automatically when your program ends or when the Workbook object goes out of scope. However it is recommended to explicitly call the C<close()> method close the Excel file and avoid the potential issues outlined below. The C<close()> method is called like this:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000409 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000410 "    $workbook->close();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000411 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000412 "The return value of C<close()> is the same as that returned by perl when it closes the file created by C<new()>. This allows you to handle error conditions in the usual way:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000413 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000414 "    $workbook->close() or die \"Error closing file: $!\";"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000415 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000416 "An explicit C<close()> is required if the file must be closed prior to performing some external action on it such as copying it, reading its size or attaching it to an email."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000417 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000418 "In addition, C<close()> may be required to prevent perl's garbage collector from disposing of the Workbook, Worksheet and Format objects in the wrong order. Situations where this can occur are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000419 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000420 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000421 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000422 "=item *"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000423 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000424 "If C<my()> was not used to declare the scope of a workbook variable created using C<new()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000425 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000426 "=item *"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000427 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000428 "If the C<new()>, C<add_worksheet()> or C<add_format()> methods are called in subroutines."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000429 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000430 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000431 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000432 "The reason for this is that Excel::Writer::XLSX relies on Perl's C<DESTROY> mechanism to trigger destructor methods in a specific sequence. This may not happen in cases where the Workbook, Worksheet and Format variables are not lexically scoped or where they have different lexical scopes."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000433 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000434 "To avoid these issues it is recommended that you always close the Excel::Writer::XLSX filehandle using C<close()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000435 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000436 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000437 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000438 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000439 "=head2 set_size( $width, $height )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000440 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000441 "The C<set_size()> method can be used to set the size of a workbook window."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000442 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000443 "    $workbook->set_size(1200, 800);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000444 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000445 "The Excel window size was used in Excel 2007 to define the width and height of a workbook window within the Multiple Document Interface (MDI). In later versions of Excel for Windows this interface was dropped. This method is currently only useful when setting the window size in Excel for Mac 2011. The units are pixels and the default size is 1073 x 644."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000446 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000447 "Note, this doesn't equate exactly to the Excel for Mac pixel size since it is based on the original Excel 2007 for Windows sizing."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000448 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000449 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000450 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000451 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000452 "=head2 set_tab_ratio( $tab_ratio )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000453 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000454 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000455 "The C<set_tab_ratio()> method can be used to set the ratio between worksheet tabs and the horizontal slider at the bottom of a workbook. This can be increased to give more room to the tabs or reduced to increase the size of the horizontal slider:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000456 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000457 "    $workbook->set_tab_ratio(75);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000458 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000459 "The default value in Excel is 60."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000460 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000461 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000462 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000463 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000464 "=head2 set_properties()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000465 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000466 "The C<set_properties> method can be used to set the document properties of the Excel file created by C<Excel::Writer::XLSX>. These properties are visible when you use the C<< Office Button -> Prepare -> Properties >> option in Excel and are also available to external applications that read or index Windows files."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000467 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000468 "The properties should be passed in hash format as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000469 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000470 "    $workbook->set_properties("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000471 "        title    => 'This is an example spreadsheet',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000472 "        author   => 'John McNamara',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000473 "        comments => 'Created with Perl and Excel::Writer::XLSX',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000474 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000475 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000476 "The properties that can be set are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000477 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000478 "    title"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000479 "    subject"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000480 "    author"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000481 "    manager"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000482 "    company"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000483 "    category"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000484 "    keywords"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000485 "    comments"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000486 "    status"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000487 "    hyperlink_base"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000488 "    created - File create date. Should be an aref of gmtime() values."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000489 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000490 "See also the C<properties.pl> program in the examples directory of the distro."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000491 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000492 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000493 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000494 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000495 "=head2 set_custom_property( $name, $value, $type)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000496 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000497 "The C<set_custom_property> method can be used to set one of more custom document properties not covered by the C<set_properties()> method above. These properties are visible when you use the C<< Office Button -> Prepare -> Properties -> Advanced Properties -> Custom >> option in Excel and are also available to external applications that read or index Windows files."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000498 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000499 "The C<set_custom_property> method takes 3 parameters:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000500 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000501 "    $workbook-> set_custom_property( $name, $value, $type);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000502 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000503 "Where the available types are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000504 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000505 "    text"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000506 "    date"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000507 "    number"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000508 "    bool"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000509 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000510 "For example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000511 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000512 "    $workbook->set_custom_property( 'Checked by',      'Eve',                  'text'   );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000513 "    $workbook->set_custom_property( 'Date completed',  '2016-12-12T23:00:00Z', 'date'   );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000514 "    $workbook->set_custom_property( 'Document number', '12345' ,               'number' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000515 "    $workbook->set_custom_property( 'Reference',       '1.2345',               'number' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000516 "    $workbook->set_custom_property( 'Has review',      1,                      'bool'   );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000517 "    $workbook->set_custom_property( 'Signed off',      0,                      'bool'   );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000518 "    $workbook->set_custom_property( 'Department',      $some_string,           'text'   );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000519 "    $workbook->set_custom_property( 'Scale',           '1.2345678901234',      'number' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000520 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000521 "Dates should by in ISO8601 C<yyyy-mm-ddThh:mm:ss.sssZ> date format in Zulu time, as shown above."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000522 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000523 "The C<text> and C<number> types are optional since they can usually be inferred from the data:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000524 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000525 "    $workbook->set_custom_property( 'Checked by', 'Eve'    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000526 "    $workbook->set_custom_property( 'Reference',  '1.2345' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000527 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000528 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000529 "The C<$name> and C<$value> parameters are limited to 255 characters by Excel."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000530 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000531 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000532 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000533 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000534 "=head2 define_name()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000535 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000536 "This method is used to defined a name that can be used to represent a value, a single cell or a range of cells in a workbook."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000537 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000538 "For example to set a global/workbook name:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000539 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000540 "    # Global/workbook names."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000541 "    $workbook->define_name( 'Exchange_rate', '=0.96' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000542 "    $workbook->define_name( 'Sales',         '=Sheet1!$G$1:$H$10' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000543 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000544 "It is also possible to define a local/worksheet name by prefixing the name with the sheet name using the syntax C<sheetname!definedname>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000545 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000546 "    # Local/worksheet name."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000547 "    $workbook->define_name( 'Sheet2!Sales',  '=Sheet2!$G$1:$G$10' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000548 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000549 "If the sheet name contains spaces or special characters you must enclose it in single quotes like in Excel:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000550 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000551 "    $workbook->define_name( \"'New Data'!Sales\",  '=Sheet2!$G$1:$G$10' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000552 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000553 "See the defined_name.pl program in the examples dir of the distro."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000554 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000555 "Refer to the following to see Excel's syntax rules for defined names: L<http://office.microsoft.com/en-001/excel-help/define-and-use-names-in-formulas-HA010147120.aspx#BMsyntax_rules_for_names>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000556 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000557 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000558 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000559 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000560 "=head2 set_tempdir()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000561 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000562 "C<Excel::Writer::XLSX> stores worksheet data in temporary files prior to assembling the final workbook."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000563 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000564 "The C<File::Temp> module is used to create these temporary files. File::Temp uses C<File::Spec> to determine an appropriate location for these files such as C</tmp> or C<c:\\windows\\temp>. You can find out which directory is used on your system as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000565 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000566 "    perl -MFile::Spec -le \"print File::Spec->tmpdir()\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000567 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000568 "If the default temporary file directory isn't accessible to your application, or doesn't contain enough space, you can specify an alternative location using the C<set_tempdir()> method:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000569 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000570 "    $workbook->set_tempdir( '/tmp/writeexcel' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000571 "    $workbook->set_tempdir( 'c:\\windows\\temp\\writeexcel' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000572 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000573 "The directory for the temporary file must exist, C<set_tempdir()> will not create a new directory."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000574 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000575 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000576 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000577 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000578 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000579 "=head2 set_custom_color( $index, $red, $green, $blue )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000580 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000581 "The method is maintained for backward compatibility with Spreadsheet::WriteExcel. Excel::Writer::XLSX programs don't require this method and colours can be specified using a Html style C<#RRGGBB> value, see L</WORKING WITH COLOURS>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000582 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000583 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000584 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000585 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000586 "=head2 sheets( 0, 1, ... )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000587 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000588 "The C<sheets()> method returns a list, or a sliced list, of the worksheets in a workbook."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000589 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000590 "If no arguments are passed the method returns a list of all the worksheets in the workbook. This is useful if you want to repeat an operation on each worksheet:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000591 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000592 "    for $worksheet ( $workbook->sheets() ) {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000593 "        print $worksheet->get_name();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000594 "    }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000595 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000596 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000597 "You can also specify a slice list to return one or more worksheet objects:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000598 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000599 "    $worksheet = $workbook->sheets( 0 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000600 "    $worksheet->write( 'A1', 'Hello' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000601 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000602 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000603 "Or since the return value from C<sheets()> is a reference to a worksheet object you can write the above example as:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000604 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000605 "    $workbook->sheets( 0 )->write( 'A1', 'Hello' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000606 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000607 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000608 "The following example returns the first and last worksheet in a workbook:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000609 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000610 "    for $worksheet ( $workbook->sheets( 0, -1 ) ) {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000611 "        # Do something"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000612 "    }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000613 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000614 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000615 "Array slices are explained in the C<perldata> manpage."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000616 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000617 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000618 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000619 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000620 "=head2 get_worksheet_by_name()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000621 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000622 "The C<get_worksheet_by_name()> function return a worksheet or chartsheet object in the workbook using the sheetname:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000623 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000624 "    $worksheet = $workbook->get_worksheet_by_name('Sheet1');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000625 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000626 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000627 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000628 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000629 "=head2 set_1904()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000630 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000631 "Excel stores dates as real numbers where the integer part stores the number of days since the epoch and the fractional part stores the percentage of the day. The epoch can be either 1900 or 1904. Excel for Windows uses 1900 and Excel for Macintosh uses 1904. However, Excel on either platform will convert automatically between one system and the other."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000632 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000633 "Excel::Writer::XLSX stores dates in the 1900 format by default. If you wish to change this you can call the C<set_1904()> workbook method. You can query the current value by calling the C<get_1904()> workbook method. This returns 0 for 1900 and 1 for 1904."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000634 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000635 "See also L</DATES AND TIME IN EXCEL> for more information about working with Excel's date system."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000636 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000637 "In general you probably won't need to use C<set_1904()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000638 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000639 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000640 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000641 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000642 "=head2 set_optimization()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000643 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000644 "The C<set_optimization()> method is used to turn on optimizations in the Excel::Writer::XLSX module. Currently there is only one optimization available and that is to reduce memory usage."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000645 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000646 "    $workbook->set_optimization();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000647 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000648 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000649 "See L</SPEED AND MEMORY USAGE> for more background information."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000650 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000651 "Note, that with this optimization turned on a row of data is written and then discarded when a cell in a new row is added via one of the Worksheet C<write_*()> methods. As such data should be written in sequential row order once the optimization is turned on."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000652 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000653 "This method must be called before any calls to C<add_worksheet()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000654 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000655 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000656 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000657 "=head2 set_calc_mode( $mode )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000658 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000659 "Set the calculation mode for formulas in the workbook. This is mainly of use for workbooks with slow formulas where you want to allow the user to calculate them manually."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000660 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000661 "The mode parameter can be one of the following strings:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000662 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000663 "=over"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000664 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000665 "=item C<auto>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000666 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000667 "The default. Excel will re-calculate formulas when a formula or a value affecting the formula changes."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000668 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000669 "=item C<manual>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000670 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000671 "Only re-calculate formulas when the user requires it. Generally by pressing F9."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000672 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000673 "=item C<auto_except_tables>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000674 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000675 "Excel will automatically re-calculate formulas except for tables."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000676 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000677 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000678 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000679 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000680 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000681 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000682 "=head2 get_default_url_format()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000683 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000684 "The C<get_default_url_format()> method gets a copy of the default url format used when a user defined format isn't specified with the worksheet C<write_url()> method. The format is the hyperlink style defined by Excel for the default theme:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000685 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000686 "    my $url_format = $workbook->get_default_url_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000687 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000688 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000689 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000690 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000691 "=head2 read_only_recommended()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000692 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000693 "The C<read_only_recommended()> method can be used to set the Excel \"Read-only Recommended\" option that is available when saving a file. This presents the user of the file with an option to open it in \"read-only\" mode. This means that any changes to the file can't be saved back to the same file and must be saved to a new file. It can be set as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000694 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000695 "    $workbook->read_only_recommended();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000696 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000697 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000698 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000699 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000700 "=head1 WORKSHEET METHODS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000701 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000702 "A new worksheet is created by calling the C<add_worksheet()> method from a workbook object:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000703 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000704 "    $worksheet1 = $workbook->add_worksheet();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000705 "    $worksheet2 = $workbook->add_worksheet();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000706 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000707 "The following methods are available through a new worksheet:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000708 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000709 "    write()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000710 "    write_number()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000711 "    write_string()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000712 "    write_rich_string()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000713 "    keep_leading_zeros()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000714 "    write_blank()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000715 "    write_row()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000716 "    write_col()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000717 "    write_date_time()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000718 "    write_url()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000719 "    write_url_range()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000720 "    write_formula()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000721 "    write_boolean()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000722 "    write_comment()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000723 "    show_comments()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000724 "    set_comments_author()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000725 "    add_write_handler()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000726 "    insert_image()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000727 "    embed_image()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000728 "    insert_chart()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000729 "    insert_shape()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000730 "    insert_button()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000731 "    data_validation()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000732 "    conditional_formatting()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000733 "    add_sparkline()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000734 "    add_table()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000735 "    get_name()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000736 "    activate()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000737 "    select()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000738 "    hide()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000739 "    very_hidden()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000740 "    set_first_sheet()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000741 "    protect()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000742 "    unprotect_range()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000743 "    set_selection()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000744 "    set_top_left_cell()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000745 "    set_row()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000746 "    set_row_pixels()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000747 "    set_default_row()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000748 "    set_column()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000749 "    set_column_pixels()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000750 "    outline_settings()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000751 "    freeze_panes()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000752 "    split_panes()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000753 "    merge_range()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000754 "    merge_range_type()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000755 "    set_zoom()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000756 "    right_to_left()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000757 "    hide_zero()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000758 "    set_background()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000759 "    set_tab_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000760 "    autofilter()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000761 "    filter_column()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000762 "    filter_column_list()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000763 "    set_vba_name()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000764 "    ignore_errors()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000765 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000766 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000767 "=head2 Cell notation"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000768 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000769 "Excel::Writer::XLSX supports two forms of notation to designate the position of cells: Row-column notation and A1 notation."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000770 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000771 "Row-column notation uses a zero based index for both row and column while A1 notation uses the standard Excel alphanumeric sequence of column letter and 1-based row. For example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000772 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000773 "    (0, 0)      # The top left cell in row-column notation."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000774 "    ('A1')      # The top left cell in A1 notation."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000775 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000776 "    (1999, 29)  # Row-column notation."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000777 "    ('AD2000')  # The same cell in A1 notation."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000778 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000779 "Row-column notation is useful if you are referring to cells programmatically:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000780 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000781 "    for my $i ( 0 .. 9 ) {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000782 "        $worksheet->write( $i, 0, 'Hello' );    # Cells A1 to A10"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000783 "    }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000784 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000785 "A1 notation is useful for setting up a worksheet manually and for working with formulas:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000786 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000787 "    $worksheet->write( 'H1', 200 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000788 "    $worksheet->write( 'H2', '=H1+1' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000789 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000790 "In formulas and applicable methods you can also use the C<A:A> column notation:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000791 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000792 "    $worksheet->write( 'A1', '=SUM(B:B)' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000793 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000794 "The C<Excel::Writer::XLSX::Utility> module that is included in the distro contains helper functions for dealing with A1 notation, for example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000795 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000796 "    use Excel::Writer::XLSX::Utility;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000797 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000798 "    ( $row, $col ) = xl_cell_to_rowcol( 'C2' );    # (1, 2)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000799 "    $str           = xl_rowcol_to_cell( 1, 2 );    # C2"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000800 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000801 "For simplicity, the parameter lists for the worksheet method calls in the following sections are given in terms of row-column notation. In all cases it is also possible to use A1 notation."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000802 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000803 "Note: in Excel it is also possible to use a R1C1 notation. This is not supported by Excel::Writer::XLSX."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000804 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000805 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000806 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000807 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000808 "=head2 write( $row, $column, $token, $format )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000809 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000810 "Excel makes a distinction between data types such as strings, numbers, blanks, formulas and hyperlinks. To simplify the process of writing data the C<write()> method acts as a general alias for several more specific methods:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000811 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000812 "    write_string()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000813 "    write_number()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000814 "    write_blank()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000815 "    write_formula()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000816 "    write_url()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000817 "    write_row()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000818 "    write_col()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000819 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000820 "The general rule is that if the data looks like a I<something> then a I<something> is written. Here are some examples in both row-column and A1 notation:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000821 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000822 "                                                        # Same as:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000823 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 0, 'Hello'                 ); # write_string()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000824 "    $worksheet->write( 1, 0, 'One'                   ); # write_string()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000825 "    $worksheet->write( 2, 0,  2                      ); # write_number()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000826 "    $worksheet->write( 3, 0,  3.00001                ); # write_number()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000827 "    $worksheet->write( 4, 0,  \"\"                     ); # write_blank()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000828 "    $worksheet->write( 5, 0,  ''                     ); # write_blank()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000829 "    $worksheet->write( 6, 0,  undef                  ); # write_blank()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000830 "    $worksheet->write( 7, 0                          ); # write_blank()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000831 "    $worksheet->write( 8, 0,  'http://www.perl.com/' ); # write_url()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000832 "    $worksheet->write( 'A9',  'ftp://ftp.cpan.org/'  ); # write_url()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000833 "    $worksheet->write( 'A10', 'internal:Sheet1!A1'   ); # write_url()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000834 "    $worksheet->write( 'A11', 'external:c:\\foo.xlsx' ); # write_url()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000835 "    $worksheet->write( 'A12', '=A3 + 3*A4'           ); # write_formula()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000836 "    $worksheet->write( 'A13', '=SIN(PI()/4)'         ); # write_formula()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000837 "    $worksheet->write( 'A14', \\@array                ); # write_row()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000838 "    $worksheet->write( 'A15', [\\@array]              ); # write_col()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000839 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000840 "    # And if the keep_leading_zeros property is set:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000841 "    $worksheet->write( 'A16', '2'                    ); # write_number()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000842 "    $worksheet->write( 'A17', '02'                   ); # write_string()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000843 "    $worksheet->write( 'A18', '00002'                ); # write_string()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000844 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000845 "    # Write an array formula. Not available in Spreadsheet::WriteExcel."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000846 "    $worksheet->write( 'A19', '{=SUM(A1:B1*A2:B2)}'  ); # write_formula()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000847 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000848 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000849 "The \"looks like\" rule is defined by regular expressions:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000850 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000851 "C<write_number()> if C<$token> is a number based on the following regex: C<$token =~ /^([+-]?)(?=\\d|\\.\\d)\\d*(\\.\\d*)?([Ee]([+-]?\\d+))?$/>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000852 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000853 "C<write_string()> if C<keep_leading_zeros()> is set and C<$token> is an integer with leading zeros based on the following regex: C<$token =~ /^0\\d+$/>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000854 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000855 "C<write_blank()> if C<$token> is undef or a blank string: C<undef>, C<\"\"> or C<''>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000856 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000857 "C<write_url()> if C<$token> is a http, https, ftp or mailto URL based on the following regexes: C<$token =~ m|^[fh]tt?ps?://|> or C<$token =~ m|^mailto:|>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000858 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000859 "C<write_url()> if C<$token> is an internal or external sheet reference based on the following regex: C<$token =~ m[^(in|ex)ternal:]>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000860 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000861 "C<write_formula()> if the first character of C<$token> is C<\"=\">."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000862 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000863 "C<write_array_formula()> if the C<$token> matches C</^{=.*}$/>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000864 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000865 "C<write_row()> if C<$token> is an array ref."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000866 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000867 "C<write_col()> if C<$token> is an array ref of array refs."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000868 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000869 "C<write_string()> if none of the previous conditions apply."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000870 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000871 "The C<$format> parameter is optional. It should be a valid Format object, see L</CELL FORMATTING>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000872 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000873 "    my $format = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000874 "    $format->set_bold();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000875 "    $format->set_color( 'red' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000876 "    $format->set_align( 'center' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000877 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000878 "    $worksheet->write( 4, 0, 'Hello', $format );    # Formatted string"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000879 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000880 "The write() method will ignore empty strings or C<undef> tokens unless a format is also supplied. As such you needn't worry about special handling for empty or C<undef> values in your data. See also the C<write_blank()> method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000881 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000882 "One problem with the C<write()> method is that occasionally data looks like a number but you don't want it treated as a number. For example, zip codes or ID numbers often start with a leading zero. If you write this data as a number then the leading zero(s) will be stripped. You can change this default behaviour by using the C<keep_leading_zeros()> method. While this property is in place any integers with leading zeros will be treated as strings and the zeros will be preserved. See the C<keep_leading_zeros()> section for a full discussion of this issue."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000883 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000884 "You can also add your own data handlers to the C<write()> method using C<add_write_handler()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000885 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000886 "The C<write()> method will also handle Unicode strings in C<UTF-8> format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000887 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000888 "The C<write> methods return:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000889 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000890 "    0 for success."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000891 "   -1 for insufficient number of arguments."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000892 "   -2 for row or column out of bounds."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000893 "   -3 for string too long."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000894 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000895 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000896 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000897 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000898 "=head2 write_number( $row, $column, $number, $format )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000899 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000900 "Write an integer or a float to the cell specified by C<$row> and C<$column>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000901 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000902 "    $worksheet->write_number( 0, 0, 123456 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000903 "    $worksheet->write_number( 'A2', 2.3451 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000904 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000905 "See the note about L</Cell notation>. The C<$format> parameter is optional."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000906 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000907 "In general it is sufficient to use the C<write()> method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000908 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000909 "B<Note>: some versions of Excel 2007 do not display the calculated values of formulas written by Excel::Writer::XLSX. Applying all available Service Packs to Excel should fix this."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000910 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000911 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000912 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000913 "=head2 write_string( $row, $column, $string, $format )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000914 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000915 "Write a string to the cell specified by C<$row> and C<$column>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000916 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000917 "    $worksheet->write_string( 0, 0, 'Your text here' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000918 "    $worksheet->write_string( 'A2', 'or here' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000919 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000920 "The maximum string size is 32767 characters. However the maximum string segment that Excel can display in a cell is 1000. All 32767 characters can be displayed in the formula bar."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000921 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000922 "The C<$format> parameter is optional."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000923 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000924 "The C<write()> method will also handle strings in C<UTF-8> format. See also the C<unicode_*.pl> programs in the examples directory of the distro."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000925 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000926 "In general it is sufficient to use the C<write()> method. However, you may sometimes wish to use the C<write_string()> method to write data that looks like a number but that you don't want treated as a number. For example, zip codes or phone numbers:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000927 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000928 "    # Write as a plain string"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000929 "    $worksheet->write_string( 'A1', '01209' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000930 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000931 "However, if the user edits this string Excel may convert it back to a number. To get around this you can use the Excel text format C<@>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000932 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000933 "    # Format as a string. Doesn't change to a number when edited"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000934 "    my $format1 = $workbook->add_format( num_format => '@' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000935 "    $worksheet->write_string( 'A2', '01209', $format1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000936 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000937 "See also the note about L</Cell notation>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000938 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000939 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000940 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000941 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000942 "=head2 write_rich_string( $row, $column, $format, $string, ..., $cell_format )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000943 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000944 "The C<write_rich_string()> method is used to write strings with multiple formats. For example to write the string \"This is B<bold> and this is I<italic>\" you would use the following:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000945 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000946 "    my $bold   = $workbook->add_format( bold   => 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000947 "    my $italic = $workbook->add_format( italic => 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000948 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000949 "    $worksheet->write_rich_string( 'A1',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000950 "        'This is ', $bold, 'bold', ' and this is ', $italic, 'italic' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000951 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000952 "The basic rule is to break the string into fragments and put a C<$format> object before the fragment that you want to format. For example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000953 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000954 "    # Unformatted string."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000955 "      'This is an example string'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000956 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000957 "    # Break it into fragments."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000958 "      'This is an ', 'example', ' string'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000959 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000960 "    # Add formatting before the fragments you want formatted."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000961 "      'This is an ', $format, 'example', ' string'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000962 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000963 "    # In Excel::Writer::XLSX."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000964 "    $worksheet->write_rich_string( 'A1',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000965 "        'This is an ', $format, 'example', ' string' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000966 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000967 "String fragments that don't have a format are given a default format. So for example when writing the string \"Some B<bold> text\" you would use the first example below but it would be equivalent to the second:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000968 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000969 "    # With default formatting:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000970 "    my $bold    = $workbook->add_format( bold => 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000971 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000972 "    $worksheet->write_rich_string( 'A1',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000973 "        'Some ', $bold, 'bold', ' text' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000974 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000975 "    # Or more explicitly:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000976 "    my $bold    = $workbook->add_format( bold => 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000977 "    my $default = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000978 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000979 "    $worksheet->write_rich_string( 'A1',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000980 "        $default, 'Some ', $bold, 'bold', $default, ' text' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000981 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000982 "As with Excel, only the font properties of the format such as font name, style, size, underline, color and effects are applied to the string fragments. Other features such as border, background, text wrap and alignment must be applied to the cell."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000983 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000984 "The C<write_rich_string()> method allows you to do this by using the last argument as a cell format (if it is a format object). The following example centers a rich string in the cell:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000985 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000986 "    my $bold   = $workbook->add_format( bold  => 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000987 "    my $center = $workbook->add_format( align => 'center' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000988 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000989 "    $worksheet->write_rich_string( 'A5',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000990 "        'Some ', $bold, 'bold text', ' centered', $center );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000991 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000992 "See the C<rich_strings.pl> example in the distro for more examples."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000993 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000994 "    my $bold   = $workbook->add_format( bold        => 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000995 "    my $italic = $workbook->add_format( italic      => 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000996 "    my $red    = $workbook->add_format( color       => 'red' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000997 "    my $blue   = $workbook->add_format( color       => 'blue' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000998 "    my $center = $workbook->add_format( align       => 'center' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000999 "    my $super  = $workbook->add_format( font_script => 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001000 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001001 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001002 "    # Write some strings with multiple formats."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001003 "    $worksheet->write_rich_string( 'A1',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001004 "        'This is ', $bold, 'bold', ' and this is ', $italic, 'italic' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001005 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001006 "    $worksheet->write_rich_string( 'A3',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001007 "        'This is ', $red, 'red', ' and this is ', $blue, 'blue' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001008 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001009 "    $worksheet->write_rich_string( 'A5',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001010 "        'Some ', $bold, 'bold text', ' centered', $center );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001011 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001012 "    $worksheet->write_rich_string( 'A7',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001013 "        $italic, 'j = k', $super, '(n-1)', $center );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001014 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001015 "=begin html"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001016 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001017 "<p><center><img src=\"http://jmcnamara.github.io/excel-writer-xlsx/images/examples/rich_strings.jpg\" width=\"640\" height=\"420\" alt=\"Output from rich_strings.pl\" /></center></p>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001018 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001019 "=end html"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001020 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001021 "As with C<write_sting()> the maximum string size is 32767 characters. See also the note about L</Cell notation>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001022 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001023 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001024 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001025 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001026 "=head2 keep_leading_zeros()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001027 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001028 "This method changes the default handling of integers with leading zeros when using the C<write()> method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001029 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001030 "The C<write()> method uses regular expressions to determine what type of data to write to an Excel worksheet. If the data looks like a number it writes a number using C<write_number()>. One problem with this approach is that occasionally data looks like a number but you don't want it treated as a number."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001031 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001032 "Zip codes and ID numbers, for example, often start with a leading zero. If you write this data as a number then the leading zero(s) will be stripped. This is the also the default behaviour when you enter data manually in Excel."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001033 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001034 "To get around this you can use one of three options. Write a formatted number, write the number as a string or use the C<keep_leading_zeros()> method to change the default behaviour of C<write()>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001035 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001036 "    # Implicitly write a number, the leading zero is removed: 1209"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001037 "    $worksheet->write( 'A1', '01209' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001038 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001039 "    # Write a zero padded number using a format: 01209"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001040 "    my $format1 = $workbook->add_format( num_format => '00000' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001041 "    $worksheet->write( 'A2', '01209', $format1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001042 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001043 "    # Write explicitly as a string: 01209"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001044 "    $worksheet->write_string( 'A3', '01209' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001045 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001046 "    # Write implicitly as a string: 01209"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001047 "    $worksheet->keep_leading_zeros();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001048 "    $worksheet->write( 'A4', '01209' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001049 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001050 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001051 "The above code would generate a worksheet that looked like the following:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001052 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001053 "     -----------------------------------------------------------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001054 "    |   |     A     |     B     |     C     |     D     | ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001055 "     -----------------------------------------------------------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001056 "    | 1 |      1209 |           |           |           | ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001057 "    | 2 |     01209 |           |           |           | ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001058 "    | 3 | 01209     |           |           |           | ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001059 "    | 4 | 01209     |           |           |           | ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001060 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001061 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001062 "The examples are on different sides of the cells due to the fact that Excel displays strings with a left justification and numbers with a right justification by default. You can change this by using a format to justify the data, see L</CELL FORMATTING>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001063 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001064 "It should be noted that if the user edits the data in examples C<A3> and C<A4> the strings will revert back to numbers. Again this is Excel's default behaviour. To avoid this you can use the text format C<@>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001065 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001066 "    # Format as a string (01209)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001067 "    my $format2 = $workbook->add_format( num_format => '@' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001068 "    $worksheet->write_string( 'A5', '01209', $format2 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001069 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001070 "The C<keep_leading_zeros()> property is off by default. The C<keep_leading_zeros()> method takes 0 or 1 as an argument. It defaults to 1 if an argument isn't specified:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001071 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001072 "    $worksheet->keep_leading_zeros();       # Set on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001073 "    $worksheet->keep_leading_zeros( 1 );    # Set on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001074 "    $worksheet->keep_leading_zeros( 0 );    # Set off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001075 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001076 "See also the C<add_write_handler()> method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001077 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001078 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001079 "=head2 write_blank( $row, $column, $format )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001080 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001081 "Write a blank cell specified by C<$row> and C<$column>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001082 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001083 "    $worksheet->write_blank( 0, 0, $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001084 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001085 "This method is used to add formatting to a cell which doesn't contain a string or number value."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001086 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001087 "Excel differentiates between an \"Empty\" cell and a \"Blank\" cell. An \"Empty\" cell is a cell which doesn't contain data whilst a \"Blank\" cell is a cell which doesn't contain data but does contain formatting. Excel stores \"Blank\" cells but ignores \"Empty\" cells."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001088 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001089 "As such, if you write an empty cell without formatting it is ignored:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001090 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001091 "    $worksheet->write( 'A1', undef, $format );    # write_blank()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001092 "    $worksheet->write( 'A2', undef );             # Ignored"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001093 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001094 "This seemingly uninteresting fact means that you can write arrays of data without special treatment for C<undef> or empty string values."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001095 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001096 "See the note about L</Cell notation>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001097 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001098 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001099 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001100 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001101 "=head2 write_row( $row, $column, $array_ref, $format )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001102 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001103 "The C<write_row()> method can be used to write a 1D or 2D array of data in one go. This is useful for converting the results of a database query into an Excel worksheet. You must pass a reference to the array of data rather than the array itself. The C<write()> method is then called for each element of the data. For example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001104 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001105 "    @array = ( 'awk', 'gawk', 'mawk' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001106 "    $array_ref = \\@array;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001107 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001108 "    $worksheet->write_row( 0, 0, $array_ref );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001109 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001110 "    # The above example is equivalent to:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001111 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 0, $array[0] );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001112 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 1, $array[1] );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001113 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 2, $array[2] );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001114 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001115 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001116 "Note: For convenience the C<write()> method behaves in the same way as C<write_row()> if it is passed an array reference. Therefore the following two method calls are equivalent:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001117 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001118 "    $worksheet->write_row( 'A1', $array_ref );    # Write a row of data"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001119 "    $worksheet->write(     'A1', $array_ref );    # Same thing"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001120 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001121 "As with all of the write methods the C<$format> parameter is optional. If a format is specified it is applied to all the elements of the data array."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001122 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001123 "Array references within the data will be treated as columns. This allows you to write 2D arrays of data in one go. For example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001124 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001125 "    @eec =  ("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001126 "                ['maggie', 'milly', 'molly', 'may'  ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001127 "                [13,       14,      15,      16     ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001128 "                ['shell',  'star',  'crab',  'stone']"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001129 "            );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001130 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001131 "    $worksheet->write_row( 'A1', \\@eec );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001132 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001133 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001134 "Would produce a worksheet as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001135 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001136 "     -----------------------------------------------------------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001137 "    |   |    A    |    B    |    C    |    D    |    E    | ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001138 "     -----------------------------------------------------------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001139 "    | 1 | maggie  | 13      | shell   | ...     |  ...    | ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001140 "    | 2 | milly   | 14      | star    | ...     |  ...    | ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001141 "    | 3 | molly   | 15      | crab    | ...     |  ...    | ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001142 "    | 4 | may     | 16      | stone   | ...     |  ...    | ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001143 "    | 5 | ...     | ...     | ...     | ...     |  ...    | ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001144 "    | 6 | ...     | ...     | ...     | ...     |  ...    | ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001145 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001146 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001147 "To write the data in a row-column order refer to the C<write_col()> method below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001148 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001149 "Any C<undef> values in the data will be ignored unless a format is applied to the data, in which case a formatted blank cell will be written. In either case the appropriate row or column value will still be incremented."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001150 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001151 "To find out more about array references refer to C<perlref> and C<perlreftut> in the main Perl documentation. To find out more about 2D arrays or \"lists of lists\" refer to C<perllol>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001152 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001153 "The C<write_row()> method returns the first error encountered when writing the elements of the data or zero if no errors were encountered. See the return values described for the C<write()> method above."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001154 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001155 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001156 "The C<write_row()> method allows the following idiomatic conversion of a text file to an Excel file:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001157 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001158 "    #!/usr/bin/perl -w"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001159 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001160 "    use strict;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001161 "    use Excel::Writer::XLSX;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001162 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001163 "    my $workbook  = Excel::Writer::XLSX->new( 'file.xlsx' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001164 "    my $worksheet = $workbook->add_worksheet();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001165 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001166 "    open INPUT, 'file.txt' or die \"Couldn't open file: $!\";"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001167 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001168 "    $worksheet->write( $. -1, 0, [split] ) while <INPUT>;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001169 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001170 "    $workbook->close();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001171 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001172 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001173 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001174 "=head2 write_col( $row, $column, $array_ref, $format )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001175 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001176 "The C<write_col()> method can be used to write a 1D or 2D array of data in one go. This is useful for converting the results of a database query into an Excel worksheet. You must pass a reference to the array of data rather than the array itself. The C<write()> method is then called for each element of the data. For example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001177 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001178 "    @array = ( 'awk', 'gawk', 'mawk' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001179 "    $array_ref = \\@array;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001180 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001181 "    $worksheet->write_col( 0, 0, $array_ref );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001182 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001183 "    # The above example is equivalent to:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001184 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 0, $array[0] );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001185 "    $worksheet->write( 1, 0, $array[1] );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001186 "    $worksheet->write( 2, 0, $array[2] );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001187 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001188 "As with all of the write methods the C<$format> parameter is optional. If a format is specified it is applied to all the elements of the data array."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001189 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001190 "Array references within the data will be treated as rows. This allows you to write 2D arrays of data in one go. For example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001191 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001192 "    @eec =  ("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001193 "                ['maggie', 'milly', 'molly', 'may'  ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001194 "                [13,       14,      15,      16     ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001195 "                ['shell',  'star',  'crab',  'stone']"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001196 "            );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001197 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001198 "    $worksheet->write_col( 'A1', \\@eec );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001199 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001200 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001201 "Would produce a worksheet as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001202 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001203 "     -----------------------------------------------------------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001204 "    |   |    A    |    B    |    C    |    D    |    E    | ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001205 "     -----------------------------------------------------------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001206 "    | 1 | maggie  | milly   | molly   | may     |  ...    | ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001207 "    | 2 | 13      | 14      | 15      | 16      |  ...    | ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001208 "    | 3 | shell   | star    | crab    | stone   |  ...    | ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001209 "    | 4 | ...     | ...     | ...     | ...     |  ...    | ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001210 "    | 5 | ...     | ...     | ...     | ...     |  ...    | ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001211 "    | 6 | ...     | ...     | ...     | ...     |  ...    | ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001212 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001213 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001214 "To write the data in a column-row order refer to the C<write_row()> method above."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001215 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001216 "Any C<undef> values in the data will be ignored unless a format is applied to the data, in which case a formatted blank cell will be written. In either case the appropriate row or column value will still be incremented."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001217 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001218 "As noted above the C<write()> method can be used as a synonym for C<write_row()> and C<write_row()> handles nested array refs as columns. Therefore, the following two method calls are equivalent although the more explicit call to C<write_col()> would be preferable for maintainability:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001219 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001220 "    $worksheet->write_col( 'A1', $array_ref     ); # Write a column of data"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001221 "    $worksheet->write(     'A1', [ $array_ref ] ); # Same thing"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001222 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001223 "To find out more about array references refer to C<perlref> and C<perlreftut> in the main Perl documentation. To find out more about 2D arrays or \"lists of lists\" refer to C<perllol>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001224 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001225 "The C<write_col()> method returns the first error encountered when writing the elements of the data or zero if no errors were encountered. See the return values described for the C<write()> method above."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001226 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001227 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001228 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001229 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001230 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001231 "=head2 write_date_time( $row, $col, $date_string, $format )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001232 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001233 "The C<write_date_time()> method can be used to write a date or time to the cell specified by C<$row> and C<$column>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001234 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001235 "    $worksheet->write_date_time( 'A1', '2004-05-13T23:20', $date_format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001236 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001237 "The C<$date_string> should be in the following format:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001238 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001239 "    yyyy-mm-ddThh:mm:ss.sss"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001240 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001241 "This conforms to an ISO8601 date but it should be noted that the full range of ISO8601 formats are not supported."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001242 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001243 "The following variations on the C<$date_string> parameter are permitted:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001244 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001245 "    yyyy-mm-ddThh:mm:ss.sss         # Standard format"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001246 "    yyyy-mm-ddT                     # No time"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001247 "              Thh:mm:ss.sss         # No date"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001248 "    yyyy-mm-ddThh:mm:ss.sssZ        # Additional Z (but not time zones)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001249 "    yyyy-mm-ddThh:mm:ss             # No fractional seconds"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001250 "    yyyy-mm-ddThh:mm                # No seconds"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001251 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001252 "Note that the C<T> is required in all cases."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001253 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001254 "A date should always have a C<$format>, otherwise it will appear as a number, see L</DATES AND TIME IN EXCEL> and L</CELL FORMATTING>. Here is a typical example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001255 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001256 "    my $date_format = $workbook->add_format( num_format => 'mm/dd/yy' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001257 "    $worksheet->write_date_time( 'A1', '2004-05-13T23:20', $date_format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001258 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001259 "Valid dates should be in the range 1900-01-01 to 9999-12-31, for the 1900 epoch and 1904-01-01 to 9999-12-31, for the 1904 epoch. As with Excel, dates outside these ranges will be written as a string."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001260 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001261 "See also the date_time.pl program in the C<examples> directory of the distro."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001262 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001263 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001264 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001265 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001266 "=head2 write_url( $row, $col, $url, $format, $label )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001267 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001268 "Write a hyperlink to a URL in the cell specified by C<$row> and C<$column>. The hyperlink is comprised of two elements: the visible label and the invisible link. The visible label is the same as the link unless an alternative label is specified. The C<$label> parameter is optional. The label is written using the C<write()> method. Therefore it is possible to write strings, numbers or formulas as labels."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001269 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001270 "The C<$format> parameter is also optional and the default Excel hyperlink style will be used if it isn't specified. If required you can access the default url format using the Workbook C<get_default_url_format> method:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001271 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001272 "    my $url_format = $workbook->get_default_url_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001273 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001274 "There are four web style URI's supported: C<http://>, C<https://>, C<ftp://> and C<mailto:>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001275 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001276 "    $worksheet->write_url( 0, 0, 'ftp://www.perl.org/' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001277 "    $worksheet->write_url( 'A3', 'http://www.perl.com/' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001278 "    $worksheet->write_url( 'A4', 'mailto:jmcnamara@cpan.org' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001279 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001280 "You can display an alternative string using the C<$label> parameter:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001281 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001282 "    $worksheet->write_url( 1, 0, 'http://www.perl.com/', undef, 'Perl' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001283 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001284 "If you wish to have some other cell data such as a number or a formula you can overwrite the cell using another call to C<write_*()>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001285 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001286 "    $worksheet->write_url( 'A1', 'http://www.perl.com/' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001287 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001288 "    # Overwrite the URL string with a formula. The cell is still a link."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001289 "    # Note the use of the default url format for consistency with other links."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001290 "    my $url_format = $workbook->get_default_url_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001291 "    $worksheet->write_formula( 'A1', '=1+1', $url_format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001292 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001293 "There are two local URIs supported: C<internal:> and C<external:>. These are used for hyperlinks to internal worksheet references or external workbook and worksheet references:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001294 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001295 "    $worksheet->write_url( 'A6',  'internal:Sheet2!A1' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001296 "    $worksheet->write_url( 'A7',  'internal:Sheet2!A1' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001297 "    $worksheet->write_url( 'A8',  'internal:Sheet2!A1:B2' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001298 "    $worksheet->write_url( 'A9',  q{internal:'Sales Data'!A1} );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001299 "    $worksheet->write_url( 'A10', 'external:c:\\temp\\foo.xlsx' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001300 "    $worksheet->write_url( 'A11', 'external:c:\\foo.xlsx#Sheet2!A1' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001301 "    $worksheet->write_url( 'A12', 'external:..\\foo.xlsx' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001302 "    $worksheet->write_url( 'A13', 'external:..\\foo.xlsx#Sheet2!A1' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001303 "    $worksheet->write_url( 'A13', 'external:\\\\\\\\NET\\share\\foo.xlsx' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001304 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001305 "All of the these URI types are recognised by the C<write()> method, see above."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001306 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001307 "Worksheet references are typically of the form C<Sheet1!A1>. You can also refer to a worksheet range using the standard Excel notation: C<Sheet1!A1:B2>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001308 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001309 "In external links the workbook and worksheet name must be separated by the C<#> character: C<external:Workbook.xlsx#Sheet1!A1'>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001310 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001311 "You can also link to a named range in the target worksheet. For example say you have a named range called C<my_name> in the workbook C<c:\\temp\\foo.xlsx> you could link to it as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001312 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001313 "    $worksheet->write_url( 'A14', 'external:c:\\temp\\foo.xlsx#my_name' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001314 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001315 "Excel requires that worksheet names containing spaces or non alphanumeric characters are single quoted as follows C<'Sales Data'!A1>. If you need to do this in a single quoted string then you can either escape the single quotes C<\\'> or use the quote operator C<q{}> as described in C<perlop> in the main Perl documentation."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001316 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001317 "Links to network files are also supported. MS/Novell Network files normally begin with two back slashes as follows C<\\\\NETWORK\\etc>. In order to generate this in a single or double quoted string you will have to escape the backslashes,  C<'\\\\\\\\NETWORK\\etc'>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001318 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001319 "If you are using double quote strings then you should be careful to escape anything that looks like a metacharacter. For more information see C<perlfaq5: Why can't I use \"C:\\temp\\foo\" in DOS paths?>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001320 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001321 "Finally, you can avoid most of these quoting problems by using forward slashes. These are translated internally to backslashes:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001322 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001323 "    $worksheet->write_url( 'A14', \"external:c:/temp/foo.xlsx\" );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001324 "    $worksheet->write_url( 'A15', 'external://NETWORK/share/foo.xlsx' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001325 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001326 "Note: Excel::Writer::XLSX will escape the following characters in URLs as required by Excel: C<< \\s \" < > \\ [  ] ` ^ { } >> unless the URL already contains C<%xx> style escapes. In which case it is assumed that the URL was escaped correctly by the user and will by passed directly to Excel."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001327 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001328 "Versions of Excel prior to Excel 2015 limited hyperlink links and anchor/locations to 255 characters each. Versions after that support urls up to 2079 characters. Excel::Writer::XLSX versions >= 1.0.2 support the new longer limit by default."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001329 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001330 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001331 "See also, the note about L</Cell notation>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001332 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001333 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001334 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001335 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001336 "=head2 write_formula( $row, $column, $formula, $format, $value )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001337 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001338 "Write a formula or function to the cell specified by C<$row> and C<$column>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001339 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001340 "    $worksheet->write_formula( 0, 0, '=$B$3 + B4' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001341 "    $worksheet->write_formula( 1, 0, '=SIN(PI()/4)' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001342 "    $worksheet->write_formula( 2, 0, '=SUM(B1:B5)' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001343 "    $worksheet->write_formula( 'A4', '=IF(A3>1,\"Yes\", \"No\")' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001344 "    $worksheet->write_formula( 'A5', '=AVERAGE(1, 2, 3, 4)' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001345 "    $worksheet->write_formula( 'A6', '=DATEVALUE(\"1-Jan-2001\")' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001346 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001347 "Array formulas are also supported:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001348 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001349 "    $worksheet->write_formula( 'A7', '{=SUM(A1:B1*A2:B2)}' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001350 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001351 "See also the C<write_array_formula()> method below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001352 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001353 "See the note about L</Cell notation>. For more information about writing Excel formulas see L</FORMULAS AND FUNCTIONS IN EXCEL>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001354 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001355 "If required, it is also possible to specify the calculated value of the formula. This is occasionally necessary when working with non-Excel applications that don't calculate the value of the formula. The calculated C<$value> is added at the end of the argument list:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001356 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001357 "    $worksheet->write( 'A1', '=2+2', $format, 4 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001358 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001359 "However, this probably isn't something that you will ever need to do. If you do use this feature then do so with care."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001360 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001361 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001362 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001363 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001364 "=head2 write_array_formula($first_row, $first_col, $last_row, $last_col, $formula, $format, $value)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001365 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001366 "Write an array formula to a cell range. In Excel an array formula is a formula that performs a calculation on a set of values. It can return a single value or a range of values."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001367 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001368 "An array formula is indicated by a pair of braces around the formula: C<{=SUM(A1:B1*A2:B2)}>.  If the array formula returns a single value then the C<$first_> and C<$last_> parameters should be the same:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001369 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001370 "    $worksheet->write_array_formula('A1:A1', '{=SUM(B1:C1*B2:C2)}');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001371 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001372 "It this case however it is easier to just use the C<write_formula()> or C<write()> methods:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001373 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001374 "    # Same as above but more concise."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001375 "    $worksheet->write( 'A1', '{=SUM(B1:C1*B2:C2)}' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001376 "    $worksheet->write_formula( 'A1', '{=SUM(B1:C1*B2:C2)}' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001377 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001378 "For array formulas that return a range of values you must specify the range that the return values will be written to:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001379 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001380 "    $worksheet->write_array_formula( 'A1:A3',    '{=TREND(C1:C3,B1:B3)}' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001381 "    $worksheet->write_array_formula( 0, 0, 2, 0, '{=TREND(C1:C3,B1:B3)}' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001382 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001383 "If required, it is also possible to specify the calculated value of the formula. This is occasionally necessary when working with non-Excel applications that don't calculate the value of the formula. However, using this parameter only writes a single value to the upper left cell in the result array. For a multi-cell array formula where the results are required, the other result values can be specified by using C<write_number()> to write to the appropriate cell:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001384 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001385 "    # Specify the result for a single cell range."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001386 "    $worksheet->write_array_formula( 'A1:A3', '{=SUM(B1:C1*B2:C2)}, $format, 2005 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001387 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001388 "    # Specify the results for a multi cell range."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001389 "    $worksheet->write_array_formula( 'A1:A3', '{=TREND(C1:C3,B1:B3)}', $format, 105 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001390 "    $worksheet->write_number( 'A2', 12, format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001391 "    $worksheet->write_number( 'A3', 14, format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001392 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001393 "In addition, some early versions of Excel 2007 don't calculate the values of array formulas when they aren't supplied. Installing the latest Office Service Pack should fix this issue."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001394 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001395 "See also the C<array_formula.pl> program in the C<examples> directory of the distro."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001396 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001397 "Note: Array formulas are not supported by Spreadsheet::WriteExcel."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001398 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001399 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001400 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001401 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001402 "=head2 write_boolean( $row, $column, $value, $format )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001403 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001404 "Write an Excel boolean value to the cell specified by C<$row> and C<$column>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001405 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001406 "    $worksheet->write_boolean( 'A1', 1          );  # TRUE"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001407 "    $worksheet->write_boolean( 'A2', 0          );  # FALSE"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001408 "    $worksheet->write_boolean( 'A3', undef      );  # FALSE"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001409 "    $worksheet->write_boolean( 'A3', 0, $format );  # FALSE, with format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001410 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001411 "A C<$value> that is true or false using Perl's rules will be written as an Excel boolean C<TRUE> or C<FALSE> value."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001412 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001413 "See the note about L</Cell notation>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001414 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001415 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001416 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001417 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001418 "=head2 store_formula( $formula )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001419 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001420 "Deprecated. This is a Spreadsheet::WriteExcel method that is no longer required by Excel::Writer::XLSX. See below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001421 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001422 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001423 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001424 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001425 "=head2 repeat_formula( $row, $col, $formula, $format )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001426 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001427 "Deprecated. This is a Spreadsheet::WriteExcel method that is no longer required by Excel::Writer::XLSX."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001428 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001429 "In Spreadsheet::WriteExcel it was computationally expensive to write formulas since they were parsed by a recursive descent parser. The C<store_formula()> and C<repeat_formula()> methods were used as a way of avoiding the overhead of repeated formulas by reusing a pre-parsed formula."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001430 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001431 "In Excel::Writer::XLSX this is no longer necessary since it is just as quick to write a formula as it is to write a string or a number."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001432 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001433 "The methods remain for backward compatibility but new Excel::Writer::XLSX programs shouldn't use them."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001434 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001435 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001436 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001437 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001438 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001439 "=head2 write_comment( $row, $column, $string, ... )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001440 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001441 "The C<write_comment()> method is used to add a comment to a cell. A cell comment is indicated in Excel by a small red triangle in the upper right-hand corner of the cell. Moving the cursor over the red triangle will reveal the comment."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001442 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001443 "The following example shows how to add a comment to a cell:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001444 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001445 "    $worksheet->write        ( 2, 2, 'Hello' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001446 "    $worksheet->write_comment( 2, 2, 'This is a comment.' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001447 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001448 "As usual you can replace the C<$row> and C<$column> parameters with an C<A1> cell reference. See the note about L</Cell notation>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001449 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001450 "    $worksheet->write        ( 'C3', 'Hello');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001451 "    $worksheet->write_comment( 'C3', 'This is a comment.' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001452 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001453 "The C<write_comment()> method will also handle strings in C<UTF-8> format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001454 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001455 "    $worksheet->write_comment( 'C3', \"\\x{263a}\" );       # Smiley"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001456 "    $worksheet->write_comment( 'C4', 'Comment ca va?' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001457 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001458 "In addition to the basic 3 argument form of C<write_comment()> you can pass in several optional key/value pairs to control the format of the comment. For example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001459 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001460 "    $worksheet->write_comment( 'C3', 'Hello', visible => 1, author => 'Perl' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001461 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001462 "Most of these options are quite specific and in general the default comment behaves will be all that you need. However, should you need greater control over the format of the cell comment the following options are available:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001463 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001464 "    author"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001465 "    visible"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001466 "    x_scale"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001467 "    width"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001468 "    y_scale"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001469 "    height"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001470 "    color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001471 "    start_cell"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001472 "    start_row"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001473 "    start_col"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001474 "    x_offset"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001475 "    y_offset"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001476 "    font"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001477 "    font_size"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001478 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001479 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001480 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001481 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001482 "=item Option: author"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001483 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001484 "This option is used to indicate who is the author of the cell comment. Excel displays the author of the comment in the status bar at the bottom of the worksheet. This is usually of interest in corporate environments where several people might review and provide comments to a workbook."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001485 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001486 "    $worksheet->write_comment( 'C3', 'Atonement', author => 'Ian McEwan' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001487 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001488 "The default author for all cell comments can be set using the C<set_comments_author()> method (see below)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001489 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001490 "    $worksheet->set_comments_author( 'Perl' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001491 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001492 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001493 "=item Option: visible"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001494 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001495 "This option is used to make a cell comment visible when the worksheet is opened. The default behaviour in Excel is that comments are initially hidden. However, it is also possible in Excel to make individual or all comments visible. In Excel::Writer::XLSX individual comments can be made visible as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001496 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001497 "    $worksheet->write_comment( 'C3', 'Hello', visible => 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001498 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001499 "It is possible to make all comments in a worksheet visible using the C<show_comments()> worksheet method (see below). Alternatively, if all of the cell comments have been made visible you can hide individual comments:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001500 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001501 "    $worksheet->write_comment( 'C3', 'Hello', visible => 0 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001502 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001503 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001504 "=item Option: x_scale"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001505 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001506 "This option is used to set the width of the cell comment box as a factor of the default width."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001507 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001508 "    $worksheet->write_comment( 'C3', 'Hello', x_scale => 2 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001509 "    $worksheet->write_comment( 'C4', 'Hello', x_scale => 4.2 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001510 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001511 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001512 "=item Option: width"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001513 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001514 "This option is used to set the width of the cell comment box explicitly in pixels."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001515 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001516 "    $worksheet->write_comment( 'C3', 'Hello', width => 200 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001517 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001518 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001519 "=item Option: y_scale"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001520 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001521 "This option is used to set the height of the cell comment box as a factor of the default height."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001522 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001523 "    $worksheet->write_comment( 'C3', 'Hello', y_scale => 2 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001524 "    $worksheet->write_comment( 'C4', 'Hello', y_scale => 4.2 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001525 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001526 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001527 "=item Option: height"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001528 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001529 "This option is used to set the height of the cell comment box explicitly in pixels."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001530 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001531 "    $worksheet->write_comment( 'C3', 'Hello', height => 200 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001532 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001533 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001534 "=item Option: color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001535 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001536 "This option is used to set the background colour of cell comment box. You can use one of the named colours recognised by Excel::Writer::XLSX or a Html style C<#RRGGBB> colour. See L</WORKING WITH COLOURS>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001537 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001538 "    $worksheet->write_comment( 'C3', 'Hello', color => 'green' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001539 "    $worksheet->write_comment( 'C4', 'Hello', color => '#FF6600' ); # Orange"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001540 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001541 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001542 "=item Option: start_cell"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001543 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001544 "This option is used to set the cell in which the comment will appear. By default Excel displays comments one cell to the right and one cell above the cell to which the comment relates. However, you can change this behaviour if you wish. In the following example the comment which would appear by default in cell C<D2> is moved to C<E2>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001545 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001546 "    $worksheet->write_comment( 'C3', 'Hello', start_cell => 'E2' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001547 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001548 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001549 "=item Option: start_row"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001550 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001551 "This option is used to set the row in which the comment will appear. See the C<start_cell> option above. The row is zero indexed."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001552 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001553 "    $worksheet->write_comment( 'C3', 'Hello', start_row => 0 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001554 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001555 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001556 "=item Option: start_col"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001557 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001558 "This option is used to set the column in which the comment will appear. See the C<start_cell> option above. The column is zero indexed."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001559 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001560 "    $worksheet->write_comment( 'C3', 'Hello', start_col => 4 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001561 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001562 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001563 "=item Option: x_offset"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001564 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001565 "This option is used to change the x offset, in pixels, of a comment within a cell:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001566 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001567 "    $worksheet->write_comment( 'C3', $comment, x_offset => 30 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001568 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001569 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001570 "=item Option: y_offset"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001571 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001572 "This option is used to change the y offset, in pixels, of a comment within a cell:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001573 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001574 "    $worksheet->write_comment('C3', $comment, y_offset => 30);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001575 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001576 "=item Option: font"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001577 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001578 "This option is used to change the font used in the comment from 'Tahoma' which is the default."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001579 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001580 "    $worksheet->write_comment('C3', $comment, font => 'Calibri');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001581 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001582 "=item Option: font_size"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001583 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001584 "This option is used to change the font size used in the comment from 8 which is the default."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001585 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001586 "    $worksheet->write_comment('C3', $comment, font_size => 20);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001587 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001588 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001589 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001590 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001591 "You can apply as many of these options as you require."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001592 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001593 "B<Note about using options that adjust the position of the cell comment such as start_cell, start_row, start_col, x_offset and y_offset>: Excel only displays offset cell comments when they are displayed as \"visible\". Excel does B<not> display hidden cells as moved when you mouse over them."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001594 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001595 "B<Note about row height and comments>. If you specify the height of a row that contains a comment then Excel::Writer::XLSX will adjust the height of the comment to maintain the default or user specified dimensions. However, the height of a row can also be adjusted automatically by Excel if the text wrap property is set or large fonts are used in the cell. This means that the height of the row is unknown to the module at run time and thus the comment box is stretched with the row. Use the C<set_row()> method to specify the row height explicitly and avoid this problem."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001596 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001597 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001598 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001599 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001600 "=head2 show_comments()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001601 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001602 "This method is used to make all cell comments visible when a worksheet is opened."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001603 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001604 "    $worksheet->show_comments();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001605 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001606 "Individual comments can be made visible using the C<visible> parameter of the C<write_comment> method (see above):"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001607 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001608 "    $worksheet->write_comment( 'C3', 'Hello', visible => 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001609 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001610 "If all of the cell comments have been made visible you can hide individual comments as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001611 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001612 "    $worksheet->show_comments();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001613 "    $worksheet->write_comment( 'C3', 'Hello', visible => 0 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001614 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001615 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001616 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001617 "=head2 set_comments_author()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001618 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001619 "This method is used to set the default author of all cell comments."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001620 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001621 "    $worksheet->set_comments_author( 'Perl' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001622 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001623 "Individual comment authors can be set using the C<author> parameter of the C<write_comment> method (see above)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001624 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001625 "The default comment author is an empty string, C<''>, if no author is specified."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001626 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001627 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001628 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001629 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001630 "=head2 add_write_handler( $re, $code_ref )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001631 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001632 "This method is used to extend the Excel::Writer::XLSX write() method to handle user defined data."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001633 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001634 "If you refer to the section on C<write()> above you will see that it acts as an alias for several more specific C<write_*> methods. However, it doesn't always act in exactly the way that you would like it to."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001635 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001636 "One solution is to filter the input data yourself and call the appropriate C<write_*> method. Another approach is to use the C<add_write_handler()> method to add your own automated behaviour to C<write()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001637 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001638 "The C<add_write_handler()> method take two arguments, C<$re>, a regular expression to match incoming data and C<$code_ref> a callback function to handle the matched data:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001639 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001640 "    $worksheet->add_write_handler( qr/^\\d\\d\\d\\d$/, \\&my_write );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001641 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001642 "(In the these examples the C<qr> operator is used to quote the regular expression strings, see L<perlop> for more details)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001643 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001644 "The method is used as follows. say you wished to write 7 digit ID numbers as a string so that any leading zeros were preserved*, you could do something like the following:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001645 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001646 "    $worksheet->add_write_handler( qr/^\\d{7}$/, \\&write_my_id );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001647 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001648 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001649 "    sub write_my_id {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001650 "        my $worksheet = shift;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001651 "        return $worksheet->write_string( @_ );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001652 "    }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001653 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001654 "* You could also use the C<keep_leading_zeros()> method for this."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001655 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001656 "Then if you call C<write()> with an appropriate string it will be handled automatically:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001657 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001658 "    # Writes 0000000. It would normally be written as a number; 0."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001659 "    $worksheet->write( 'A1', '0000000' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001660 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001661 "The callback function will receive a reference to the calling worksheet and all of the other arguments that were passed to C<write()>. The callback will see an C<@_> argument list that looks like the following:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001662 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001663 "    $_[0]   A ref to the calling worksheet. *"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001664 "    $_[1]   Zero based row number."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001665 "    $_[2]   Zero based column number."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001666 "    $_[3]   A number or string or token."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001667 "    $_[4]   A format ref if any."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001668 "    $_[5]   Any other arguments."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001669 "    ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001670 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001671 "    *  It is good style to shift this off the list so the @_ is the same"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001672 "       as the argument list seen by write()."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001673 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001674 "Your callback should C<return()> the return value of the C<write_*> method that was called or C<undef> to indicate that you rejected the match and want C<write()> to continue as normal."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001675 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001676 "So for example if you wished to apply the previous filter only to ID values that occur in the first column you could modify your callback function as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001677 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001678 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001679 "    sub write_my_id {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001680 "        my $worksheet = shift;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001681 "        my $col       = $_[1];"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001682 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001683 "        if ( $col == 0 ) {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001684 "            return $worksheet->write_string( @_ );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001685 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001686 "        else {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001687 "            # Reject the match and return control to write()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001688 "            return undef;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001689 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001690 "    }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001691 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001692 "Now, you will get different behaviour for the first column and other columns:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001693 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001694 "    $worksheet->write( 'A1', '0000000' );    # Writes 0000000"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001695 "    $worksheet->write( 'B1', '0000000' );    # Writes 0"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001696 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001697 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001698 "You may add more than one handler in which case they will be called in the order that they were added."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001699 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001700 "Note, the C<add_write_handler()> method is particularly suited for handling dates."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001701 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001702 "See the C<write_handler 1-4> programs in the C<examples> directory for further examples."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001703 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001704 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001705 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001706 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001707 "=head2 insert_image( $row, $col, $filename, { %options } )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001708 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001709 "This method can be used to insert a image into a worksheet. The image can be in PNG, JPEG, GIF or BMP format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001710 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001711 "    $worksheet1->insert_image( 'A1', 'perl.bmp' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001712 "    $worksheet2->insert_image( 'A1', '../images/perl.bmp' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001713 "    $worksheet3->insert_image( 'A1', '.c:\\images\\perl.bmp' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001714 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001715 "This is the equivalent of Excel's menu option to insert an image using the option to \"Place over Cells\". See C<embed_image()> below for the equivalent method to \"Place in Cell\"."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001716 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001717 "The optional C<options> hash/hashref parameter can be used to set various options for the image. The defaults are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001718 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001719 "    %options = ("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001720 "        x_offset        => 0,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001721 "        y_offset        => 0,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001722 "        x_scale         => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001723 "        y_scale         => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001724 "        object_position => 2,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001725 "        url             => undef,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001726 "        tip             => undef,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001727 "        description     => $filename,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001728 "        decorative      => 0,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001729 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001730 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001731 "The parameters C<x_offset> and C<y_offset> can be used to specify an offset from the top left hand corner of the cell specified by C<$row> and C<$col>. The offset values are in pixels."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001732 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001733 "    $worksheet1->insert_image('A1', 'perl.bmp', { x_offset =>32, y_offset => 10 });"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001734 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001735 "The offsets can be greater than the width or height of the underlying cell. This can be occasionally useful if you wish to align two or more images relative to the same cell."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001736 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001737 "The parameters C<x_scale> and C<y_scale> can be used to scale the inserted image horizontally and vertically:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001738 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001739 "    # Scale the inserted image: width x 2.0, height x 0.8"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001740 "    $worksheet->insert_image( 'A1', 'perl.bmp', { y_scale => 2, y_scale => 0.8 } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001741 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001742 "The positioning of the image when cells are resized can be set with the C<object_position> parameter:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001743 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001744 "    $worksheet->insert_image( 'A1', 'perl.bmp', { object_position => 1 } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001745 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001746 "The C<object_position> parameter can have one of the following allowable values:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001747 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001748 "    1. Move and size with cells."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001749 "    2. Move but don't size with cells."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001750 "    3. Don't move or size with cells."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001751 "    4. Same as Option 1, see below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001752 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001753 "Option 4 appears in Excel as Option 1. However, the worksheet object is sized to take hidden rows or columns into account. This allows the user to hide an image in a cell, possibly as part of an autofilter."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001754 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001755 "The C<url> option can be use to used to add a hyperlink to an image:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001756 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001757 "    $worksheet->insert_image( 'A1', 'logo.png',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001758 "        { url => 'https://github.com/jmcnamara' } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001759 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001760 "The supported url formats are the same as those supported by the C<write_url()> method and the same rules/limits apply."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001761 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001762 "The C<tip> option can be use to used to add a mouseover tip to the hyperlink:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001763 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001764 "    $worksheet->insert_image( 'A1', 'logo.png',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001765 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001766 "            url => 'https://github.com/jmcnamara',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001767 "            tip => 'GitHub'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001768 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001769 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001770 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001771 "The C<description> parameter can be used to specify a description or \"alt text\" string for the image. In general this would be used to provide a text description of the image to help accessibility. It is an optional parameter and defaults to the filename of the image. It can be used as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001772 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001773 "    $worksheet->insert_image( 'E9', 'logo.png',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001774 "                              {description => \"This is some alternative text\"} );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001775 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001776 "The optional C<decorative> parameter is also used to help accessibility. It is used to mark the image as decorative, and thus uninformative, for automated screen readers. As in Excel, if this parameter is in use the C<description> field isn't written. It is used as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001777 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001778 "    $worksheet->insert_image( 'E9', 'logo.png', {decorative => 1} );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001779 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001780 "Note: you must call C<set_row()> or C<set_column()> before C<insert_image()> if you wish to change the default dimensions of any of the rows or columns that the image occupies. The height of a row can also change if you use a font that is larger than the default. This in turn will affect the scaling of your image. To avoid this you should explicitly set the height of the row using C<set_row()> if it contains a font size that will change the row height."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001781 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001782 "BMP images must be 24 bit, true colour, bitmaps. In general it is best to avoid BMP images since they aren't compressed."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001783 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001784 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001785 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001786 "=head2 embed_image( $row, $col, $filename, { %options } )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001787 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001788 "This method can be used to embed an image into a worksheet. The image can be"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001789 "in PNG, JPEG, GIF or BMP format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001790 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001791 "    $worksheet1->embed_image( 'A1', 'perl.bmp' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001792 "    $worksheet2->embed_image( 'A1', '../images/perl.bmp' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001793 "    $worksheet3->embed_image( 'A1', '.c:\\images\\perl.bmp' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001794 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001795 "This method can be used to embed a image into a worksheet cell and have the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001796 "image automatically scale to the width and height of the cell. The X/Y scaling"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001797 "of the image is preserved but the size of the image is adjusted to fit the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001798 "largest possible width or height depending on the cell dimensions."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001799 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001800 "This is the equivalent of Excel's menu option to insert an image using the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001801 "option to \"Place in Cell\" which is only available in Excel 365 versions from"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001802 "2023 onwards. For older versions of Excel a ``#VALUE!`` error is displayed."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001803 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001804 "See C<insert_image()> for the equivalent method to \"Place over Cells\"."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001805 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001806 "The optional C<options> hash/hashref parameter can be used to set various"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001807 "options for the image. The defaults are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001808 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001809 "    %options = ("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001810 "        cell_format     => format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001811 "        url             => undef,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001812 "        tip             => undef,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001813 "        description     => $filename,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001814 "        decorative      => 0,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001815 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001816 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001817 "The C<cell_format> parameters can be an standard Format to set the formatting"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001818 "of the cell behind the image."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001819 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001820 "The C<url> option can be use to used to add a hyperlink to an image:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001821 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001822 "    $worksheet->insert_image( 'A1', 'logo.png',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001823 "        { url => 'https://github.com/jmcnamara' } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001824 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001825 "The supported url formats are the same as those supported by the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001826 "C<write_url()> method and the same rules/limits apply."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001827 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001828 "The C<tip> option can be use to used to add a mouseover tip to the hyperlink:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001829 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001830 "    $worksheet->insert_image( 'A1', 'logo.png',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001831 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001832 "            url => 'https://github.com/jmcnamara',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001833 "            tip => 'GitHub'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001834 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001835 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001836 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001837 "The C<description> parameter can be used to specify a description or \"alt"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001838 "text\" string for the image. In general this would be used to provide a text"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001839 "description of the image to help accessibility. It is an optional parameter"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001840 "and defaults to the filename of the image. It can be used as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001841 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001842 "    $worksheet->insert_image( 'E9', 'logo.png',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001843 "                              {description => \"This is some alternative text\"} );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001844 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001845 "The optional C<decorative> parameter is also used to help accessibility. It is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001846 "used to mark the image as decorative, and thus uninformative, for automated"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001847 "screen readers. As in Excel, if this parameter is in use the C<description>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001848 "field isn't written. It is used as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001849 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001850 "    $worksheet->insert_image( 'E9', 'logo.png', {decorative => 1} );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001851 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001852 "Note: you must call C<set_row()> or C<set_column()> before C<insert_image()>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001853 "if you wish to change the default dimensions of any of the rows or columns"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001854 "that the image occupies. The height of a row can also change if you use a font"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001855 "that is larger than the default. This in turn will affect the scaling of your"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001856 "image. To avoid this you should explicitly set the height of the row using"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001857 "C<set_row()> if it contains a font size that will change the row height."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001858 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001859 "BMP images must be 24 bit, true colour, bitmaps. In general it is best to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001860 "avoid BMP images since they aren't compressed."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001861 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001862 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001863 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001864 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001865 "=head2 insert_chart( $row, $col, $chart, { %options } )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001866 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001867 "This method can be used to insert a Chart object into a worksheet. The Chart must be created by the C<add_chart()> Workbook method and it must have the C<embedded> option set."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001868 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001869 "    my $chart = $workbook->add_chart( type => 'line', embedded => 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001870 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001871 "    # Configure the chart."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001872 "    ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001873 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001874 "    # Insert the chart into the a worksheet."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001875 "    $worksheet->insert_chart( 'E2', $chart );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001876 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001877 "See C<add_chart()> for details on how to create the Chart object and L<Excel::Writer::XLSX::Chart> for details on how to configure it. See also the C<chart_*.pl> programs in the examples directory of the distro."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001878 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001879 "The optional C<options> hash/hashref parameter can be used to set various options for the chart. The defaults are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001880 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001881 "    %options = ("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001882 "        x_offset        => 0,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001883 "        y_offset        => 0,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001884 "        x_scale         => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001885 "        y_scale         => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001886 "        object_position => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001887 "        description     => undef,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001888 "        decorative      => 0,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001889 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001890 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001891 "The parameters C<x_offset> and C<y_offset> can be used to specify an offset from the top left hand corner of the cell specified by C<$row> and C<$col>. The offset values are in pixels."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001892 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001893 "    $worksheet1->insert_chart( 'E2', $chart, { x_offset =>10, y_offset => 20 });"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001894 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001895 "The parameters C<x_scale> and C<y_scale> can be used to scale the inserted chart horizontally and vertically:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001896 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001897 "    # Scale the width by 120% and the height by 150%"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001898 "    $worksheet->insert_chart( 'E2', $chart, { y_scale => 1.2, y_scale => 1.5 } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001899 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001900 "The positioning of the chart when cells are resized can be set with the C<object_position> parameter:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001901 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001902 "    $worksheet->insert_chart( 'E2', $chart, { object_position => 2 } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001903 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001904 "The C<object_position> parameter can have one of the following allowable values:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001905 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001906 "    1. Move and size with cells."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001907 "    2. Move but don't size with cells."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001908 "    3. Don't move or size with cells."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001909 "    4. Same as Option 1, see below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001910 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001911 "Option 4 appears in Excel as Option 1. However, the worksheet object is sized to take hidden rows or columns into account. This is generally only useful for images and not for charts."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001912 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001913 "The C<description> parameter can be used to specify a description or \"alt text\" string for the chart. In general this would be used to provide a text description of the chart to help accessibility. It is an optional parameter and has no default. It can be used as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001914 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001915 "    $worksheet->insert_chart( 'E9', $chart, {description => 'Some alternative text'} );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001916 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001917 "The optional C<decorative> parameter is also used to help accessibility. It is used to mark the chart as decorative, and thus uninformative, for automated screen readers. As in Excel, if this parameter is in use the C<description> field isn't written. It is used as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001918 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001919 "    $worksheet->insert_chart( 'E9', $chart, {decorative => 1} );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001920 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001921 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001922 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001923 "=head2 insert_shape( $row, $col, $shape, $x, $y, $x_scale, $y_scale )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001924 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001925 "This method can be used to insert a Shape object into a worksheet. The Shape must be created by the C<add_shape()> Workbook method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001926 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001927 "    my $shape = $workbook->add_shape( name => 'My Shape', type => 'plus' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001928 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001929 "    # Configure the shape."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001930 "    $shape->set_text('foo');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001931 "    ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001932 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001933 "    # Insert the shape into the a worksheet."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001934 "    $worksheet->insert_shape( 'E2', $shape );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001935 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001936 "See C<add_shape()> for details on how to create the Shape object and L<Excel::Writer::XLSX::Shape> for details on how to configure it."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001937 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001938 "The C<$x>, C<$y>, C<$x_scale> and C<$y_scale> parameters are optional."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001939 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001940 "The parameters C<$x> and C<$y> can be used to specify an offset from the top left hand corner of the cell specified by C<$row> and C<$col>. The offset values are in pixels."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001941 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001942 "    $worksheet1->insert_shape( 'E2', $chart, 3, 3 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001943 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001944 "The parameters C<$x_scale> and C<$y_scale> can be used to scale the inserted shape horizontally and vertically:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001945 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001946 "    # Scale the width by 120% and the height by 150%"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001947 "    $worksheet->insert_shape( 'E2', $shape, 0, 0, 1.2, 1.5 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001948 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001949 "See also the C<shape*.pl> programs in the examples directory of the distro."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001950 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001951 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001952 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001953 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001954 "=head2 insert_button( $row, $col, { %options })"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001955 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001956 "The C<insert_button()> method can be used to insert an Excel form button into a worksheet."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001957 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001958 "This method is generally only useful when used in conjunction with the Workbook C<add_vba_project()> method to tie the button to a macro from an embedded VBA project:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001959 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001960 "    my $workbook  = Excel::Writer::XLSX->new( 'file.xlsm' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001961 "    ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001962 "    $workbook->add_vba_project( './vbaProject.bin' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001963 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001964 "    $worksheet->insert_button( 'C2', { macro => 'my_macro' } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001965 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001966 "The properties of the button that can be set are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001967 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001968 "    macro"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001969 "    caption"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001970 "    width"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001971 "    height"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001972 "    x_scale"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001973 "    y_scale"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001974 "    x_offset"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001975 "    y_offset"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001976 "    description"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001977 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001978 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001979 "=over"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001980 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001981 "=item Option: macro"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001982 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001983 "This option is used to set the macro that the button will invoke when the user clicks on it. The macro should be included using the Workbook C<add_vba_project()> method shown above."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001984 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001985 "    $worksheet->insert_button( 'C2', { macro => 'my_macro' } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001986 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001987 "The default macro is C<ButtonX_Click> where X is the button number."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001988 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001989 "=item Option: caption"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001990 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001991 "This option is used to set the caption on the button. The default is C<Button X> where X is the button number."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001992 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001993 "    $worksheet->insert_button( 'C2', { macro => 'my_macro', caption => 'Hello' } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001994 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001995 "=item Option: width"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001996 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001997 "This option is used to set the width of the button in pixels."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001998 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001999 "    $worksheet->insert_button( 'C2', { macro => 'my_macro', width => 128 } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002000 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002001 "The default button width is 64 pixels which is the width of a default cell."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002002 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002003 "=item Option: height"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002004 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002005 "This option is used to set the height of the button in pixels."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002006 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002007 "    $worksheet->insert_button( 'C2', { macro => 'my_macro', height => 40 } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002008 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002009 "The default button height is 20 pixels which is the height of a default cell."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002010 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002011 "=item Option: x_scale"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002012 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002013 "This option is used to set the width of the button as a factor of the default width."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002014 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002015 "    $worksheet->insert_button( 'C2', { macro => 'my_macro', x_scale => 2.0 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002016 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002017 "=item Option: y_scale"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002018 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002019 "This option is used to set the height of the button as a factor of the default height."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002020 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002021 "    $worksheet->insert_button( 'C2', { macro => 'my_macro', y_scale => 2.0 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002022 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002023 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002024 "=item Option: x_offset"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002025 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002026 "This option is used to change the x offset, in pixels, of a button within a cell:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002027 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002028 "    $worksheet->insert_button( 'C2', { macro => 'my_macro', x_offset => 2 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002029 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002030 "=item Option: y_offset"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002031 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002032 "This option is used to change the y offset, in pixels, of a comment within a cell."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002033 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002034 "=item Option: description"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002035 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002036 "The option is used to specify a description or \"alt text\" string for the button."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002037 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002038 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002039 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002040 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002041 "Note: Button is the only Excel form element that is available in Excel::Writer::XLSX. Form elements represent a lot of work to implement and the underlying VML syntax isn't very much fun."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002042 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002043 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002044 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002045 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002046 "=head2 data_validation()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002047 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002048 "The C<data_validation()> method is used to construct an Excel data validation or to limit the user input to a dropdown list of values."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002049 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002050 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002051 "    $worksheet->data_validation('B3',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002052 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002053 "            validate => 'integer',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002054 "            criteria => '>',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002055 "            value    => 100,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002056 "        });"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002057 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002058 "    $worksheet->data_validation('B5:B9',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002059 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002060 "            validate => 'list',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002061 "            value    => ['open', 'high', 'close'],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002062 "        });"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002063 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002064 "This method contains a lot of parameters and is described in detail in a separate section L</DATA VALIDATION IN EXCEL>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002065 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002066 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002067 "See also the C<data_validate.pl> program in the examples directory of the distro"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002068 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002069 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002070 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002071 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002072 "=head2 conditional_formatting()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002073 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002074 "The C<conditional_formatting()> method is used to add formatting to a cell or range of cells based on user defined criteria."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002075 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002076 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:J10',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002077 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002078 "            type     => 'cell',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002079 "            criteria => '>=',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002080 "            value    => 50,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002081 "            format   => $format1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002082 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002083 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002084 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002085 "This method contains a lot of parameters and is described in detail in a separate section L<CONDITIONAL FORMATTING IN EXCEL>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002086 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002087 "See also the C<conditional_format.pl> program in the examples directory of the distro"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002088 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002089 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002090 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002091 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002092 "=head2 add_sparkline()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002093 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002094 "The C<add_sparkline()> worksheet method is used to add sparklines to a cell or a range of cells."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002095 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002096 "    $worksheet->add_sparkline("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002097 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002098 "            location => 'F2',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002099 "            range    => 'Sheet1!A2:E2',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002100 "            type     => 'column',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002101 "            style    => 12,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002102 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002103 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002104 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002105 "This method contains a lot of parameters and is described in detail in a separate section L</SPARKLINES IN EXCEL>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002106 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002107 "See also the C<sparklines1.pl> and C<sparklines2.pl> example programs in the C<examples> directory of the distro."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002108 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002109 "B<Note:> Sparklines are a feature of Excel 2010+ only. You can write them to an XLSX file that can be read by Excel 2007 but they won't be displayed."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002110 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002111 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002112 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002113 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002114 "=head2 add_table()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002115 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002116 "The C<add_table()> method is used to group a range of cells into an Excel Table."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002117 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002118 "    $worksheet->add_table( 'B3:F7', { ... } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002119 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002120 "This method contains a lot of parameters and is described in detail in a separate section L<TABLES IN EXCEL>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002121 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002122 "See also the C<tables.pl> program in the examples directory of the distro"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002123 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002124 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002125 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002126 "=head2 get_name()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002127 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002128 "The C<get_name()> method is used to retrieve the name of a worksheet. For example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002129 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002130 "    for my $sheet ( $workbook->sheets() ) {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002131 "        print $sheet->get_name();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002132 "    }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002133 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002134 "For reasons related to the design of Excel::Writer::XLSX and to the internals of Excel there is no C<set_name()> method. The only way to set the worksheet name is via the C<add_worksheet()> method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002135 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002136 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002137 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002138 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002139 "=head2 activate()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002140 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002141 "The C<activate()> method is used to specify which worksheet is initially visible in a multi-sheet workbook:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002142 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002143 "    $worksheet1 = $workbook->add_worksheet( 'To' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002144 "    $worksheet2 = $workbook->add_worksheet( 'the' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002145 "    $worksheet3 = $workbook->add_worksheet( 'wind' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002146 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002147 "    $worksheet3->activate();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002148 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002149 "This is similar to the Excel VBA activate method. More than one worksheet can be selected via the C<select()> method, see below, however only one worksheet can be active."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002150 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002151 "The default active worksheet is the first worksheet."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002152 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002153 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002154 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002155 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002156 "=head2 select()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002157 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002158 "The C<select()> method is used to indicate that a worksheet is selected in a multi-sheet workbook:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002159 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002160 "    $worksheet1->activate();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002161 "    $worksheet2->select();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002162 "    $worksheet3->select();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002163 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002164 "A selected worksheet has its tab highlighted. Selecting worksheets is a way of grouping them together so that, for example, several worksheets could be printed in one go. A worksheet that has been activated via the C<activate()> method will also appear as selected."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002165 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002166 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002167 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002168 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002169 "=head2 hide()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002170 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002171 "The C<hide()> method is used to hide a worksheet:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002172 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002173 "    $worksheet2->hide();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002174 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002175 "You may wish to hide a worksheet in order to avoid confusing a user with intermediate data or calculations."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002176 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002177 "A hidden worksheet can not be activated or selected so this method is mutually exclusive with the C<activate()> and C<select()> methods. In addition, since the first worksheet will default to being the active worksheet, you cannot hide the first worksheet without activating another sheet:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002178 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002179 "    $worksheet2->activate();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002180 "    $worksheet1->hide();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002181 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002182 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002183 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002184 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002185 "=head2 very_hidden()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002186 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002187 "The C<very_hidden()> method can be used to hide a worksheet similar to the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002188 "C<hide()> method. The difference is that the worksheet cannot be unhidden in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002189 "the the Excel user interface. The Excel worksheet \"xlSheetVeryHidden\" option"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002190 "can only be unset programmatically by VBA."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002191 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002192 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002193 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002194 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002195 "=head2 set_first_sheet()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002196 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002197 "The C<activate()> method determines which worksheet is initially selected. However, if there are a large number of worksheets the selected worksheet may not appear on the screen. To avoid this you can select which is the leftmost visible worksheet using C<set_first_sheet()>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002198 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002199 "    for ( 1 .. 20 ) {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002200 "        $workbook->add_worksheet;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002201 "    }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002202 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002203 "    $worksheet21 = $workbook->add_worksheet();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002204 "    $worksheet22 = $workbook->add_worksheet();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002205 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002206 "    $worksheet21->set_first_sheet();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002207 "    $worksheet22->activate();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002208 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002209 "This method is not required very often. The default value is the first worksheet."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002210 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002211 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002212 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002213 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002214 "=head2 protect( $password, \\%options )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002215 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002216 "The C<protect()> method is used to protect a worksheet from modification:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002217 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002218 "    $worksheet->protect();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002219 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002220 "The C<protect()> method also has the effect of enabling a cell's C<locked> and C<hidden> properties if they have been set. A I<locked> cell cannot be edited and this property is on by default for all cells. A I<hidden> cell will display the results of a formula but not the formula itself."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002221 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002222 "See the C<protection.pl> program in the examples directory of the distro for an illustrative example and the C<set_locked> and C<set_hidden> format methods in L</CELL FORMATTING>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002223 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002224 "You can optionally add a password to the worksheet protection:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002225 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002226 "    $worksheet->protect( 'drowssap' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002227 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002228 "The password should be an ASCII string. Passing the empty string C<''> is the same as turning on protection without a password."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002229 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002230 "Note, the worksheet level password in Excel provides very weak protection. It does not encrypt your data and is very easy to deactivate. Full workbook encryption is not supported by C<Excel::Writer::XLSX> since it requires a completely different file format and would take several man months to implement."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002231 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002232 "You can specify which worksheet elements you wish to protect by passing a hash_ref with any or all of the following keys:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002233 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002234 "    # Default shown."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002235 "    %options = ("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002236 "        objects               => 0,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002237 "        scenarios             => 0,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002238 "        format_cells          => 0,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002239 "        format_columns        => 0,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002240 "        format_rows           => 0,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002241 "        insert_columns        => 0,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002242 "        insert_rows           => 0,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002243 "        insert_hyperlinks     => 0,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002244 "        delete_columns        => 0,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002245 "        delete_rows           => 0,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002246 "        select_locked_cells   => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002247 "        sort                  => 0,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002248 "        autofilter            => 0,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002249 "        pivot_tables          => 0,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002250 "        select_unlocked_cells => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002251 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002252 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002253 "The default boolean values are shown above. Individual elements can be protected as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002254 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002255 "    $worksheet->protect( 'drowssap', { insert_rows => 1 } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002256 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002257 "For chartsheets the allowable options and default values are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002258 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002259 "    %options = ("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002260 "        objects               => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002261 "        content               => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002262 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002263 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002264 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002265 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002266 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002267 "=head2 unprotect_range( $cell_range, $range_name )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002268 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002269 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002270 "The C<unprotect_range()> method is used to unprotect ranges in a protected worksheet. It can be used to set a single range or multiple ranges:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002271 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002272 "    $worksheet->unprotect_range( 'A1' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002273 "    $worksheet->unprotect_range( 'C1' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002274 "    $worksheet->unprotect_range( 'E1:E3' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002275 "    $worksheet->unprotect_range( 'G1:K100' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002276 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002277 "As in Excel the ranges are given sequential names like C<Range1> and C<Range2> but a user defined name can also be specified:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002278 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002279 "    $worksheet->unprotect_range( 'G4:I6', 'MyRange' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002280 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002281 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002282 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002283 "=head2 set_selection( $first_row, $first_col, $last_row, $last_col )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002284 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002285 "This method can be used to specify which cell or cells are selected in a worksheet. The most common requirement is to select a single cell, in which case C<$last_row> and C<$last_col> can be omitted. The active cell within a selected range is determined by the order in which C<$first> and C<$last> are specified. It is also possible to specify a cell or a range using A1 notation. See the note about L</Cell notation>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002286 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002287 "Examples:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002288 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002289 "    $worksheet1->set_selection( 3, 3 );          # 1. Cell D4."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002290 "    $worksheet2->set_selection( 3, 3, 6, 6 );    # 2. Cells D4 to G7."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002291 "    $worksheet3->set_selection( 6, 6, 3, 3 );    # 3. Cells G7 to D4."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002292 "    $worksheet4->set_selection( 'D4' );          # Same as 1."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002293 "    $worksheet5->set_selection( 'D4:G7' );       # Same as 2."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002294 "    $worksheet6->set_selection( 'G7:D4' );       # Same as 3."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002295 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002296 "The default cell selections is (0, 0), 'A1'."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002297 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002298 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002299 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002300 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002301 "=head2 set_top_left_cell( $row, $col )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002302 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002303 "This method can be used to set the top leftmost visible cell in the worksheet:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002304 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002305 "    $worksheet->set_top_left_cell( 31, 26 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002306 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002307 "    # Same as:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002308 "    $worksheet->set_top_left_cell( 'AA32' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002309 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002310 "You can also use A1 notation, as shown above, see the note about L</Cell notation>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002311 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002312 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002313 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002314 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002315 "=head2 set_row( $row, $height, $format, $hidden, $level, $collapsed )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002316 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002317 "This method can be used to change the default properties of a row. All parameters apart from C<$row> are optional."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002318 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002319 "The most common use for this method is to change the height of a row."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002320 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002321 "    $worksheet->set_row( 0, 20 );    # Row 1 height set to 20"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002322 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002323 "Note: the row height is in Excel character units. To set the height in pixels use the C<set_row_pixels()> method, see below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002324 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002325 "If you wish to set the format without changing the height you can pass C<undef> as the height parameter:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002326 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002327 "    $worksheet->set_row( 0, undef, $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002328 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002329 "The C<$format> parameter will be applied to any cells in the row that don't have a format. For example"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002330 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002331 "    $worksheet->set_row( 0, undef, $format1 );    # Set the format for row 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002332 "    $worksheet->write( 'A1', 'Hello' );           # Defaults to $format1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002333 "    $worksheet->write( 'B1', 'Hello', $format2 ); # Keeps $format2"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002334 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002335 "If you wish to define a row format in this way you should call the method before any calls to C<write()>. Calling it afterwards will overwrite any format that was previously specified."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002336 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002337 "The C<$hidden> parameter should be set to 1 if you wish to hide a row. This can be used, for example, to hide intermediary steps in a complicated calculation:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002338 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002339 "    $worksheet->set_row( 0, 20,    $format, 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002340 "    $worksheet->set_row( 1, undef, undef,   1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002341 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002342 "The C<$level> parameter is used to set the outline level of the row. Outlines are described in L</OUTLINES AND GROUPING IN EXCEL>. Adjacent rows with the same outline level are grouped together into a single outline."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002343 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002344 "The following example sets an outline level of 1 for rows 2 and 3 (zero-indexed):"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002345 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002346 "    $worksheet->set_row( 1, undef, undef, 0, 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002347 "    $worksheet->set_row( 2, undef, undef, 0, 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002348 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002349 "The C<$hidden> parameter can also be used to hide collapsed outlined rows when used in conjunction with the C<$level> parameter."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002350 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002351 "    $worksheet->set_row( 1, undef, undef, 1, 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002352 "    $worksheet->set_row( 2, undef, undef, 1, 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002353 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002354 "For collapsed outlines you should also indicate which row has the collapsed C<+> symbol using the optional C<$collapsed> parameter."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002355 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002356 "    $worksheet->set_row( 3, undef, undef, 0, 0, 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002357 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002358 "For a more complete example see the C<outline.pl> and C<outline_collapsed.pl> programs in the examples directory of the distro."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002359 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002360 "Excel allows up to 7 outline levels. Therefore the C<$level> parameter should be in the range C<0 E<lt>= $level E<lt>= 7>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002361 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002362 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002363 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002364 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002365 "=head2 set_row_pixels( $row, $height, $format, $hidden, $level, $collapsed )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002366 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002367 "This method is the same as C<set_row()> except that C<$height> is in pixels."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002368 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002369 "    $worksheet->set_row       ( 0, 24 );    # Set row height in character units"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002370 "    $worksheet->set_row_pixels( 1, 18 );    # Set row to same height in pixels"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002371 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002372 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002373 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002374 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002375 "=head2 set_column( $first_col, $last_col, $width, $format, $hidden, $level, $collapsed )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002376 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002377 "This method can be used to change the default properties of a single column or a range of columns. All parameters apart from C<$first_col> and C<$last_col> are optional."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002378 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002379 "If C<set_column()> is applied to a single column the value of C<$first_col> and C<$last_col> should be the same. In the case where C<$last_col> is zero it is set to the same value as C<$first_col>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002380 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002381 "It is also possible, and generally clearer, to specify a column range using the form of A1 notation used for columns. See the note about L</Cell notation>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002382 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002383 "Examples:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002384 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002385 "    $worksheet->set_column( 0, 0, 20 );    # Column  A   width set to 20"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002386 "    $worksheet->set_column( 1, 3, 30 );    # Columns B-D width set to 30"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002387 "    $worksheet->set_column( 'E:E', 20 );   # Column  E   width set to 20"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002388 "    $worksheet->set_column( 'F:H', 30 );   # Columns F-H width set to 30"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002389 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002390 "The width corresponds to the column width value that is specified in Excel. It is approximately equal to the length of a string in the default font of Calibri 11. To set the width in pixels use the C<set_column_pixels()> method, see below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002391 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002392 "See also the C<autofit()> method to set the column widths based on the data in the column, approximately."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002393 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002394 "As usual the C<$format> parameter is optional, for additional information, see L</CELL FORMATTING>. If you wish to set the format without changing the width you can pass C<undef> as the width parameter:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002395 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002396 "    $worksheet->set_column( 0, 0, undef, $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002397 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002398 "The C<$format> parameter will be applied to any cells in the column that don't have a format. For example"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002399 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002400 "    $worksheet->set_column( 'A:A', undef, $format1 );    # Set format for col 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002401 "    $worksheet->write( 'A1', 'Hello' );                  # Defaults to $format1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002402 "    $worksheet->write( 'A2', 'Hello', $format2 );        # Keeps $format2"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002403 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002404 "If you wish to define a column format in this way you should call the method before any calls to C<write()>. If you call it afterwards it won't have any effect."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002405 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002406 "A default row format takes precedence over a default column format"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002407 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002408 "    $worksheet->set_row( 0, undef, $format1 );           # Set format for row 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002409 "    $worksheet->set_column( 'A:A', undef, $format2 );    # Set format for col 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002410 "    $worksheet->write( 'A1', 'Hello' );                  # Defaults to $format1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002411 "    $worksheet->write( 'A2', 'Hello' );                  # Defaults to $format2"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002412 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002413 "The C<$hidden> parameter should be set to 1 if you wish to hide a column. This can be used, for example, to hide intermediary steps in a complicated calculation:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002414 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002415 "    $worksheet->set_column( 'D:D', 20,    $format, 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002416 "    $worksheet->set_column( 'E:E', undef, undef,   1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002417 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002418 "The C<$level> parameter is used to set the outline level of the column. Outlines are described in L</OUTLINES AND GROUPING IN EXCEL>. Adjacent columns with the same outline level are grouped together into a single outline."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002419 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002420 "The following example sets an outline level of 1 for columns B to G:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002421 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002422 "    $worksheet->set_column( 'B:G', undef, undef, 0, 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002423 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002424 "The C<$hidden> parameter can also be used to hide collapsed outlined columns when used in conjunction with the C<$level> parameter."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002425 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002426 "    $worksheet->set_column( 'B:G', undef, undef, 1, 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002427 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002428 "For collapsed outlines you should also indicate which row has the collapsed C<+> symbol using the optional C<$collapsed> parameter."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002429 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002430 "    $worksheet->set_column( 'H:H', undef, undef, 0, 0, 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002431 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002432 "For a more complete example see the C<outline.pl> and C<outline_collapsed.pl> programs in the examples directory of the distro."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002433 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002434 "Excel allows up to 7 outline levels. Therefore the C<$level> parameter should be in the range C<0 E<lt>= $level E<lt>= 7>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002435 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002436 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002437 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002438 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002439 "=head2 set_column_pixels( $first_col, $last_col, $width, $format, $hidden, $level, $collapsed )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002440 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002441 "This method is the same as C<set_column()> except that C<$width> is in pixels."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002442 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002443 "    $worksheet->set_column       ( 0, 0, 10 ); # Column A width set to 10 in character units"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002444 "    $worksheet->set_column_pixels( 1, 1, 75 ); # Column B set to the same width in pixels"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002445 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002446 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002447 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002448 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002449 "=head2 set_default_row( $height, $hide_unused_rows )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002450 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002451 "The C<set_default_row()> method is used to set the limited number of default row properties allowed by Excel. These are the default height and the option to hide unused rows."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002452 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002453 "    $worksheet->set_default_row( 24 );  # Set the default row height to 24."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002454 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002455 "The option to hide unused rows is used by Excel as an optimisation so that the user can hide a large number of rows without generating a very large file with an entry for each hidden row."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002456 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002457 "    $worksheet->set_default_row( undef, 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002458 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002459 "See the C<hide_row_col.pl> example program."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002460 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002461 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002462 "=head2 autofit($max_width)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002463 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002464 "Autofit the worksheet column widths to the widest data in the column, approximately."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002465 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002466 "    $worksheet->autofit();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002467 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002468 "Excel autofits columns at runtime when it has access to all of the required worksheet information as well as the Windows functions for calculating display areas based on fonts and formatting. Excel::Writer::XLSX doesn't have access to these Windows functions so it simulates autofit by calculating string widths based on metrics taken from Excel. This isn't perfect but for most cases it should be sufficient and indistinguishable from the output of Excel. However there are some limitations to be aware of when using this method:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002469 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002470 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002471 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002472 "=item * It is based on the default Excel font type and size of Calibri 11. It will not give accurate results for other fonts or font sizes."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002473 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002474 "=item * It doesn't take formatting of numbers or dates account, although this may be addressed in a later version."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002475 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002476 "=item * Autofit is a relatively expensive operation since it performs a calculation for all the populated cells in a worksheet. See the note on performance below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002477 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002478 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002479 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002480 "For cases that don't match your desired output you can set explicit column widths via C<set_column()> or C<set_column_pixels()> method ignores columns that have already been explicitly set if the width is greater than the calculated autofit width. Alternatively, setting the column width explicitly after calling C<autofit()> will override the autofit value. You can also set an upper limit using the optional C<$max_width> parameter as explained below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002481 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002482 "Excel autofits very long strings up to limit of 1790 pixels/255 characters. This is often too wide to display on a single screen at normal zoom. As such the optional C<$max_width> parameter is provided to enable a smaller upper pixel limit for autofitting long strings. A value of 300 pixels is recommended as a good compromise between column width and readability:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002483 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002484 "    $worksheet->autofit(300);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002485 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002486 "If you need more control over the autofit effect you can use the L<Excel::Writer::XLSX::Utility> C<xl_cell_autofit_width()> function to calculate the autofit width for a cell value. This is the same calculation used internally by C<autofit()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002487 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002488 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002489 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002490 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002491 "=head2 outline_settings( $visible, $symbols_below, $symbols_right, $auto_style )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002492 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002493 "The C<outline_settings()> method is used to control the appearance of outlines in Excel. Outlines are described in L</OUTLINES AND GROUPING IN EXCEL>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002494 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002495 "The C<$visible> parameter is used to control whether or not outlines are visible. Setting this parameter to 0 will cause all outlines on the worksheet to be hidden. They can be unhidden in Excel by means of the \"Show Outline Symbols\" command button. The default setting is 1 for visible outlines."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002496 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002497 "    $worksheet->outline_settings( 0 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002498 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002499 "The C<$symbols_below> parameter is used to control whether the row outline symbol will appear above or below the outline level bar. The default setting is 1 for symbols to appear below the outline level bar."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002500 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002501 "The C<$symbols_right> parameter is used to control whether the column outline symbol will appear to the left or the right of the outline level bar. The default setting is 1 for symbols to appear to the right of the outline level bar."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002502 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002503 "The C<$auto_style> parameter is used to control whether the automatic outline generator in Excel uses automatic styles when creating an outline. This has no effect on a file generated by C<Excel::Writer::XLSX> but it does have an effect on how the worksheet behaves after it is created. The default setting is 0 for \"Automatic Styles\" to be turned off."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002504 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002505 "The default settings for all of these parameters correspond to Excel's default parameters."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002506 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002507 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002508 "The worksheet parameters controlled by C<outline_settings()> are rarely used."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002509 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002510 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002511 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002512 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002513 "=head2 freeze_panes( $row, $col, $top_row, $left_col )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002514 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002515 "This method can be used to divide a worksheet into horizontal or vertical regions known as panes and to also \"freeze\" these panes so that the splitter bars are not visible. This is the same as the C<Window-E<gt>Freeze Panes> menu command in Excel"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002516 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002517 "The parameters C<$row> and C<$col> are used to specify the location of the split. It should be noted that the split is specified at the top or left of a cell and that the method uses zero based indexing. Therefore to freeze the first row of a worksheet it is necessary to specify the split at row 2 (which is 1 as the zero-based index). This might lead you to think that you are using a 1 based index but this is not the case."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002518 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002519 "You can set one of the C<$row> and C<$col> parameters as zero if you do not want either a vertical or horizontal split."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002520 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002521 "Examples:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002522 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002523 "    $worksheet->freeze_panes( 1, 0 );    # Freeze the first row"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002524 "    $worksheet->freeze_panes( 'A2' );    # Same using A1 notation"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002525 "    $worksheet->freeze_panes( 0, 1 );    # Freeze the first column"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002526 "    $worksheet->freeze_panes( 'B1' );    # Same using A1 notation"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002527 "    $worksheet->freeze_panes( 1, 2 );    # Freeze first row and first 2 columns"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002528 "    $worksheet->freeze_panes( 'C2' );    # Same using A1 notation"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002529 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002530 "The parameters C<$top_row> and C<$left_col> are optional. They are used to specify the top-most or left-most visible row or column in the scrolling region of the panes. For example to freeze the first row and to have the scrolling region begin at row twenty:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002531 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002532 "    $worksheet->freeze_panes( 1, 0, 20, 0 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002533 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002534 "You cannot use A1 notation for the C<$top_row> and C<$left_col> parameters."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002535 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002536 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002537 "See also the C<panes.pl> program in the C<examples> directory of the distribution."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002538 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002539 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002540 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002541 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002542 "=head2 split_panes( $y, $x, $top_row, $left_col )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002543 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002544 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002545 "This method can be used to divide a worksheet into horizontal or vertical regions known as panes. This method is different from the C<freeze_panes()> method in that the splits between the panes will be visible to the user and each pane will have its own scroll bars."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002546 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002547 "The parameters C<$y> and C<$x> are used to specify the vertical and horizontal position of the split. The units for C<$y> and C<$x> are the same as those used by Excel to specify row height and column width. However, the vertical and horizontal units are different from each other. Therefore you must specify the C<$y> and C<$x> parameters in terms of the row heights and column widths that you have set or the default values which are C<15> for a row and C<8.43> for a column."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002548 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002549 "You can set one of the C<$y> and C<$x> parameters as zero if you do not want either a vertical or horizontal split. The parameters C<$top_row> and C<$left_col> are optional. They are used to specify the top-most or left-most visible row or column in the bottom-right pane."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002550 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002551 "Example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002552 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002553 "    $worksheet->split_panes( 15, 0,   );    # First row"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002554 "    $worksheet->split_panes( 0,  8.43 );    # First column"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002555 "    $worksheet->split_panes( 15, 8.43 );    # First row and column"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002556 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002557 "You cannot use A1 notation with this method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002558 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002559 "See also the C<freeze_panes()> method and the C<panes.pl> program in the C<examples> directory of the distribution."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002560 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002561 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002562 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002563 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002564 "=head2 merge_range( $first_row, $first_col, $last_row, $last_col, $token, $format )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002565 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002566 "The C<merge_range()> method allows you to merge cells that contain other types of alignment in addition to the merging:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002567 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002568 "    my $format = $workbook->add_format("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002569 "        border => 6,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002570 "        valign => 'vcenter',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002571 "        align  => 'center',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002572 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002573 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002574 "    $worksheet->merge_range( 'B3:D4', 'Vertical and horizontal', $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002575 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002576 "C<merge_range()> writes its C<$token> argument using the worksheet C<write()> method. Therefore it will handle numbers, strings, formulas or urls as required. If you need to specify the required C<write_*()> method use the C<merge_range_type()> method, see below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002577 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002578 "The full possibilities of this method are shown in the C<merge3.pl> to C<merge6.pl> programs in the C<examples> directory of the distribution."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002579 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002580 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002581 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002582 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002583 "=head2 merge_range_type( $type, $first_row, $first_col, $last_row, $last_col, ... )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002584 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002585 "The C<merge_range()> method, see above, uses C<write()> to insert the required data into to a merged range. However, there may be times where this isn't what you require so as an alternative the C<merge_range_type ()> method allows you to specify the type of data you wish to write. For example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002586 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002587 "    $worksheet->merge_range_type( 'number',  'B2:C2', 123,    $format1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002588 "    $worksheet->merge_range_type( 'string',  'B4:C4', 'foo',  $format2 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002589 "    $worksheet->merge_range_type( 'formula', 'B6:C6', '=1+2', $format3 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002590 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002591 "The C<$type> must be one of the following, which corresponds to a C<write_*()> method:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002592 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002593 "    'number'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002594 "    'string'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002595 "    'formula'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002596 "    'array_formula'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002597 "    'blank'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002598 "    'rich_string'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002599 "    'date_time'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002600 "    'url'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002601 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002602 "Any arguments after the range should be whatever the appropriate method accepts:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002603 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002604 "    $worksheet->merge_range_type( 'rich_string', 'B8:C8',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002605 "                                  'This is ', $bold, 'bold', $format4 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002606 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002607 "Note, you must always pass a C<$format> object as an argument, even if it is a default format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002608 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002609 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002610 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002611 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002612 "=head2 set_zoom( $scale )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002613 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002614 "Set the worksheet zoom factor in the range C<10 E<lt>= $scale E<lt>= 400>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002615 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002616 "    $worksheet1->set_zoom( 50 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002617 "    $worksheet2->set_zoom( 75 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002618 "    $worksheet3->set_zoom( 300 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002619 "    $worksheet4->set_zoom( 400 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002620 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002621 "The default zoom factor is 100. You cannot zoom to \"Selection\" because it is calculated by Excel at run-time."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002622 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002623 "Note, C<set_zoom()> does not affect the scale of the printed page. For that you should use C<set_print_scale()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002624 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002625 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002626 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002627 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002628 "=head2 right_to_left()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002629 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002630 "The C<right_to_left()> method is used to change the default direction of the worksheet from left-to-right, with the A1 cell in the top left, to right-to-left, with the A1 cell in the top right."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002631 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002632 "    $worksheet->right_to_left();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002633 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002634 "This is useful when creating Arabic, Hebrew or other near or far eastern worksheets that use right-to-left as the default direction."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002635 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002636 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002637 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002638 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002639 "=head2 hide_zero()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002640 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002641 "The C<hide_zero()> method is used to hide any zero values that appear in cells."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002642 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002643 "    $worksheet->hide_zero();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002644 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002645 "In Excel this option is found under Tools->Options->View."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002646 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002647 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002648 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002649 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002650 "=head2 set_background( $filename )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002651 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002652 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002653 "The C<set_background()> method can be used to set the background image for the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002654 "worksheet:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002655 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002656 "    $worksheet->set_background( 'logo.png' )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002657 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002658 "The C<set_background()> method supports all the image formats supported by"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002659 "C<insert_image()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002660 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002661 "Some people use this method to add a watermark background to their"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002662 "document. However, Microsoft recommends using a header image L<to set a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002663 "watermark|https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/add-a-watermark-in-excel-a372182a-d733-484e-825c-18ddf3edf009>. The"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002664 "choice of method depends on whether you want the watermark to be visible in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002665 "normal viewing mode or just when the file is printed. In Excel::Writer::XLSX"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002666 "you can get the header watermark effect using C<set_header()>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002667 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002668 "    $worksheet->set_header( '&C&G', undef, { image_center => 'watermark.png' } )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002669 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002670 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002671 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002672 "=head2 set_tab_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002673 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002674 "The C<set_tab_color()> method is used to change the colour of the worksheet tab. You can use one of the standard colour names provided by the Format object or a Html style C<#RRGGBB> colour. See L</WORKING WITH COLOURS>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002675 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002676 "    $worksheet1->set_tab_color( 'red' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002677 "    $worksheet2->set_tab_color( '#FF6600' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002678 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002679 "See the C<tab_colors.pl> program in the examples directory of the distro."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002680 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002681 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002682 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002683 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002684 "=head2 autofilter( $first_row, $first_col, $last_row, $last_col )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002685 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002686 "This method allows an autofilter to be added to a worksheet. An autofilter is a way of adding drop down lists to the headers of a 2D range of worksheet data. This allows users to filter the data based on simple criteria so that some data is shown and some is hidden."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002687 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002688 "To add an autofilter to a worksheet:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002689 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002690 "    $worksheet->autofilter( 0, 0, 10, 3 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002691 "    $worksheet->autofilter( 'A1:D11' );    # Same as above in A1 notation."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002692 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002693 "Filter conditions can be applied using the C<filter_column()> or C<filter_column_list()> method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002694 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002695 "See the C<autofilter.pl> program in the examples directory of the distro for a more detailed example."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002696 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002697 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002698 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002699 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002700 "=head2 filter_column( $column, $expression )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002701 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002702 "The C<filter_column> method can be used to filter columns in a autofilter range based on simple conditions."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002703 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002704 "B<NOTE:> It isn't sufficient to just specify the filter condition. You must also hide any rows that don't match the filter condition. Rows are hidden using the C<set_row()> C<visible> parameter. C<Excel::Writer::XLSX> cannot do this automatically since it isn't part of the file format. See the C<autofilter.pl> program in the examples directory of the distro for an example."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002705 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002706 "The conditions for the filter are specified using simple expressions:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002707 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002708 "    $worksheet->filter_column( 'A', 'x > 2000' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002709 "    $worksheet->filter_column( 'B', 'x > 2000 and x < 5000' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002710 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002711 "The C<$column> parameter can either be a zero indexed column number or a string column name."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002712 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002713 "The following operators are available:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002714 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002715 "    Operator        Synonyms"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002716 "       ==           =   eq  =~"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002717 "       !=           <>  ne  !="
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002718 "       >"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002719 "       <"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002720 "       >="
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002721 "       <="
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002722 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002723 "       and          &&"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002724 "       or           ||"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002725 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002726 "The operator synonyms are just syntactic sugar to make you more comfortable using the expressions. It is important to remember that the expressions will be interpreted by Excel and not by perl."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002727 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002728 "An expression can comprise a single statement or two statements separated by the C<and> and C<or> operators. For example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002729 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002730 "    'x <  2000'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002731 "    'x >  2000'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002732 "    'x == 2000'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002733 "    'x >  2000 and x <  5000'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002734 "    'x == 2000 or  x == 5000'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002735 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002736 "Filtering of blank or non-blank data can be achieved by using a value of C<Blanks> or C<NonBlanks> in the expression:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002737 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002738 "    'x == Blanks'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002739 "    'x == NonBlanks'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002740 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002741 "Excel also allows some simple string matching operations:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002742 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002743 "    'x =~ b*'   # begins with b"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002744 "    'x !~ b*'   # doesn't begin with b"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002745 "    'x =~ *b'   # ends with b"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002746 "    'x !~ *b'   # doesn't end with b"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002747 "    'x =~ *b*'  # contains b"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002748 "    'x !~ *b*'  # doesn't contains b"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002749 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002750 "You can also use C<*> to match any character or number and C<?> to match any single character or number. No other regular expression quantifier is supported by Excel's filters. Excel's regular expression characters can be escaped using C<~>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002751 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002752 "The placeholder variable C<x> in the above examples can be replaced by any simple string. The actual placeholder name is ignored internally so the following are all equivalent:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002753 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002754 "    'x     < 2000'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002755 "    'col   < 2000'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002756 "    'Price < 2000'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002757 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002758 "Also, note that a filter condition can only be applied to a column in a range specified by the C<autofilter()> Worksheet method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002759 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002760 "See the C<autofilter.pl> program in the examples directory of the distro for a more detailed example."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002761 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002762 "B<Note> L<Spreadsheet::WriteExcel> supports Top 10 style filters. These aren't currently supported by Excel::Writer::XLSX but may be added later."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002763 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002764 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002765 "=head2 filter_column_list( $column, @matches )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002766 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002767 "Prior to Excel 2007 it was only possible to have either 1 or 2 filter conditions such as the ones shown above in the C<filter_column> method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002768 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002769 "Excel 2007 introduced a new list style filter where it is possible to specify 1 or more 'or' style criteria. For example if your column contained data for the first six months the initial data would be displayed as all selected as shown on the left. Then if you selected 'March', 'April' and 'May' they would be displayed as shown on the right."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002770 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002771 "    No criteria selected      Some criteria selected."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002772 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002773 "    [/] (Select all)          [X] (Select all)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002774 "    [/] January               [ ] January"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002775 "    [/] February              [ ] February"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002776 "    [/] March                 [/] March"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002777 "    [/] April                 [/] April"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002778 "    [/] May                   [/] May"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002779 "    [/] June                  [ ] June"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002780 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002781 "The C<filter_column_list()> method can be used to represent these types of filters:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002782 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002783 "    $worksheet->filter_column_list( 'A', 'March', 'April', 'May' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002784 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002785 "The C<$column> parameter can either be a zero indexed column number or a string column name."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002786 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002787 "One or more criteria can be selected:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002788 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002789 "    $worksheet->filter_column_list( 0, 'March' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002790 "    $worksheet->filter_column_list( 1, 100, 110, 120, 130 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002791 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002792 "B<NOTE:> It isn't sufficient to just specify the filter condition. You must also hide any rows that don't match the filter condition. Rows are hidden using the C<set_row()> C<visible> parameter. C<Excel::Writer::XLSX> cannot do this automatically since it isn't part of the file format. See the C<autofilter.pl> program in the examples directory of the distro for an example."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002793 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002794 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002795 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002796 "=head2 convert_date_time( $date_string )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002797 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002798 "The C<convert_date_time()> method is used internally by the C<write_date_time()> method to convert date strings to a number that represents an Excel date and time."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002799 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002800 "It is exposed as a public method for utility purposes."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002801 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002802 "The C<$date_string> format is detailed in the C<write_date_time()> method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002803 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002804 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002805 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002806 "=head2 set_vba_name()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002807 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002808 "The Worksheet C<set_vba_name()> method can be used to set the VBA codename for the worksheet (there is a similar method for the workbook VBA name). This is sometimes required when a C<vbaProject> macro included via C<add_vba_project()> refers to the worksheet. The default Excel VBA name of C<Sheet1>, etc., is used if a user defined name isn't specified."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002809 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002810 "See also L<WORKING WITH VBA MACROS>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002811 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002812 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002813 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002814 "=head2 ignore_errors()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002815 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002816 "The C<ignore_errors()> method can be used to ignore various worksheet cell errors/warnings. For example the following code writes a string that looks like a number:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002817 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002818 "    $worksheet->write_string('D2', '123');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002819 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002820 "This causes Excel to display a small green triangle in the top left hand corner of the cell to indicate an error/warning."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002821 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002822 "Sometimes these warnings are useful indicators that there is an issue in the spreadsheet but sometimes it is preferable to turn them off. Warnings can be turned off at the Excel level for all workbooks and worksheets by using the using \"Excel options -> Formulas -> Error checking rules\". Alternatively you can turn them off for individual cells in a worksheet, or ranges of cells, using the C<ignore_errors()> method with a hashref of options and ranges like this:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002823 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002824 "    $worksheet->ignore_errors({number_stored_as_text => 'A1:H50'});"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002825 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002826 "    # Or for more than one option:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002827 "    $worksheet->ignore_errors({number_stored_as_text => 'A1:H50',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002828 "                               eval_error =>            'A1:H50'});"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002829 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002830 "The range can be a single cell, a range of cells, or multiple cells and ranges separated by spaces:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002831 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002832 "    # Single cell."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002833 "    $worksheet->ignore_errors({eval_error => 'C6'});"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002834 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002835 "    # Or a single range:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002836 "    $worksheet->ignore_errors({eval_error => 'C6:G8'});"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002837 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002838 "    # Or multiple cells and ranges:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002839 "    $worksheet->ignore_errors({eval_error => 'C6 E6 G1:G20 J2:J6'});"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002840 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002841 "Note: calling C<ignore_errors> multiple times will overwrite the previous settings."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002842 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002843 "You can turn off warnings for an entire column by specifying the range from the first cell in the column to the last cell in the column:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002844 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002845 "    $worksheet->ignore_errors({number_stored_as_text => 'A1:A1048576'});"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002846 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002847 "Or for the entire worksheet by specifying the range from the first cell in the worksheet to the last cell in the worksheet:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002848 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002849 "    $worksheet->ignore_errors({number_stored_as_text => 'A1:XFD1048576'});"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002850 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002851 "The worksheet errors/warnings that can be ignored are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002852 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002853 "=over"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002854 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002855 "=item * C<number_stored_as_text>: Turn off errors/warnings for numbers stores as text."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002856 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002857 "=item * C<eval_error>: Turn off errors/warnings for formula errors (such as divide by zero)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002858 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002859 "=item * C<formula_differs>: Turn off errors/warnings for formulas that differ from surrounding formulas."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002860 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002861 "=item * C<formula_range>: Turn off errors/warnings for formulas that omit cells in a range."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002862 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002863 "=item * C<formula_unlocked>: Turn off errors/warnings for unlocked cells that contain formulas."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002864 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002865 "=item * C<empty_cell_reference>: Turn off errors/warnings for formulas that refer to empty cells."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002866 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002867 "=item * C<list_data_validation>: Turn off errors/warnings for cells in a table that do not comply with applicable data validation rules."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002868 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002869 "=item * C<calculated_column>: Turn off errors/warnings for cell formulas that differ from the column formula."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002870 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002871 "=item * C<two_digit_text_year>: Turn off errors/warnings for formulas that contain a two digit text representation of a year."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002872 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002873 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002874 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002875 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002876 "=head1 PAGE SET-UP METHODS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002877 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002878 "Page set-up methods affect the way that a worksheet looks when it is printed. They control features such as page headers and footers and margins. These methods are really just standard worksheet methods. They are documented here in a separate section for the sake of clarity."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002879 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002880 "The following methods are available for page set-up:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002881 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002882 "    set_landscape()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002883 "    set_portrait()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002884 "    set_page_view()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002885 "    set_pagebreak_view()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002886 "    set_paper()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002887 "    center_horizontally()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002888 "    center_vertically()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002889 "    set_margins()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002890 "    set_header()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002891 "    set_footer()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002892 "    repeat_rows()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002893 "    repeat_columns()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002894 "    hide_gridlines()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002895 "    print_row_col_headers()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002896 "    print_area()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002897 "    print_across()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002898 "    fit_to_pages()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002899 "    set_start_page()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002900 "    set_print_scale()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002901 "    print_black_and_white()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002902 "    set_h_pagebreaks()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002903 "    set_v_pagebreaks()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002904 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002905 "A common requirement when working with Excel::Writer::XLSX is to apply the same page set-up features to all of the worksheets in a workbook. To do this you can use the C<sheets()> method of the C<workbook> class to access the array of worksheets in a workbook:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002906 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002907 "    for $worksheet ( $workbook->sheets() ) {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002908 "        $worksheet->set_landscape();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002909 "    }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002910 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002911 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002912 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002913 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002914 "=head2 set_landscape()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002915 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002916 "This method is used to set the orientation of a worksheet's printed page to landscape:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002917 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002918 "    $worksheet->set_landscape();    # Landscape mode"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002919 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002920 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002921 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002922 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002923 "=head2 set_portrait()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002924 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002925 "This method is used to set the orientation of a worksheet's printed page to portrait. The default worksheet orientation is portrait, so you won't generally need to call this method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002926 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002927 "    $worksheet->set_portrait();    # Portrait mode"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002928 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002929 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002930 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002931 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002932 "=head2 set_page_view()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002933 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002934 "This method is used to display the worksheet in \"Page View/Layout\" mode."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002935 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002936 "    $worksheet->set_page_view();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002937 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002938 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002939 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002940 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002941 "=head2 set_pagebreak_view()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002942 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002943 "This method is used to display the worksheet in \"Page Break Preview\" mode."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002944 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002945 "    $worksheet->set_pagebreak_view();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002946 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002947 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002948 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002949 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002950 "=head2 set_paper( $index )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002951 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002952 "This method is used to set the paper format for the printed output of a worksheet. The following paper styles are available:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002953 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002954 "    Index   Paper format            Paper size"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002955 "    =====   ============            =========="
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002956 "      0     Printer default         -"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002957 "      1     Letter                  8 1/2 x 11 in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002958 "      2     Letter Small            8 1/2 x 11 in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002959 "      3     Tabloid                 11 x 17 in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002960 "      4     Ledger                  17 x 11 in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002961 "      5     Legal                   8 1/2 x 14 in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002962 "      6     Statement               5 1/2 x 8 1/2 in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002963 "      7     Executive               7 1/4 x 10 1/2 in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002964 "      8     A3                      297 x 420 mm"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002965 "      9     A4                      210 x 297 mm"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002966 "     10     A4 Small                210 x 297 mm"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002967 "     11     A5                      148 x 210 mm"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002968 "     12     B4                      250 x 354 mm"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002969 "     13     B5                      182 x 257 mm"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002970 "     14     Folio                   8 1/2 x 13 in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002971 "     15     Quarto                  215 x 275 mm"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002972 "     16     -                       10x14 in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002973 "     17     -                       11x17 in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002974 "     18     Note                    8 1/2 x 11 in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002975 "     19     Envelope  9             3 7/8 x 8 7/8"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002976 "     20     Envelope 10             4 1/8 x 9 1/2"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002977 "     21     Envelope 11             4 1/2 x 10 3/8"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002978 "     22     Envelope 12             4 3/4 x 11"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002979 "     23     Envelope 14             5 x 11 1/2"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002980 "     24     C size sheet            -"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002981 "     25     D size sheet            -"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002982 "     26     E size sheet            -"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002983 "     27     Envelope DL             110 x 220 mm"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002984 "     28     Envelope C3             324 x 458 mm"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002985 "     29     Envelope C4             229 x 324 mm"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002986 "     30     Envelope C5             162 x 229 mm"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002987 "     31     Envelope C6             114 x 162 mm"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002988 "     32     Envelope C65            114 x 229 mm"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002989 "     33     Envelope B4             250 x 353 mm"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002990 "     34     Envelope B5             176 x 250 mm"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002991 "     35     Envelope B6             176 x 125 mm"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002992 "     36     Envelope                110 x 230 mm"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002993 "     37     Monarch                 3.875 x 7.5 in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002994 "     38     Envelope                3 5/8 x 6 1/2 in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002995 "     39     Fanfold                 14 7/8 x 11 in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002996 "     40     German Std Fanfold      8 1/2 x 12 in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002997 "     41     German Legal Fanfold    8 1/2 x 13 in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002998 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002999 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003000 "Note, it is likely that not all of these paper types will be available to the end user since it will depend on the paper formats that the user's printer supports. Therefore, it is best to stick to standard paper types."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003001 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003002 "    $worksheet->set_paper( 1 );    # US Letter"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003003 "    $worksheet->set_paper( 9 );    # A4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003004 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003005 "If you do not specify a paper type the worksheet will print using the printer's default paper."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003006 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003007 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003008 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003009 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003010 "=head2 center_horizontally()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003011 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003012 "Center the worksheet data horizontally between the margins on the printed page:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003013 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003014 "    $worksheet->center_horizontally();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003015 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003016 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003017 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003018 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003019 "=head2 center_vertically()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003020 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003021 "Center the worksheet data vertically between the margins on the printed page:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003022 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003023 "    $worksheet->center_vertically();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003024 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003025 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003026 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003027 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003028 "=head2 set_margins( $inches )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003029 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003030 "There are several methods available for setting the worksheet margins on the printed page:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003031 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003032 "    set_margins()        # Set all margins to the same value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003033 "    set_margins_LR()     # Set left and right margins to the same value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003034 "    set_margins_TB()     # Set top and bottom margins to the same value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003035 "    set_margin_left();   # Set left margin"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003036 "    set_margin_right();  # Set right margin"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003037 "    set_margin_top();    # Set top margin"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003038 "    set_margin_bottom(); # Set bottom margin"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003039 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003040 "All of these methods take a distance in inches as a parameter. Note: 1 inch = 25.4mm. C<;-)> The default left and right margin is 0.7 inch. The default top and bottom margin is 0.75 inch. Note, these defaults are different from the defaults used in the binary file format by Spreadsheet::WriteExcel."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003041 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003042 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003043 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003044 "=head2 set_header( $string, $margin )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003045 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003046 "Headers and footers are generated using a C<$string> which is a combination of plain text and control characters. The C<$margin> parameter is optional."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003047 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003048 "The available control character are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003049 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003050 "    Control             Category            Description"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003051 "    =======             ========            ==========="
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003052 "    &L                  Justification       Left"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003053 "    &C                                      Center"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003054 "    &R                                      Right"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003055 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003056 "    &P                  Information         Page number"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003057 "    &N                                      Total number of pages"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003058 "    &D                                      Date"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003059 "    &T                                      Time"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003060 "    &F                                      File name"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003061 "    &A                                      Worksheet name"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003062 "    &Z                                      Workbook path"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003063 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003064 "    &fontsize           Font                Font size"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003065 "    &\"font,style\"                           Font name and style"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003066 "    &U                                      Single underline"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003067 "    &E                                      Double underline"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003068 "    &S                                      Strikethrough"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003069 "    &X                                      Superscript"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003070 "    &Y                                      Subscript"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003071 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003072 "    &[Picture]          Images              Image placeholder"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003073 "    &G                                      Same as &[Picture]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003074 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003075 "    &&                  Miscellaneous       Literal ampersand &"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003076 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003077 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003078 "Text in headers and footers can be justified (aligned) to the left, center and right by prefixing the text with the control characters C<&L>, C<&C> and C<&R>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003079 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003080 "For example (with ASCII art representation of the results):"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003081 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003082 "    $worksheet->set_header('&LHello');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003083 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003084 "     ---------------------------------------------------------------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003085 "    |                                                               |"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003086 "    | Hello                                                         |"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003087 "    |                                                               |"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003088 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003089 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003090 "    $worksheet->set_header('&CHello');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003091 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003092 "     ---------------------------------------------------------------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003093 "    |                                                               |"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003094 "    |                          Hello                                |"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003095 "    |                                                               |"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003096 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003097 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003098 "    $worksheet->set_header('&RHello');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003099 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003100 "     ---------------------------------------------------------------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003101 "    |                                                               |"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003102 "    |                                                         Hello |"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003103 "    |                                                               |"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003104 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003105 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003106 "For simple text, if you do not specify any justification the text will be centred. However, you must prefix the text with C<&C> if you specify a font name or any other formatting:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003107 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003108 "    $worksheet->set_header('Hello');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003109 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003110 "     ---------------------------------------------------------------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003111 "    |                                                               |"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003112 "    |                          Hello                                |"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003113 "    |                                                               |"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003114 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003115 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003116 "You can have text in each of the justification regions:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003117 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003118 "    $worksheet->set_header('&LCiao&CBello&RCielo');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003119 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003120 "     ---------------------------------------------------------------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003121 "    |                                                               |"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003122 "    | Ciao                     Bello                          Cielo |"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003123 "    |                                                               |"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003124 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003125 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003126 "The information control characters act as variables that Excel will update as the workbook or worksheet changes. Times and dates are in the users default format:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003127 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003128 "    $worksheet->set_header('&CPage &P of &N');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003129 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003130 "     ---------------------------------------------------------------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003131 "    |                                                               |"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003132 "    |                        Page 1 of 6                            |"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003133 "    |                                                               |"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003134 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003135 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003136 "    $worksheet->set_header('&CUpdated at &T');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003137 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003138 "     ---------------------------------------------------------------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003139 "    |                                                               |"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003140 "    |                    Updated at 12:30 PM                        |"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003141 "    |                                                               |"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003142 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003143 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003144 "Images can be inserted using the options shown below. Each image must have a placeholder in header string using the C<&[Picture]> or C<&G> control characters:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003145 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003146 "    $worksheet->set_header( '&L&G', 0.3, { image_left => 'logo.jpg' });"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003147 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003148 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003149 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003150 "You can specify the font size of a section of the text by prefixing it with the control character C<&n> where C<n> is the font size:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003151 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003152 "    $worksheet1->set_header( '&C&30Hello Big' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003153 "    $worksheet2->set_header( '&C&10Hello Small' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003154 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003155 "You can specify the font of a section of the text by prefixing it with the control sequence C<&\"font,style\"> where C<fontname> is a font name such as \"Courier New\" or \"Times New Roman\" and C<style> is one of the standard Windows font descriptions: \"Regular\", \"Italic\", \"Bold\" or \"Bold Italic\":"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003156 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003157 "    $worksheet1->set_header( '&C&\"Courier New,Italic\"Hello' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003158 "    $worksheet2->set_header( '&C&\"Courier New,Bold Italic\"Hello' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003159 "    $worksheet3->set_header( '&C&\"Times New Roman,Regular\"Hello' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003160 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003161 "It is possible to combine all of these features together to create sophisticated headers and footers. As an aid to setting up complicated headers and footers you can record a page set-up as a macro in Excel and look at the format strings that VBA produces. Remember however that VBA uses two double quotes C<\"\"> to indicate a single double quote. For the last example above the equivalent VBA code looks like this:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003162 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003163 "    .LeftHeader   = \"\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003164 "    .CenterHeader = \"&\"\"Times New Roman,Regular\"\"Hello\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003165 "    .RightHeader  = \"\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003166 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003167 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003168 "To include a single literal ampersand C<&> in a header or footer you should use a double ampersand C<&&>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003169 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003170 "    $worksheet1->set_header('&CCuriouser && Curiouser - Attorneys at Law');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003171 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003172 "As stated above the margin parameter is optional. As with the other margins the value should be in inches. The default header and footer margin is 0.3 inch. Note, the default margin is different from the default used in the binary file format by Spreadsheet::WriteExcel. The header and footer margin size can be set as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003173 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003174 "    $worksheet->set_header( '&CHello', 0.75 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003175 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003176 "The header and footer margins are independent of the top and bottom margins."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003177 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003178 "The available options are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003179 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003180 "=over"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003181 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003182 "=item * C<image_left> The path to the image. Requires a C<&G> or C<&[Picture]> placeholder."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003183 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003184 "=item * C<image_center> Same as above."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003185 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003186 "=item * C<image_right> Same as above."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003187 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003188 "=item * C<scale_with_doc> Scale header with document. Defaults to true."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003189 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003190 "=item * C<align_with_margins> Align header to margins. Defaults to true."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003191 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003192 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003193 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003194 "The image options must have an accompanying C<&[Picture]> or C<&G> control"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003195 "character in the header string:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003196 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003197 "    $worksheet->set_header("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003198 "        '&L&[Picture]&C&[Picture]&R&[Picture]',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003199 "        undef, # If you don't want to change the margin."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003200 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003201 "            image_left   => 'red.jpg',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003202 "            image_center => 'blue.jpg',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003203 "            image_right  => 'yellow.jpg'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003204 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003205 "      );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003206 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003207 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003208 "Note, the header or footer string must be less than 255 characters. Strings longer than this will not be written and a warning will be generated."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003209 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003210 "The C<set_header()> method can also handle Unicode strings in C<UTF-8> format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003211 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003212 "    $worksheet->set_header( \"&C\\x{263a}\" )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003213 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003214 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003215 "See, also the C<headers.pl> program in the C<examples> directory of the distribution."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003216 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003217 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003218 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003219 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003220 "=head2 set_footer( $string, $margin )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003221 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003222 "The syntax of the C<set_footer()> method is the same as C<set_header()>,  see above."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003223 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003224 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003225 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003226 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003227 "=head2 repeat_rows( $first_row, $last_row )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003228 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003229 "Set the number of rows to repeat at the top of each printed page."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003230 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003231 "For large Excel documents it is often desirable to have the first row or rows of the worksheet print out at the top of each page. This can be achieved by using the C<repeat_rows()> method. The parameters C<$first_row> and C<$last_row> are zero based. The C<$last_row> parameter is optional if you only wish to specify one row:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003232 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003233 "    $worksheet1->repeat_rows( 0 );    # Repeat the first row"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003234 "    $worksheet2->repeat_rows( 0, 1 ); # Repeat the first two rows"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003235 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003236 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003237 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003238 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003239 "=head2 repeat_columns( $first_col, $last_col )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003240 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003241 "Set the columns to repeat at the left hand side of each printed page."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003242 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003243 "For large Excel documents it is often desirable to have the first column or columns of the worksheet print out at the left hand side of each page. This can be achieved by using the C<repeat_columns()> method. The parameters C<$first_column> and C<$last_column> are zero based. The C<$last_column> parameter is optional if you only wish to specify one column. You can also specify the columns using A1 column notation, see the note about L</Cell notation>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003244 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003245 "    $worksheet1->repeat_columns( 0 );        # Repeat the first column"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003246 "    $worksheet2->repeat_columns( 0, 1 );     # Repeat the first two columns"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003247 "    $worksheet3->repeat_columns( 'A:A' );    # Repeat the first column"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003248 "    $worksheet4->repeat_columns( 'A:B' );    # Repeat the first two columns"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003249 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003250 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003251 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003252 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003253 "=head2 hide_gridlines( $option )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003254 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003255 "This method is used to hide the gridlines on the screen and printed page. Gridlines are the lines that divide the cells on a worksheet. Screen and printed gridlines are turned on by default in an Excel worksheet. If you have defined your own cell borders you may wish to hide the default gridlines."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003256 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003257 "    $worksheet->hide_gridlines();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003258 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003259 "The following values of C<$option> are valid:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003260 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003261 "    0 : Don't hide gridlines"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003262 "    1 : Hide printed gridlines only"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003263 "    2 : Hide screen and printed gridlines"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003264 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003265 "If you don't supply an argument or use C<undef> the default option is 1, i.e. only the printed gridlines are hidden."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003266 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003267 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003268 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003269 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003270 "=head2 print_row_col_headers()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003271 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003272 "Set the option to print the row and column headers on the printed page."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003273 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003274 "An Excel worksheet looks something like the following;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003275 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003276 "     ------------------------------------------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003277 "    |   |   A   |   B   |   C   |   D   |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003278 "     ------------------------------------------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003279 "    | 1 |       |       |       |       |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003280 "    | 2 |       |       |       |       |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003281 "    | 3 |       |       |       |       |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003282 "    | 4 |       |       |       |       |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003283 "    |...|  ...  |  ...  |  ...  |  ...  |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003284 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003285 "The headers are the letters and numbers at the top and the left of the worksheet. Since these headers serve mainly as a indication of position on the worksheet they generally do not appear on the printed page. If you wish to have them printed you can use the C<print_row_col_headers()> method:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003286 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003287 "    $worksheet->print_row_col_headers();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003288 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003289 "Do not confuse these headers with page headers as described in the C<set_header()> section above."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003290 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003291 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003292 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003293 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003294 "=head2 hide_row_col_headers()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003295 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003296 "Similar to C<print_row_col_headers()> above but set the option to hide the row and column headers within Excel so that they aren't visible to the user:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003297 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003298 "    $worksheet->hide_row_col_headers();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003299 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003300 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003301 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003302 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003303 "=head2 print_area( $first_row, $first_col, $last_row, $last_col )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003304 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003305 "This method is used to specify the area of the worksheet that will be printed. All four parameters must be specified. You can also use A1 notation, see the note about L</Cell notation>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003306 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003307 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003308 "    $worksheet1->print_area( 'A1:H20' );    # Cells A1 to H20"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003309 "    $worksheet2->print_area( 0, 0, 19, 7 ); # The same"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003310 "    $worksheet2->print_area( 'A:H' );       # Columns A to H if rows have data"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003311 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003312 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003313 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003314 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003315 "=head2 print_across()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003316 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003317 "The C<print_across> method is used to change the default print direction. This is referred to by Excel as the sheet \"page order\"."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003318 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003319 "    $worksheet->print_across();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003320 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003321 "The default page order is shown below for a worksheet that extends over 4 pages. The order is called \"down then across\":"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003322 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003323 "    [1] [3]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003324 "    [2] [4]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003325 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003326 "However, by using the C<print_across> method the print order will be changed to \"across then down\":"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003327 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003328 "    [1] [2]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003329 "    [3] [4]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003330 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003331 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003332 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003333 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003334 "=head2 fit_to_pages( $width, $height )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003335 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003336 "The C<fit_to_pages()> method is used to fit the printed area to a specific number of pages both vertically and horizontally. If the printed area exceeds the specified number of pages it will be scaled down to fit. This guarantees that the printed area will always appear on the specified number of pages even if the page size or margins change."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003337 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003338 "    $worksheet1->fit_to_pages( 1, 1 );    # Fit to 1x1 pages"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003339 "    $worksheet2->fit_to_pages( 2, 1 );    # Fit to 2x1 pages"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003340 "    $worksheet3->fit_to_pages( 1, 2 );    # Fit to 1x2 pages"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003341 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003342 "The print area can be defined using the C<print_area()> method as described above."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003343 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003344 "A common requirement is to fit the printed output to I<n> pages wide but have the height be as long as necessary. To achieve this set the C<$height> to zero:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003345 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003346 "    $worksheet1->fit_to_pages( 1, 0 );    # 1 page wide and as long as necessary"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003347 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003348 "Note that although it is valid to use both C<fit_to_pages()> and C<set_print_scale()> on the same worksheet only one of these options can be active at a time. The last method call made will set the active option."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003349 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003350 "Note that C<fit_to_pages()> will override any manual page breaks that are defined in the worksheet."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003351 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003352 "Note: When using C<fit_to_pages()> it may also be required to set the printer paper size using C<set_paper()> or else Excel will default to \"US Letter\"."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003353 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003354 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003355 "=head2 set_start_page( $start_page )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003356 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003357 "The C<set_start_page()> method is used to set the number of the starting page when the worksheet is printed out. The default value is 1."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003358 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003359 "    $worksheet->set_start_page( 2 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003360 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003361 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003362 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003363 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003364 "=head2 set_print_scale( $scale )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003365 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003366 "Set the scale factor of the printed page. Scale factors in the range C<10 E<lt>= $scale E<lt>= 400> are valid:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003367 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003368 "    $worksheet1->set_print_scale( 50 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003369 "    $worksheet2->set_print_scale( 75 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003370 "    $worksheet3->set_print_scale( 300 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003371 "    $worksheet4->set_print_scale( 400 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003372 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003373 "The default scale factor is 100. Note, C<set_print_scale()> does not affect the scale of the visible page in Excel. For that you should use C<set_zoom()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003374 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003375 "Note also that although it is valid to use both C<fit_to_pages()> and C<set_print_scale()> on the same worksheet only one of these options can be active at a time. The last method call made will set the active option."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003376 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003377 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003378 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003379 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003380 "=head2 print_black_and_white()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003381 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003382 "Set the option to print the worksheet in black and white:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003383 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003384 "    $worksheet->print_black_and_white();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003385 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003386 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003387 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003388 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003389 "=head2 set_h_pagebreaks( @breaks )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003390 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003391 "Add horizontal page breaks to a worksheet. A page break causes all the data that follows it to be printed on the next page. Horizontal page breaks act between rows. To create a page break between rows 20 and 21 you must specify the break at row 21. However in zero index notation this is actually row 20. So you can pretend for a small while that you are using 1 index notation:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003392 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003393 "    $worksheet1->set_h_pagebreaks( 20 );    # Break between row 20 and 21"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003394 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003395 "The C<set_h_pagebreaks()> method will accept a list of page breaks and you can call it more than once:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003396 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003397 "    $worksheet2->set_h_pagebreaks( 20,  40,  60,  80,  100 );    # Add breaks"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003398 "    $worksheet2->set_h_pagebreaks( 120, 140, 160, 180, 200 );    # Add some more"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003399 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003400 "Note: If you specify the \"fit to page\" option via the C<fit_to_pages()> method it will override all manual page breaks."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003401 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003402 "There is a silent limitation of about 1000 horizontal page breaks per worksheet in line with an Excel internal limitation."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003403 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003404 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003405 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003406 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003407 "=head2 set_v_pagebreaks( @breaks )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003408 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003409 "Add vertical page breaks to a worksheet. A page break causes all the data that follows it to be printed on the next page. Vertical page breaks act between columns. To create a page break between columns 20 and 21 you must specify the break at column 21. However in zero index notation this is actually column 20. So you can pretend for a small while that you are using 1 index notation:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003410 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003411 "    $worksheet1->set_v_pagebreaks(20); # Break between column 20 and 21"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003412 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003413 "The C<set_v_pagebreaks()> method will accept a list of page breaks and you can call it more than once:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003414 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003415 "    $worksheet2->set_v_pagebreaks( 20,  40,  60,  80,  100 );    # Add breaks"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003416 "    $worksheet2->set_v_pagebreaks( 120, 140, 160, 180, 200 );    # Add some more"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003417 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003418 "Note: If you specify the \"fit to page\" option via the C<fit_to_pages()> method it will override all manual page breaks."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003419 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003420 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003421 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003422 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003423 "=head1 CELL FORMATTING"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003424 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003425 "This section describes the methods and properties that are available for formatting cells in Excel. The properties of a cell that can be formatted include: fonts, colours, patterns, borders, alignment and number formatting."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003426 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003427 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003428 "=head2 Creating and using a Format object"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003429 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003430 "Cell formatting is defined through a Format object. Format objects are created by calling the workbook C<add_format()> method as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003431 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003432 "    my $format1 = $workbook->add_format();            # Set properties later"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003433 "    my $format2 = $workbook->add_format( %props );    # Set at creation"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003434 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003435 "The format object holds all the formatting properties that can be applied to a cell, a row or a column. The process of setting these properties is discussed in the next section."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003436 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003437 "Once a Format object has been constructed and its properties have been set it can be passed as an argument to the worksheet C<write> methods as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003438 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003439 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 0, 'One', $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003440 "    $worksheet->write_string( 1, 0, 'Two', $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003441 "    $worksheet->write_number( 2, 0, 3, $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003442 "    $worksheet->write_blank( 3, 0, $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003443 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003444 "Formats can also be passed to the worksheet C<set_row()> and C<set_column()> methods to define the default property for a row or column."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003445 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003446 "    $worksheet->set_row( 0, 15, $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003447 "    $worksheet->set_column( 0, 0, 15, $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003448 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003449 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003450 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003451 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003452 "=head2 Format methods and Format properties"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003453 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003454 "The following table shows the Excel format categories, the formatting properties that can be applied and the equivalent object method:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003455 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003456 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003457 "    Category   Description       Property        Method Name"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003458 "    --------   -----------       --------        -----------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003459 "    Font       Font type         font            set_font()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003460 "               Font size         size            set_size()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003461 "               Font color        color           set_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003462 "               Bold              bold            set_bold()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003463 "               Italic            italic          set_italic()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003464 "               Underline         underline       set_underline()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003465 "               Strikeout         font_strikeout  set_font_strikeout()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003466 "               Super/Subscript   font_script     set_font_script()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003467 "               Outline           font_outline    set_font_outline()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003468 "               Shadow            font_shadow     set_font_shadow()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003469 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003470 "    Number     Numeric format    num_format      set_num_format()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003471 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003472 "    Protection Lock cells        locked          set_locked()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003473 "               Hide formulas     hidden          set_hidden()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003474 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003475 "    Alignment  Horizontal align  align           set_align()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003476 "               Vertical align    valign          set_align()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003477 "               Rotation          rotation        set_rotation()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003478 "               Text wrap         text_wrap       set_text_wrap()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003479 "               Justify last      text_justlast   set_text_justlast()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003480 "               Center across     center_across   set_center_across()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003481 "               Indentation       indent          set_indent()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003482 "               Shrink to fit     shrink          set_shrink()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003483 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003484 "    Pattern    Cell pattern      pattern         set_pattern()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003485 "               Background color  bg_color        set_bg_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003486 "               Foreground color  fg_color        set_fg_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003487 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003488 "    Border     Cell border       border          set_border()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003489 "               Bottom border     bottom          set_bottom()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003490 "               Top border        top             set_top()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003491 "               Left border       left            set_left()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003492 "               Right border      right           set_right()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003493 "               Border color      border_color    set_border_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003494 "               Bottom color      bottom_color    set_bottom_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003495 "               Top color         top_color       set_top_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003496 "               Left color        left_color      set_left_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003497 "               Right color       right_color     set_right_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003498 "               Diagonal type     diag_type       set_diag_type()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003499 "               Diagonal border   diag_border     set_diag_border()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003500 "               Diagonal color    diag_color      set_diag_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003501 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003502 "There are two ways of setting Format properties: by using the object method interface or by setting the property directly. For example, a typical use of the method interface would be as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003503 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003504 "    my $format = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003505 "    $format->set_bold();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003506 "    $format->set_color( 'red' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003507 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003508 "By comparison the properties can be set directly by passing a hash of properties to the Format constructor:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003509 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003510 "    my $format = $workbook->add_format( bold => 1, color => 'red' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003511 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003512 "or after the Format has been constructed by means of the C<set_format_properties()> method as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003513 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003514 "    my $format = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003515 "    $format->set_format_properties( bold => 1, color => 'red' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003516 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003517 "You can also store the properties in one or more named hashes and pass them to the required method:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003518 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003519 "    my %font = ("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003520 "        font  => 'Calibri',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003521 "        size  => 12,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003522 "        color => 'blue',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003523 "        bold  => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003524 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003525 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003526 "    my %shading = ("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003527 "        bg_color => 'green',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003528 "        pattern  => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003529 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003530 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003531 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003532 "    my $format1 = $workbook->add_format( %font );            # Font only"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003533 "    my $format2 = $workbook->add_format( %font, %shading );  # Font and shading"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003534 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003535 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003536 "The provision of two ways of setting properties might lead you to wonder which is the best way. The method mechanism may be better if you prefer setting properties via method calls (which the author did when the code was first written) otherwise passing properties to the constructor has proved to be a little more flexible and self documenting in practice. An additional advantage of working with property hashes is that it allows you to share formatting between workbook objects as shown in the example above."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003537 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003538 "The Perl/Tk style of adding properties is also supported:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003539 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003540 "    my %font = ("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003541 "        -font  => 'Calibri',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003542 "        -size  => 12,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003543 "        -color => 'blue',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003544 "        -bold  => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003545 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003546 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003547 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003548 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003549 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003550 "=head2 Working with formats"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003551 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003552 "The default format is Calibri 11 with all other properties off."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003553 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003554 "Each unique format in Excel::Writer::XLSX must have a corresponding Format object. It isn't possible to use a Format with a write() method and then redefine the Format for use at a later stage. This is because a Format is applied to a cell not in its current state but in its final state. Consider the following example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003555 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003556 "    my $format = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003557 "    $format->set_bold();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003558 "    $format->set_color( 'red' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003559 "    $worksheet->write( 'A1', 'Cell A1', $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003560 "    $format->set_color( 'green' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003561 "    $worksheet->write( 'B1', 'Cell B1', $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003562 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003563 "Cell A1 is assigned the Format C<$format> which is initially set to the colour red. However, the colour is subsequently set to green. When Excel displays Cell A1 it will display the final state of the Format which in this case will be the colour green."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003564 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003565 "In general a method call without an argument will turn a property on, for example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003566 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003567 "    my $format1 = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003568 "    $format1->set_bold();       # Turns bold on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003569 "    $format1->set_bold( 1 );    # Also turns bold on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003570 "    $format1->set_bold( 0 );    # Turns bold off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003571 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003572 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003573 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003574 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003575 "=head1 FORMAT METHODS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003576 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003577 "The Format object methods are described in more detail in the following sections. In addition, there is a Perl program called C<formats.pl> in the C<examples> directory of the WriteExcel distribution. This program creates an Excel workbook called C<formats.xlsx> which contains examples of almost all the format types."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003578 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003579 "The following Format methods are available:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003580 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003581 "    set_font()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003582 "    set_size()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003583 "    set_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003584 "    set_bold()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003585 "    set_italic()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003586 "    set_underline()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003587 "    set_font_strikeout()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003588 "    set_font_script()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003589 "    set_font_outline()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003590 "    set_font_shadow()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003591 "    set_num_format()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003592 "    set_locked()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003593 "    set_hidden()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003594 "    set_align()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003595 "    set_rotation()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003596 "    set_text_wrap()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003597 "    set_text_justlast()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003598 "    set_center_across()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003599 "    set_indent()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003600 "    set_shrink()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003601 "    set_pattern()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003602 "    set_bg_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003603 "    set_fg_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003604 "    set_border()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003605 "    set_bottom()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003606 "    set_top()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003607 "    set_left()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003608 "    set_right()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003609 "    set_border_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003610 "    set_bottom_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003611 "    set_top_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003612 "    set_left_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003613 "    set_right_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003614 "    set_diag_type()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003615 "    set_diag_border()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003616 "    set_diag_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003617 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003618 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003619 "The above methods can also be applied directly as properties. For example C<< $format->set_bold() >> is equivalent to C<< $workbook->add_format(bold => 1) >>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003620 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003621 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003622 "=head2 set_format_properties( %properties )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003623 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003624 "The properties of an existing Format object can be also be set by means of C<set_format_properties()>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003625 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003626 "    my $format = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003627 "    $format->set_format_properties( bold => 1, color => 'red' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003628 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003629 "However, this method is here mainly for legacy reasons. It is preferable to set the properties in the format constructor:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003630 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003631 "    my $format = $workbook->add_format( bold => 1, color => 'red' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003632 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003633 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003634 "=head2 set_font( $fontname )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003635 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003636 "    Default state:      Font is Calibri"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003637 "    Default action:     None"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003638 "    Valid args:         Any valid font name"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003639 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003640 "Specify the font used:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003641 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003642 "    $format->set_font('Times New Roman');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003643 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003644 "Excel can only display fonts that are installed on the system that it is running on. Therefore it is best to use the fonts that come as standard such as 'Calibri', 'Times New Roman' and 'Courier New'. See also the Fonts worksheet created by formats.pl"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003645 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003646 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003647 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003648 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003649 "=head2 set_size()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003650 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003651 "    Default state:      Font size is 10"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003652 "    Default action:     Set font size to 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003653 "    Valid args:         Integer values from 1 to as big as your screen."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003654 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003655 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003656 "Set the font size. Excel adjusts the height of a row to accommodate the largest font size in the row. You can also explicitly specify the height of a row using the set_row() worksheet method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003657 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003658 "    my $format = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003659 "    $format->set_size( 30 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003660 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003661 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003662 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003663 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003664 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003665 "=head2 set_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003666 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003667 "    Default state:      Excels default color, usually black"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003668 "    Default action:     Set the default color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003669 "    Valid args:         Html RGB strings like \"#FF0000\" or the following shortcut strings:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003670 "                        'black'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003671 "                        'blue'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003672 "                        'brown'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003673 "                        'cyan'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003674 "                        'gray'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003675 "                        'green'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003676 "                        'lime'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003677 "                        'magenta'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003678 "                        'navy'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003679 "                        'orange'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003680 "                        'pink'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003681 "                        'purple'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003682 "                        'red'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003683 "                        'silver'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003684 "                        'white'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003685 "                        'yellow'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003686 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003687 "Set the font colour. The C<set_color()> method is used as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003688 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003689 "    my $format = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003690 "    $format->set_color( 'red' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003691 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 0, 'wheelbarrow', $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003692 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003693 "Note: The C<set_color()> method is used to set the colour of the font in a cell. To set the colour of a cell use the C<set_bg_color()> and C<set_pattern()> methods."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003694 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003695 "For additional examples see the 'Named colors' and 'Standard colors' worksheets created by formats.pl in the examples directory."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003696 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003697 "See also L</WORKING WITH COLOURS>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003698 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003699 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003700 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003701 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003702 "=head2 set_bold()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003703 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003704 "    Default state:      bold is off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003705 "    Default action:     Turn bold on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003706 "    Valid args:         0, 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003707 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003708 "Set the bold property of the font:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003709 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003710 "    $format->set_bold();  # Turn bold on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003711 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003712 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003713 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003714 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003715 "=head2 set_italic()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003716 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003717 "    Default state:      Italic is off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003718 "    Default action:     Turn italic on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003719 "    Valid args:         0, 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003720 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003721 "Set the italic property of the font:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003722 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003723 "    $format->set_italic();  # Turn italic on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003724 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003725 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003726 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003727 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003728 "=head2 set_underline()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003729 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003730 "    Default state:      Underline is off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003731 "    Default action:     Turn on single underline"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003732 "    Valid args:         0  = No underline"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003733 "                        1  = Single underline"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003734 "                        2  = Double underline"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003735 "                        33 = Single accounting underline"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003736 "                        34 = Double accounting underline"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003737 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003738 "Set the underline property of the font."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003739 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003740 "    $format->set_underline();   # Single underline"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003741 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003742 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003743 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003744 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003745 "=head2 set_font_strikeout()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003746 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003747 "    Default state:      Strikeout is off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003748 "    Default action:     Turn strikeout on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003749 "    Valid args:         0, 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003750 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003751 "Set the strikeout property of the font."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003752 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003753 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003754 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003755 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003756 "=head2 set_font_script()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003757 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003758 "    Default state:      Super/Subscript is off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003759 "    Default action:     Turn Superscript on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003760 "    Valid args:         0  = Normal"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003761 "                        1  = Superscript"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003762 "                        2  = Subscript"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003763 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003764 "Set the superscript/subscript property of the font."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003765 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003766 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003767 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003768 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003769 "=head2 set_font_outline()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003770 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003771 "    Default state:      Outline is off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003772 "    Default action:     Turn outline on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003773 "    Valid args:         0, 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003774 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003775 "Macintosh only."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003776 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003777 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003778 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003779 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003780 "=head2 set_font_shadow()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003781 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003782 "    Default state:      Shadow is off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003783 "    Default action:     Turn shadow on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003784 "    Valid args:         0, 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003785 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003786 "Macintosh only."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003787 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003788 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003789 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003790 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003791 "=head2 set_num_format()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003792 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003793 "    Default state:      General format"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003794 "    Default action:     Format index 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003795 "    Valid args:         See the following table"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003796 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003797 "This method is used to define the numerical format of a number in Excel. It controls whether a number is displayed as an integer, a floating point number, a date, a currency value or some other user defined format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003798 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003799 "The numerical format of a cell can be specified by using a format string or an index to one of Excel's built-in formats:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003800 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003801 "    my $format1 = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003802 "    my $format2 = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003803 "    $format1->set_num_format( 'd mmm yyyy' );    # Format string"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003804 "    $format2->set_num_format( 0x0f );            # Format index"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003805 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003806 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 0, 36892.521, $format1 );    # 1 Jan 2001"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003807 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 0, 36892.521, $format2 );    # 1-Jan-01"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003808 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003809 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003810 "Using format strings you can define very sophisticated formatting of numbers."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003811 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003812 "    $format01->set_num_format( '0.000' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003813 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 0, 3.1415926, $format01 );    # 3.142"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003814 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003815 "    $format02->set_num_format( '#,##0' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003816 "    $worksheet->write( 1, 0, 1234.56, $format02 );      # 1,235"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003817 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003818 "    $format03->set_num_format( '#,##0.00' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003819 "    $worksheet->write( 2, 0, 1234.56, $format03 );      # 1,234.56"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003820 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003821 "    $format04->set_num_format( '$0.00' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003822 "    $worksheet->write( 3, 0, 49.99, $format04 );        # $49.99"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003823 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003824 "    # Note you can use other currency symbols such as the pound or yen as well."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003825 "    # Other currencies may require the use of Unicode."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003826 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003827 "    $format07->set_num_format( 'mm/dd/yy' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003828 "    $worksheet->write( 6, 0, 36892.521, $format07 );    # 01/01/01"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003829 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003830 "    $format08->set_num_format( 'mmm d yyyy' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003831 "    $worksheet->write( 7, 0, 36892.521, $format08 );    # Jan 1 2001"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003832 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003833 "    $format09->set_num_format( 'd mmmm yyyy' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003834 "    $worksheet->write( 8, 0, 36892.521, $format09 );    # 1 January 2001"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003835 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003836 "    $format10->set_num_format( 'dd/mm/yyyy hh:mm AM/PM' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003837 "    $worksheet->write( 9, 0, 36892.521, $format10 );    # 01/01/2001 12:30 PM"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003838 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003839 "    $format11->set_num_format( '0 \"dollar and\" .00 \"cents\"' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003840 "    $worksheet->write( 10, 0, 1.87, $format11 );        # 1 dollar and .87 cents"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003841 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003842 "    # Conditional numerical formatting."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003843 "    $format12->set_num_format( '[Green]General;[Red]-General;General' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003844 "    $worksheet->write( 11, 0, 123, $format12 );         # > 0 Green"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003845 "    $worksheet->write( 12, 0, -45, $format12 );         # < 0 Red"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003846 "    $worksheet->write( 13, 0, 0,   $format12 );         # = 0 Default colour"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003847 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003848 "    # Zip code"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003849 "    $format13->set_num_format( '00000' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003850 "    $worksheet->write( 14, 0, '01209', $format13 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003851 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003852 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003853 "The number system used for dates is described in L</DATES AND TIME IN EXCEL>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003854 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003855 "The colour format should have one of the following values:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003856 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003857 "    [Black] [Blue] [Cyan] [Green] [Magenta] [Red] [White] [Yellow]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003858 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003859 "Alternatively you can specify the colour based on a colour index as follows: C<[Color n]>, where n is a standard Excel colour index - 7. See the 'Standard colors' worksheet created by formats.pl."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003860 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003861 "For more information refer to the documentation on formatting in the C<docs> directory of the Excel::Writer::XLSX distro, the Excel on-line help or L<http://office.microsoft.com/en-gb/assistance/HP051995001033.aspx>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003862 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003863 "You should ensure that the format string is valid in Excel prior to using it in WriteExcel."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003864 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003865 "Excel's built-in formats are shown in the following table:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003866 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003867 "    Index   Index   Format String"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003868 "    0       0x00    General"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003869 "    1       0x01    0"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003870 "    2       0x02    0.00"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003871 "    3       0x03    #,##0"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003872 "    4       0x04    #,##0.00"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003873 "    5       0x05    ($#,##0_);($#,##0)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003874 "    6       0x06    ($#,##0_);[Red]($#,##0)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003875 "    7       0x07    ($#,##0.00_);($#,##0.00)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003876 "    8       0x08    ($#,##0.00_);[Red]($#,##0.00)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003877 "    9       0x09    0%"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003878 "    10      0x0a    0.00%"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003879 "    11      0x0b    0.00E+00"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003880 "    12      0x0c    # ?/?"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003881 "    13      0x0d    # ??/??"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003882 "    14      0x0e    m/d/yy"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003883 "    15      0x0f    d-mmm-yy"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003884 "    16      0x10    d-mmm"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003885 "    17      0x11    mmm-yy"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003886 "    18      0x12    h:mm AM/PM"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003887 "    19      0x13    h:mm:ss AM/PM"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003888 "    20      0x14    h:mm"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003889 "    21      0x15    h:mm:ss"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003890 "    22      0x16    m/d/yy h:mm"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003891 "    ..      ....    ..........."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003892 "    37      0x25    (#,##0_);(#,##0)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003893 "    38      0x26    (#,##0_);[Red](#,##0)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003894 "    39      0x27    (#,##0.00_);(#,##0.00)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003895 "    40      0x28    (#,##0.00_);[Red](#,##0.00)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003896 "    41      0x29    _(* #,##0_);_(* (#,##0);_(* \"-\"_);_(@_)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003897 "    42      0x2a    _($* #,##0_);_($* (#,##0);_($* \"-\"_);_(@_)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003898 "    43      0x2b    _(* #,##0.00_);_(* (#,##0.00);_(* \"-\"??_);_(@_)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003899 "    44      0x2c    _($* #,##0.00_);_($* (#,##0.00);_($* \"-\"??_);_(@_)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003900 "    45      0x2d    mm:ss"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003901 "    46      0x2e    [h]:mm:ss"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003902 "    47      0x2f    mm:ss.0"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003903 "    48      0x30    ##0.0E+0"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003904 "    49      0x31    @"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003905 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003906 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003907 "For examples of these formatting codes see the 'Numerical formats' worksheet created by formats.pl. See also the number_formats1.html and the number_formats2.html documents in the C<docs> directory of the distro."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003908 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003909 "Note 1. Numeric formats 23 to 36 are not documented by Microsoft and may differ in international versions."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003910 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003911 "Note 2. The built-in formats are localised according to the locale settings (regional settings on Windows) of the user when opening the file in Excel:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003912 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003913 "=over"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003914 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003915 "=item * The dot appears as the defined local decimal separator."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003916 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003917 "=item * The comma appears as the defined local digit groups separator."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003918 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003919 "=item * The dollar sign appears as the defined local currency symbol."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003920 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003921 "=item * The date, time and duration formats appear as the local equivalent date or time format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003922 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003923 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003924 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003925 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003926 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003927 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003928 "=head2 set_locked()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003929 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003930 "    Default state:      Cell locking is on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003931 "    Default action:     Turn locking on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003932 "    Valid args:         0, 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003933 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003934 "This property can be used to prevent modification of a cells contents. Following Excel's convention, cell locking is turned on by default. However, it only has an effect if the worksheet has been protected, see the worksheet C<protect()> method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003935 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003936 "    my $locked = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003937 "    $locked->set_locked( 1 );    # A non-op"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003938 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003939 "    my $unlocked = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003940 "    $unlocked->set_locked( 0 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003941 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003942 "    # Enable worksheet protection"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003943 "    $worksheet->protect();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003944 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003945 "    # This cell cannot be edited."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003946 "    $worksheet->write( 'A1', '=1+2', $locked );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003947 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003948 "    # This cell can be edited."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003949 "    $worksheet->write( 'A2', '=1+2', $unlocked );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003950 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003951 "Note: This offers weak protection even with a password, see the note in relation to the C<protect()> method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003952 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003953 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003954 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003955 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003956 "=head2 set_hidden()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003957 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003958 "    Default state:      Formula hiding is off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003959 "    Default action:     Turn hiding on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003960 "    Valid args:         0, 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003961 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003962 "This property is used to hide a formula while still displaying its result. This is generally used to hide complex calculations from end users who are only interested in the result. It only has an effect if the worksheet has been protected, see the worksheet C<protect()> method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003963 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003964 "    my $hidden = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003965 "    $hidden->set_hidden();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003966 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003967 "    # Enable worksheet protection"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003968 "    $worksheet->protect();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003969 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003970 "    # The formula in this cell isn't visible"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003971 "    $worksheet->write( 'A1', '=1+2', $hidden );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003972 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003973 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003974 "Note: This offers weak protection even with a password, see the note in relation to the C<protect()> method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003975 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003976 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003977 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003978 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003979 "=head2 set_align()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003980 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003981 "    Default state:      Alignment is off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003982 "    Default action:     Left alignment"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003983 "    Valid args:         'left'              Horizontal"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003984 "                        'center'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003985 "                        'right'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003986 "                        'fill'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003987 "                        'justify'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003988 "                        'center_across'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003989 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003990 "                        'top'               Vertical"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003991 "                        'vcenter'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003992 "                        'bottom'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003993 "                        'vjustify'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003994 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003995 "This method is used to set the horizontal and vertical text alignment within a cell. Vertical and horizontal alignments can be combined. The method is used as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003996 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003997 "    my $format = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003998 "    $format->set_align( 'center' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003999 "    $format->set_align( 'vcenter' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004000 "    $worksheet->set_row( 0, 30 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004001 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 0, 'X', $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004002 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004003 "Text can be aligned across two or more adjacent cells using the C<center_across> property. However, for genuine merged cells it is better to use the C<merge_range()> worksheet method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004004 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004005 "The C<vjustify> (vertical justify) option can be used to provide automatic text wrapping in a cell. The height of the cell will be adjusted to accommodate the wrapped text. To specify where the text wraps use the C<set_text_wrap()> method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004006 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004007 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004008 "For further examples see the 'Alignment' worksheet created by formats.pl."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004009 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004010 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004011 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004012 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004013 "=head2 set_center_across()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004014 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004015 "    Default state:      Center across selection is off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004016 "    Default action:     Turn center across on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004017 "    Valid args:         1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004018 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004019 "Text can be aligned across two or more adjacent cells using the C<set_center_across()> method. This is an alias for the C<set_align('center_across')> method call."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004020 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004021 "Only one cell should contain the text, the other cells should be blank:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004022 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004023 "    my $format = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004024 "    $format->set_center_across();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004025 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004026 "    $worksheet->write( 1, 1, 'Center across selection', $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004027 "    $worksheet->write_blank( 1, 2, $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004028 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004029 "See also the C<merge1.pl> to C<merge6.pl> programs in the C<examples> directory and the C<merge_range()> method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004030 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004031 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004032 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004033 "=head2 set_text_wrap()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004034 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004035 "    Default state:      Text wrap is off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004036 "    Default action:     Turn text wrap on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004037 "    Valid args:         0, 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004038 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004039 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004040 "Here is an example using the text wrap property, the escape character C<\\n> is used to indicate the end of line:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004041 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004042 "    my $format = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004043 "    $format->set_text_wrap();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004044 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 0, \"It's\\na bum\\nwrap\", $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004045 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004046 "Excel will adjust the height of the row to accommodate the wrapped text. A similar effect can be obtained without newlines using the C<set_align('vjustify')> method. See the C<textwrap.pl> program in the C<examples> directory."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004047 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004048 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004049 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004050 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004051 "=head2 set_rotation()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004052 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004053 "    Default state:      Text rotation is off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004054 "    Default action:     None"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004055 "    Valid args:         Integers in the range -90 to 90 and 270"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004056 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004057 "Set the rotation of the text in a cell. The rotation can be any angle in the range -90 to 90 degrees."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004058 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004059 "    my $format = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004060 "    $format->set_rotation( 30 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004061 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 0, 'This text is rotated', $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004062 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004063 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004064 "The angle 270 is also supported. This indicates text where the letters run from top to bottom."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004065 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004066 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004067 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004068 "=head2 set_indent()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004069 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004070 "    Default state:      Text indentation is off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004071 "    Default action:     Indent text 1 level"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004072 "    Valid args:         Positive integers"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004073 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004074 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004075 "This method can be used to indent text. The argument, which should be an integer, is taken as the level of indentation:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004076 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004077 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004078 "    my $format = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004079 "    $format->set_indent( 2 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004080 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 0, 'This text is indented', $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004081 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004082 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004083 "Indentation is a horizontal alignment property. It will override any other horizontal properties but it can be used in conjunction with vertical properties."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004084 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004085 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004086 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004087 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004088 "=head2 set_shrink()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004089 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004090 "    Default state:      Text shrinking is off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004091 "    Default action:     Turn \"shrink to fit\" on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004092 "    Valid args:         1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004093 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004094 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004095 "This method can be used to shrink text so that it fits in a cell."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004096 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004097 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004098 "    my $format = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004099 "    $format->set_shrink();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004100 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 0, 'Honey, I shrunk the text!', $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004101 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004102 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004103 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004104 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004105 "=head2 set_text_justlast()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004106 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004107 "    Default state:      Justify last is off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004108 "    Default action:     Turn justify last on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004109 "    Valid args:         0, 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004110 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004111 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004112 "Only applies to Far Eastern versions of Excel."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004113 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004114 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004115 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004116 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004117 "=head2 set_pattern()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004118 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004119 "    Default state:      Pattern is off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004120 "    Default action:     Solid fill is on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004121 "    Valid args:         0 .. 18"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004122 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004123 "Set the background pattern of a cell."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004124 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004125 "Examples of the available patterns are shown in the 'Patterns' worksheet created by formats.pl. However, it is unlikely that you will ever need anything other than Pattern 1 which is a solid fill of the background color."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004126 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004127 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004128 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004129 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004130 "=head2 set_bg_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004131 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004132 "    Default state:      Color is off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004133 "    Default action:     Solid fill."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004134 "    Valid args:         See set_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004135 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004136 "The C<set_bg_color()> method can be used to set the background colour of a pattern. Patterns are defined via the C<set_pattern()> method. If a pattern hasn't been defined then a solid fill pattern is used as the default."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004137 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004138 "Here is an example of how to set up a solid fill in a cell:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004139 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004140 "    my $format = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004141 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004142 "    $format->set_pattern();    # This is optional when using a solid fill"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004143 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004144 "    $format->set_bg_color( 'green' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004145 "    $worksheet->write( 'A1', 'Ray', $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004146 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004147 "For further examples see the 'Patterns' worksheet created by formats.pl."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004148 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004149 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004150 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004151 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004152 "=head2 set_fg_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004153 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004154 "    Default state:      Color is off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004155 "    Default action:     Solid fill."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004156 "    Valid args:         See set_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004157 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004158 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004159 "The C<set_fg_color()> method can be used to set the foreground colour of a pattern."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004160 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004161 "For further examples see the 'Patterns' worksheet created by formats.pl."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004162 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004163 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004164 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004165 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004166 "=head2 set_border()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004167 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004168 "    Also applies to:    set_bottom()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004169 "                        set_top()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004170 "                        set_left()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004171 "                        set_right()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004172 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004173 "    Default state:      Border is off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004174 "    Default action:     Set border type 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004175 "    Valid args:         0-13, See below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004176 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004177 "A cell border is comprised of a border on the bottom, top, left and right. These can be set to the same value using C<set_border()> or individually using the relevant method calls shown above."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004178 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004179 "The following shows the border styles sorted by Excel::Writer::XLSX index number:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004180 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004181 "    Index   Name            Weight   Style"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004182 "    =====   =============   ======   ==========="
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004183 "    0       None            0"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004184 "    1       Continuous      1        -----------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004185 "    2       Continuous      2        -----------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004186 "    3       Dash            1        - - - - - -"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004187 "    4       Dot             1        . . . . . ."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004188 "    5       Continuous      3        -----------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004189 "    6       Double          3        ==========="
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004190 "    7       Continuous      0        -----------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004191 "    8       Dash            2        - - - - - -"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004192 "    9       Dash Dot        1        - . - . - ."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004193 "    10      Dash Dot        2        - . - . - ."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004194 "    11      Dash Dot Dot    1        - . . - . ."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004195 "    12      Dash Dot Dot    2        - . . - . ."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004196 "    13      SlantDash Dot   2        / - . / - ."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004197 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004198 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004199 "The following shows the borders sorted by style:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004200 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004201 "    Name            Weight   Style         Index"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004202 "    =============   ======   ===========   ====="
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004203 "    Continuous      0        -----------   7"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004204 "    Continuous      1        -----------   1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004205 "    Continuous      2        -----------   2"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004206 "    Continuous      3        -----------   5"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004207 "    Dash            1        - - - - - -   3"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004208 "    Dash            2        - - - - - -   8"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004209 "    Dash Dot        1        - . - . - .   9"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004210 "    Dash Dot        2        - . - . - .   10"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004211 "    Dash Dot Dot    1        - . . - . .   11"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004212 "    Dash Dot Dot    2        - . . - . .   12"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004213 "    Dot             1        . . . . . .   4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004214 "    Double          3        ===========   6"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004215 "    None            0                      0"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004216 "    SlantDash Dot   2        / - . / - .   13"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004217 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004218 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004219 "The following shows the borders in the order shown in the Excel Dialog."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004220 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004221 "    Index   Style             Index   Style"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004222 "    =====   =====             =====   ====="
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004223 "    0       None              12      - . . - . ."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004224 "    7       -----------       13      / - . / - ."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004225 "    4       . . . . . .       10      - . - . - ."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004226 "    11      - . . - . .       8       - - - - - -"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004227 "    9       - . - . - .       2       -----------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004228 "    3       - - - - - -       5       -----------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004229 "    1       -----------       6       ==========="
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004230 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004231 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004232 "Examples of the available border styles are shown in the 'Borders' worksheet created by formats.pl."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004233 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004234 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004235 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004236 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004237 "=head2 set_border_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004238 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004239 "    Also applies to:    set_bottom_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004240 "                        set_top_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004241 "                        set_left_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004242 "                        set_right_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004243 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004244 "    Default state:      Color is off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004245 "    Default action:     Undefined"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004246 "    Valid args:         See set_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004247 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004248 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004249 "Set the colour of the cell borders. A cell border is comprised of a border on the bottom, top, left and right. These can be set to the same colour using C<set_border_color()> or individually using the relevant method calls shown above. Examples of the border styles and colours are shown in the 'Borders' worksheet created by formats.pl."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004250 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004251 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004252 "=head2 set_diag_type()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004253 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004254 "    Default state:      Diagonal border is off."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004255 "    Default action:     None."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004256 "    Valid args:         1-3, See below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004257 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004258 "Set the diagonal border type for the cell. Three types of diagonal borders are available in Excel:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004259 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004260 "   1: From bottom left to top right."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004261 "   2: From top left to bottom right."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004262 "   3: Same as 1 and 2 combined."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004263 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004264 "For example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004265 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004266 "    $format->set_diag_type( 3 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004267 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004268 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004269 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004270 "=head2 set_diag_border()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004271 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004272 "    Default state:      Border is off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004273 "    Default action:     Set border type 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004274 "    Valid args:         0-13, See below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004275 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004276 "Set the diagonal border style. Same as the parameter to C<set_border()> above."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004277 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004278 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004279 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004280 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004281 "=head2 set_diag_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004282 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004283 "    Default state:      Color is off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004284 "    Default action:     Undefined"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004285 "    Valid args:         See set_color()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004286 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004287 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004288 "Set the colour of the diagonal cell border:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004289 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004290 "    $format->set_diag_type( 3 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004291 "    $format->set_diag_border( 7 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004292 "    $format->set_diag_color( 'red' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004293 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004294 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004295 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004296 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004297 "=head2 set_quote_prefix()"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004298 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004299 "    Default state:      quote prefix is off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004300 "    Default action:     Turn quote prefix on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004301 "    Valid args:         0, 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004302 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004303 "Set the quote prefix property of a format to ensure a string is treated as a string after editing. This is the same as prefixing the string with a single quote in Excel. You don't need to add the quote to the string but you do need to add the format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004304 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004305 "Set the quote prefix property of the format:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004306 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004307 "    $format->set_quote_prefix();  # Turn quote prefix on"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004308 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004309 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004310 "=head2 copy( $format )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004311 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004312 "This method is used to copy all of the properties from one Format object to another:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004313 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004314 "    my $lorry1 = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004315 "    $lorry1->set_bold();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004316 "    $lorry1->set_italic();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004317 "    $lorry1->set_color( 'red' );    # lorry1 is bold, italic and red"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004318 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004319 "    my $lorry2 = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004320 "    $lorry2->copy( $lorry1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004321 "    $lorry2->set_color( 'yellow' );    # lorry2 is bold, italic and yellow"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004322 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004323 "The C<copy()> method is only useful if you are using the method interface to Format properties. It generally isn't required if you are setting Format properties directly using hashes."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004324 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004325 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004326 "Note: this is not a copy constructor, both objects must exist prior to copying."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004327 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004328 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004329 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004330 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004331 "=head1 UNICODE IN EXCEL"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004332 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004333 "The following is a brief introduction to handling Unicode in C<Excel::Writer::XLSX>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004334 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004335 "I<For a more general introduction to Unicode handling in Perl see> L<perlunitut> and L<perluniintro>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004336 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004337 "Excel::Writer::XLSX writer differs from Spreadsheet::WriteExcel in that it only handles Unicode data in C<UTF-8> format and doesn't try to handle legacy UTF-16 Excel formats."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004338 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004339 "If the data is in C<UTF-8> format then Excel::Writer::XLSX will handle it automatically."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004340 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004341 "If you are dealing with non-ASCII characters that aren't in C<UTF-8> then perl provides useful tools in the guise of the C<Encode> module to help you to convert to the required format. For example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004342 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004343 "    use Encode 'decode';"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004344 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004345 "    my $string = 'some string with koi8-r characters';"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004346 "       $string = decode('koi8-r', $string); # koi8-r to utf8"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004347 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004348 "Alternatively you can read data from an encoded file and convert it to C<UTF-8> as you read it in:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004349 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004350 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004351 "    my $file = 'unicode_koi8r.txt';"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004352 "    open FH, '<:encoding(koi8-r)', $file or die \"Couldn't open $file: $!\\n\";"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004353 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004354 "    my $row = 0;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004355 "    while ( <FH> ) {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004356 "        # Data read in is now in utf8 format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004357 "        chomp;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004358 "        $worksheet->write( $row++, 0, $_ );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004359 "    }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004360 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004361 "These methodologies are explained in more detail in L<perlunitut>, L<perluniintro> and L<perlunicode>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004362 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004363 "If the program contains UTF-8 text then you will also need to add C<use utf8> to the includes:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004364 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004365 "    use utf8;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004366 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004367 "    ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004368 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004369 "    $worksheet->write( 'A1', 'Some UTF-8 string' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004370 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004371 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004372 "See also the C<unicode_*.pl> programs in the examples directory of the distro."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004373 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004374 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004375 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004376 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004377 "=head1 WORKING WITH COLOURS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004378 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004379 "Throughout Excel::Writer::XLSX colours can be specified using a Html style C<#RRGGBB> value. For example with a Format object:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004380 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004381 "    $format->set_color( '#FF0000' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004382 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004383 "For backward compatibility a limited number of color names are supported:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004384 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004385 "    $format->set_color( 'red' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004386 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004387 "The color names supported are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004388 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004389 "    black"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004390 "    blue"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004391 "    brown"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004392 "    cyan"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004393 "    gray"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004394 "    green"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004395 "    lime"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004396 "    magenta"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004397 "    navy"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004398 "    orange"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004399 "    pink"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004400 "    purple"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004401 "    red"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004402 "    silver"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004403 "    white"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004404 "    yellow"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004405 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004406 "See also C<colors.pl> in the C<examples> directory."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004407 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004408 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004409 "=head1 DATES AND TIME IN EXCEL"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004410 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004411 "There are two important things to understand about dates and times in Excel:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004412 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004413 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004414 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004415 "=item 1 A date/time in Excel is a real number plus an Excel number format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004416 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004417 "=item 2 Excel::Writer::XLSX doesn't automatically convert date/time strings in C<write()> to an Excel date/time."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004418 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004419 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004420 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004421 "These two points are explained in more detail below along with some suggestions on how to convert times and dates to the required format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004422 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004423 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004424 "=head2 An Excel date/time is a number plus a format"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004425 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004426 "If you write a date string with C<write()> then all you will get is a string:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004427 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004428 "    $worksheet->write( 'A1', '02/03/04' );   # !! Writes a string not a date. !!"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004429 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004430 "Dates and times in Excel are represented by real numbers, for example \"Jan 1 2001 12:30 PM\" is represented by the number 36892.521."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004431 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004432 "The integer part of the number stores the number of days since the epoch and the fractional part stores the percentage of the day."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004433 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004434 "A date or time in Excel is just like any other number. To have the number display as a date you must apply an Excel number format to it. Here are some examples."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004435 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004436 "    #!/usr/bin/perl -w"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004437 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004438 "    use strict;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004439 "    use Excel::Writer::XLSX;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004440 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004441 "    my $workbook  = Excel::Writer::XLSX->new( 'date_examples.xlsx' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004442 "    my $worksheet = $workbook->add_worksheet();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004443 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004444 "    $worksheet->set_column( 'A:A', 30 );    # For extra visibility."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004445 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004446 "    my $number = 39506.5;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004447 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004448 "    $worksheet->write( 'A1', $number );             #   39506.5"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004449 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004450 "    my $format2 = $workbook->add_format( num_format => 'dd/mm/yy' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004451 "    $worksheet->write( 'A2', $number, $format2 );    #  28/02/08"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004452 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004453 "    my $format3 = $workbook->add_format( num_format => 'mm/dd/yy' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004454 "    $worksheet->write( 'A3', $number, $format3 );    #  02/28/08"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004455 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004456 "    my $format4 = $workbook->add_format( num_format => 'd-m-yyyy' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004457 "    $worksheet->write( 'A4', $number, $format4 );    #  28-2-2008"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004458 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004459 "    my $format5 = $workbook->add_format( num_format => 'dd/mm/yy hh:mm' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004460 "    $worksheet->write( 'A5', $number, $format5 );    #  28/02/08 12:00"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004461 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004462 "    my $format6 = $workbook->add_format( num_format => 'd mmm yyyy' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004463 "    $worksheet->write( 'A6', $number, $format6 );    # 28 Feb 2008"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004464 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004465 "    my $format7 = $workbook->add_format( num_format => 'mmm d yyyy hh:mm AM/PM' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004466 "    $worksheet->write('A7', $number , $format7);     #  Feb 28 2008 12:00 PM"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004467 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004468 "    $workbook->close();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004469 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004470 "=head2 Excel::Writer::XLSX doesn't automatically convert date/time strings"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004471 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004472 "Excel::Writer::XLSX doesn't automatically convert input date strings into Excel's formatted date numbers due to the large number of possible date formats and also due to the possibility of misinterpretation."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004473 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004474 "For example, does C<02/03/04> mean March 2 2004, February 3 2004 or even March 4 2002."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004475 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004476 "Therefore, in order to handle dates you will have to convert them to numbers and apply an Excel format. Some methods for converting dates are listed in the next section."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004477 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004478 "The most direct way is to convert your dates to the ISO8601 C<yyyy-mm-ddThh:mm:ss.sss> date format and use the C<write_date_time()> worksheet method:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004479 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004480 "    $worksheet->write_date_time( 'A2', '2001-01-01T12:20', $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004481 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004482 "See the C<write_date_time()> section of the documentation for more details."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004483 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004484 "A general methodology for handling date strings with C<write_date_time()> is:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004485 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004486 "    1. Identify incoming date/time strings with a regex."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004487 "    2. Extract the component parts of the date/time using the same regex."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004488 "    3. Convert the date/time to the ISO8601 format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004489 "    4. Write the date/time using write_date_time() and a number format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004490 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004491 "Here is an example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004492 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004493 "    #!/usr/bin/perl -w"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004494 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004495 "    use strict;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004496 "    use Excel::Writer::XLSX;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004497 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004498 "    my $workbook  = Excel::Writer::XLSX->new( 'example.xlsx' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004499 "    my $worksheet = $workbook->add_worksheet();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004500 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004501 "    # Set the default format for dates."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004502 "    my $date_format = $workbook->add_format( num_format => 'mmm d yyyy' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004503 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004504 "    # Increase column width to improve visibility of data."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004505 "    $worksheet->set_column( 'A:C', 20 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004506 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004507 "    # Simulate reading from a data source."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004508 "    my $row = 0;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004509 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004510 "    while ( <DATA> ) {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004511 "        chomp;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004512 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004513 "        my $col  = 0;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004514 "        my @data = split ' ';"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004515 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004516 "        for my $item ( @data ) {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004517 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004518 "            # Match dates in the following formats: d/m/yy, d/m/yyyy"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004519 "            if ( $item =~ qr[^(\\d{1,2})/(\\d{1,2})/(\\d{4})$] ) {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004520 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004521 "                # Change to the date format required by write_date_time()."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004522 "                my $date = sprintf \"%4d-%02d-%02dT\", $3, $2, $1;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004523 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004524 "                $worksheet->write_date_time( $row, $col++, $date,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004525 "                    $date_format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004526 "            }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004527 "            else {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004528 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004529 "                # Just plain data"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004530 "                $worksheet->write( $row, $col++, $item );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004531 "            }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004532 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004533 "        $row++;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004534 "    }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004535 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004536 "    $workbook->close();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004537 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004538 "    __DATA__"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004539 "    Item    Cost    Date"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004540 "    Book    10      1/9/2007"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004541 "    Beer    4       12/9/2007"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004542 "    Bed     500     5/10/2007"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004543 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004544 "For a slightly more advanced solution you can modify the C<write()> method to handle date formats of your choice via the C<add_write_handler()> method. See the C<add_write_handler()> section of the docs and the write_handler3.pl and write_handler4.pl programs in the examples directory of the distro."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004545 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004546 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004547 "=head2 Converting dates and times to an Excel date or time"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004548 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004549 "The C<write_date_time()> method above is just one way of handling dates and times."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004550 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004551 "You can also use the C<convert_date_time()> worksheet method to convert from an ISO8601 style date string to an Excel date and time number."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004552 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004553 "The L<Excel::Writer::XLSX::Utility> module which is included in the distro has date/time handling functions:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004554 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004555 "    use Excel::Writer::XLSX::Utility;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004556 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004557 "    $date           = xl_date_list(2002, 1, 1);         # 37257"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004558 "    $date           = xl_parse_date(\"11 July 1997\");    # 35622"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004559 "    $time           = xl_parse_time('3:21:36 PM');      # 0.64"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004560 "    $date           = xl_decode_date_EU(\"13 May 2002\"); # 37389"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004561 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004562 "Note: some of these functions require additional CPAN modules."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004563 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004564 "For date conversions using the CPAN C<DateTime> framework see L<DateTime::Format::Excel> L<http://search.cpan.org/search?dist=DateTime-Format-Excel>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004565 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004566 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004567 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004568 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004569 "=head1 OUTLINES AND GROUPING IN EXCEL"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004570 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004571 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004572 "Excel allows you to group rows or columns so that they can be hidden or displayed with a single mouse click. This feature is referred to as outlines."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004573 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004574 "Outlines can reduce complex data down to a few salient sub-totals or summaries."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004575 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004576 "This feature is best viewed in Excel but the following is an ASCII representation of what a worksheet with three outlines might look like. Rows 3-4 and rows 7-8 are grouped at level 2. Rows 2-9 are grouped at level 1. The lines at the left hand side are called outline level bars."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004577 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004578 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004579 "            ------------------------------------------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004580 "     1 2 3 |   |   A   |   B   |   C   |   D   |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004581 "            ------------------------------------------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004582 "      _    | 1 |   A   |       |       |       |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004583 "     |  _  | 2 |   B   |       |       |       |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004584 "     | |   | 3 |  (C)  |       |       |       |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004585 "     | |   | 4 |  (D)  |       |       |       |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004586 "     | -   | 5 |   E   |       |       |       |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004587 "     |  _  | 6 |   F   |       |       |       |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004588 "     | |   | 7 |  (G)  |       |       |       |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004589 "     | |   | 8 |  (H)  |       |       |       |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004590 "     | -   | 9 |   I   |       |       |       |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004591 "     -     | . |  ...  |  ...  |  ...  |  ...  |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004592 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004593 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004594 "Clicking the minus sign on each of the level 2 outlines will collapse and hide the data as shown in the next figure. The minus sign changes to a plus sign to indicate that the data in the outline is hidden."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004595 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004596 "            ------------------------------------------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004597 "     1 2 3 |   |   A   |   B   |   C   |   D   |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004598 "            ------------------------------------------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004599 "      _    | 1 |   A   |       |       |       |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004600 "     |     | 2 |   B   |       |       |       |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004601 "     | +   | 5 |   E   |       |       |       |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004602 "     |     | 6 |   F   |       |       |       |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004603 "     | +   | 9 |   I   |       |       |       |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004604 "     -     | . |  ...  |  ...  |  ...  |  ...  |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004605 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004606 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004607 "Clicking on the minus sign on the level 1 outline will collapse the remaining rows as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004608 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004609 "            ------------------------------------------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004610 "     1 2 3 |   |   A   |   B   |   C   |   D   |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004611 "            ------------------------------------------"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004612 "           | 1 |   A   |       |       |       |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004613 "     +     | . |  ...  |  ...  |  ...  |  ...  |  ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004614 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004615 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004616 "Grouping in C<Excel::Writer::XLSX> is achieved by setting the outline level via the C<set_row()> and C<set_column()> worksheet methods:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004617 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004618 "    set_row( $row, $height, $format, $hidden, $level, $collapsed )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004619 "    set_column( $first_col, $last_col, $width, $format, $hidden, $level, $collapsed )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004620 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004621 "The following example sets an outline level of 1 for rows 2 and 3 (zero-indexed) and columns B to G. The parameters C<$height> and C<$XF> are assigned default values since they are undefined:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004622 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004623 "    $worksheet->set_row( 1, undef, undef, 0, 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004624 "    $worksheet->set_row( 2, undef, undef, 0, 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004625 "    $worksheet->set_column( 'B:G', undef, undef, 0, 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004626 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004627 "Excel allows up to 7 outline levels. Therefore the C<$level> parameter should be in the range C<0 E<lt>= $level E<lt>= 7>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004628 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004629 "Rows and columns can be collapsed by setting the C<$hidden> flag for the hidden rows/columns and setting the C<$collapsed> flag for the row/column that has the collapsed C<+> symbol:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004630 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004631 "    $worksheet->set_row( 1, undef, undef, 1, 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004632 "    $worksheet->set_row( 2, undef, undef, 1, 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004633 "    $worksheet->set_row( 3, undef, undef, 0, 0, 1 );          # Collapsed flag."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004634 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004635 "    $worksheet->set_column( 'B:G', undef, undef, 1, 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004636 "    $worksheet->set_column( 'H:H', undef, undef, 0, 0, 1 );   # Collapsed flag."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004637 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004638 "Note: Setting the C<$collapsed> flag is particularly important for compatibility with OpenOffice.org and Gnumeric."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004639 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004640 "For a more complete example see the C<outline.pl> and C<outline_collapsed.pl> programs in the examples directory of the distro."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004641 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004642 "Some additional outline properties can be set via the C<outline_settings()> worksheet method, see above."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004643 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004644 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004645 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004646 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004647 "=head1 DATA VALIDATION IN EXCEL"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004648 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004649 "Data validation is a feature of Excel which allows you to restrict the data that a users enters in a cell and to display help and warning messages. It also allows you to restrict input to values in a drop down list."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004650 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004651 "A typical use case might be to restrict data in a cell to integer values in a certain range, to provide a help message to indicate the required value and to issue a warning if the input data doesn't meet the stated criteria. In Excel::Writer::XLSX we could do that as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004652 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004653 "    $worksheet->data_validation('B3',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004654 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004655 "            validate        => 'integer',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004656 "            criteria        => 'between',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004657 "            minimum         => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004658 "            maximum         => 100,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004659 "            input_title     => 'Input an integer:',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004660 "            input_message   => 'Between 1 and 100',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004661 "            error_message   => 'Sorry, try again.',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004662 "        });"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004663 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004664 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004665 "=begin html"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004666 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004667 "<p><center><img src=\"http://jmcnamara.github.io/excel-writer-xlsx/images/examples/validation_example.jpg\" alt=\"The output from the above example\"/></center></p>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004668 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004669 "=end html"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004670 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004671 "For more information on data validation see the following Microsoft support article \"Description and examples of data validation in Excel\": L<http://support.microsoft.com/kb/211485>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004672 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004673 "The following sections describe how to use the C<data_validation()> method and its various options."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004674 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004675 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004676 "=head2 data_validation( $row, $col, { parameter => 'value', ... } )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004677 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004678 "The C<data_validation()> method is used to construct an Excel data validation."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004679 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004680 "It can be applied to a single cell or a range of cells. You can pass 3 parameters such as C<($row, $col, {...})> or 5 parameters such as C<($first_row, $first_col, $last_row, $last_col, {...})>. You can also use C<A1> style notation. For example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004681 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004682 "    $worksheet->data_validation( 0, 0,       {...} );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004683 "    $worksheet->data_validation( 0, 0, 4, 1, {...} );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004684 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004685 "    # Which are the same as:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004686 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004687 "    $worksheet->data_validation( 'A1',       {...} );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004688 "    $worksheet->data_validation( 'A1:B5',    {...} );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004689 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004690 "See also the note about L</Cell notation> for more information."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004691 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004692 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004693 "The last parameter in C<data_validation()> must be a hash ref containing the parameters that describe the type and style of the data validation. The allowable parameters are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004694 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004695 "    validate"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004696 "    criteria"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004697 "    value | minimum | source"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004698 "    maximum"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004699 "    ignore_blank"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004700 "    dropdown"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004701 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004702 "    input_title"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004703 "    input_message"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004704 "    show_input"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004705 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004706 "    error_title"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004707 "    error_message"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004708 "    error_type"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004709 "    show_error"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004710 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004711 "These parameters are explained in the following sections. Most of the parameters are optional, however, you will generally require the three main options C<validate>, C<criteria> and C<value>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004712 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004713 "    $worksheet->data_validation('B3',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004714 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004715 "            validate => 'integer',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004716 "            criteria => '>',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004717 "            value    => 100,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004718 "        });"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004719 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004720 "The C<data_validation> method returns:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004721 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004722 "     0 for success."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004723 "    -1 for insufficient number of arguments."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004724 "    -2 for row or column out of bounds."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004725 "    -3 for incorrect parameter or value."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004726 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004727 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004728 "=head2 validate"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004729 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004730 "This parameter is passed in a hash ref to C<data_validation()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004731 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004732 "The C<validate> parameter is used to set the type of data that you wish to validate. It is always required and it has no default value. Allowable values are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004733 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004734 "    any"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004735 "    integer"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004736 "    decimal"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004737 "    list"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004738 "    date"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004739 "    time"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004740 "    length"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004741 "    custom"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004742 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004743 "=over"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004744 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004745 "=item * B<any> is used to specify that the type of data is unrestricted. This is useful to display an input message without restricting the data that can be entered."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004746 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004747 "=item * B<integer> restricts the cell to integer values. Excel refers to this as 'whole number'."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004748 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004749 "    validate => 'integer',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004750 "    criteria => '>',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004751 "    value    => 100,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004752 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004753 "=item * B<decimal> restricts the cell to decimal values."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004754 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004755 "    validate => 'decimal',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004756 "    criteria => '>',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004757 "    value    => 38.6,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004758 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004759 "=item * B<list> restricts the cell to a set of user specified values. These can be passed in an array ref or as a cell range (named ranges aren't currently supported):"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004760 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004761 "    validate => 'list',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004762 "    value    => ['open', 'high', 'close'],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004763 "    # Or like this:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004764 "    value    => 'B1:B3',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004765 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004766 "Excel requires that range references are only to cells on the same worksheet."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004767 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004768 "=item * B<date> restricts the cell to date values. Dates in Excel are expressed as integer values but you can also pass an ISO8601 style string as used in C<write_date_time()>. See also L</DATES AND TIME IN EXCEL> for more information about working with Excel's dates."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004769 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004770 "    validate => 'date',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004771 "    criteria => '>',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004772 "    value    => 39653, # 24 July 2008"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004773 "    # Or like this:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004774 "    value    => '2008-07-24T',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004775 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004776 "=item * B<time> restricts the cell to time values. Times in Excel are expressed as decimal values but you can also pass an ISO8601 style string as used in C<write_date_time()>. See also L</DATES AND TIME IN EXCEL> for more information about working with Excel's times."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004777 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004778 "    validate => 'time',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004779 "    criteria => '>',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004780 "    value    => 0.5, # Noon"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004781 "    # Or like this:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004782 "    value    => 'T12:00:00',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004783 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004784 "=item * B<length> restricts the cell data based on an integer string length. Excel refers to this as 'Text length'."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004785 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004786 "    validate => 'length',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004787 "    criteria => '>',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004788 "    value    => 10,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004789 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004790 "=item * B<custom> restricts the cell based on an external Excel formula that returns a C<TRUE/FALSE> value."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004791 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004792 "    validate => 'custom',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004793 "    value    => '=IF(A10>B10,TRUE,FALSE)',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004794 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004795 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004796 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004797 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004798 "=head2 criteria"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004799 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004800 "This parameter is passed in a hash ref to C<data_validation()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004801 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004802 "The C<criteria> parameter is used to set the criteria by which the data in the cell is validated. It is almost always required except for the C<list> and C<custom> validate options. It has no default value. Allowable values are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004803 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004804 "    'between'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004805 "    'not between'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004806 "    'equal to'                  |  '=='  |  '='"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004807 "    'not equal to'              |  '!='  |  '<>'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004808 "    'greater than'              |  '>'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004809 "    'less than'                 |  '<'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004810 "    'greater than or equal to'  |  '>='"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004811 "    'less than or equal to'     |  '<='"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004812 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004813 "You can either use Excel's textual description strings, in the first column above, or the more common symbolic alternatives. The following are equivalent:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004814 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004815 "    validate => 'integer',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004816 "    criteria => 'greater than',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004817 "    value    => 100,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004818 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004819 "    validate => 'integer',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004820 "    criteria => '>',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004821 "    value    => 100,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004822 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004823 "The C<list> and C<custom> validate options don't require a C<criteria>. If you specify one it will be ignored."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004824 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004825 "    validate => 'list',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004826 "    value    => ['open', 'high', 'close'],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004827 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004828 "    validate => 'custom',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004829 "    value    => '=IF(A10>B10,TRUE,FALSE)',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004830 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004831 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004832 "=head2 value | minimum | source"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004833 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004834 "This parameter is passed in a hash ref to C<data_validation()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004835 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004836 "The C<value> parameter is used to set the limiting value to which the C<criteria> is applied. It is always required and it has no default value. You can also use the synonyms C<minimum> or C<source> to make the validation a little clearer and closer to Excel's description of the parameter:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004837 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004838 "    # Use 'value'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004839 "    validate => 'integer',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004840 "    criteria => '>',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004841 "    value    => 100,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004842 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004843 "    # Use 'minimum'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004844 "    validate => 'integer',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004845 "    criteria => 'between',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004846 "    minimum  => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004847 "    maximum  => 100,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004848 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004849 "    # Use 'source'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004850 "    validate => 'list',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004851 "    source   => '$B$1:$B$3',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004852 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004853 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004854 "=head2 maximum"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004855 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004856 "This parameter is passed in a hash ref to C<data_validation()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004857 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004858 "The C<maximum> parameter is used to set the upper limiting value when the C<criteria> is either C<'between'> or C<'not between'>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004859 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004860 "    validate => 'integer',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004861 "    criteria => 'between',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004862 "    minimum  => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004863 "    maximum  => 100,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004864 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004865 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004866 "=head2 ignore_blank"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004867 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004868 "This parameter is passed in a hash ref to C<data_validation()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004869 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004870 "The C<ignore_blank> parameter is used to toggle on and off the 'Ignore blank' option in the Excel data validation dialog. When the option is on the data validation is not applied to blank data in the cell. It is on by default."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004871 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004872 "    ignore_blank => 0,  # Turn the option off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004873 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004874 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004875 "=head2 dropdown"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004876 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004877 "This parameter is passed in a hash ref to C<data_validation()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004878 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004879 "The C<dropdown> parameter is used to toggle on and off the 'In-cell dropdown' option in the Excel data validation dialog. When the option is on a dropdown list will be shown for C<list> validations. It is on by default."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004880 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004881 "    dropdown => 0,      # Turn the option off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004882 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004883 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004884 "=head2 input_title"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004885 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004886 "This parameter is passed in a hash ref to C<data_validation()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004887 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004888 "The C<input_title> parameter is used to set the title of the input message that is displayed when a cell is entered. It has no default value and is only displayed if the input message is displayed. See the C<input_message> parameter below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004889 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004890 "    input_title   => 'This is the input title',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004891 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004892 "The maximum title length is 32 characters."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004893 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004894 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004895 "=head2 input_message"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004896 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004897 "This parameter is passed in a hash ref to C<data_validation()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004898 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004899 "The C<input_message> parameter is used to set the input message that is displayed when a cell is entered. It has no default value."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004900 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004901 "    validate      => 'integer',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004902 "    criteria      => 'between',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004903 "    minimum       => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004904 "    maximum       => 100,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004905 "    input_title   => 'Enter the applied discount:',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004906 "    input_message => 'between 1 and 100',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004907 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004908 "The message can be split over several lines using newlines, C<\"\\n\"> in double quoted strings."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004909 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004910 "    input_message => \"This is\\na test.\","
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004911 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004912 "The maximum message length is 255 characters."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004913 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004914 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004915 "=head2 show_input"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004916 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004917 "This parameter is passed in a hash ref to C<data_validation()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004918 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004919 "The C<show_input> parameter is used to toggle on and off the 'Show input message when cell is selected' option in the Excel data validation dialog. When the option is off an input message is not displayed even if it has been set using C<input_message>. It is on by default."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004920 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004921 "    show_input => 0,      # Turn the option off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004922 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004923 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004924 "=head2 error_title"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004925 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004926 "This parameter is passed in a hash ref to C<data_validation()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004927 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004928 "The C<error_title> parameter is used to set the title of the error message that is displayed when the data validation criteria is not met. The default error title is 'Microsoft Excel'."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004929 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004930 "    error_title   => 'Input value is not valid',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004931 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004932 "The maximum title length is 32 characters."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004933 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004934 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004935 "=head2 error_message"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004936 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004937 "This parameter is passed in a hash ref to C<data_validation()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004938 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004939 "The C<error_message> parameter is used to set the error message that is displayed when a cell is entered. The default error message is \"The value you entered is not valid.\\nA user has restricted values that can be entered into the cell.\"."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004940 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004941 "    validate      => 'integer',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004942 "    criteria      => 'between',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004943 "    minimum       => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004944 "    maximum       => 100,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004945 "    error_title   => 'Input value is not valid',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004946 "    error_message => 'It should be an integer between 1 and 100',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004947 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004948 "The message can be split over several lines using newlines, C<\"\\n\"> in double quoted strings."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004949 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004950 "    input_message => \"This is\\na test.\","
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004951 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004952 "The maximum message length is 255 characters."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004953 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004954 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004955 "=head2 error_type"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004956 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004957 "This parameter is passed in a hash ref to C<data_validation()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004958 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004959 "The C<error_type> parameter is used to specify the type of error dialog that is displayed. There are 3 options:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004960 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004961 "    'stop'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004962 "    'warning'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004963 "    'information'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004964 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004965 "The default is C<'stop'>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004966 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004967 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004968 "=head2 show_error"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004969 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004970 "This parameter is passed in a hash ref to C<data_validation()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004971 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004972 "The C<show_error> parameter is used to toggle on and off the 'Show error alert after invalid data is entered' option in the Excel data validation dialog. When the option is off an error message is not displayed even if it has been set using C<error_message>. It is on by default."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004973 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004974 "    show_error => 0,      # Turn the option off"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004975 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004976 "=head2 Data Validation Examples"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004977 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004978 "Example 1. Limiting input to an integer greater than a fixed value."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004979 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004980 "    $worksheet->data_validation('A1',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004981 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004982 "            validate        => 'integer',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004983 "            criteria        => '>',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004984 "            value           => 0,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004985 "        });"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004986 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004987 "Example 2. Limiting input to an integer greater than a fixed value where the value is referenced from a cell."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004988 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004989 "    $worksheet->data_validation('A2',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004990 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004991 "            validate        => 'integer',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004992 "            criteria        => '>',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004993 "            value           => '=E3',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004994 "        });"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004995 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004996 "Example 3. Limiting input to a decimal in a fixed range."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004997 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004998 "    $worksheet->data_validation('A3',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00004999 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005000 "            validate        => 'decimal',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005001 "            criteria        => 'between',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005002 "            minimum         => 0.1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005003 "            maximum         => 0.5,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005004 "        });"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005005 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005006 "Example 4. Limiting input to a value in a dropdown list."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005007 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005008 "    $worksheet->data_validation('A4',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005009 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005010 "            validate        => 'list',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005011 "            source          => ['open', 'high', 'close'],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005012 "        });"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005013 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005014 "Example 5. Limiting input to a value in a dropdown list where the list is specified as a cell range."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005015 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005016 "    $worksheet->data_validation('A5',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005017 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005018 "            validate        => 'list',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005019 "            source          => '=$E$4:$G$4',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005020 "        });"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005021 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005022 "Example 6. Limiting input to a date in a fixed range."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005023 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005024 "    $worksheet->data_validation('A6',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005025 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005026 "            validate        => 'date',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005027 "            criteria        => 'between',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005028 "            minimum         => '2008-01-01T',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005029 "            maximum         => '2008-12-12T',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005030 "        });"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005031 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005032 "Example 7. Displaying a message when the cell is selected."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005033 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005034 "    $worksheet->data_validation('A7',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005035 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005036 "            validate      => 'integer',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005037 "            criteria      => 'between',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005038 "            minimum       => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005039 "            maximum       => 100,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005040 "            input_title   => 'Enter an integer:',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005041 "            input_message => 'between 1 and 100',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005042 "        });"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005043 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005044 "See also the C<data_validate.pl> program in the examples directory of the distro."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005045 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005046 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005047 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005048 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005049 "=head1 CONDITIONAL FORMATTING IN EXCEL"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005050 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005051 "Conditional formatting is a feature of Excel which allows you to apply a format to a cell or a range of cells based on a certain criteria."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005052 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005053 "For example the following criteria is used to highlight cells >= 50 in red in the C<conditional_format.pl> example from the distro:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005054 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005055 "    # Write a conditional format over a range."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005056 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'B3:K12',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005057 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005058 "            type     => 'cell',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005059 "            criteria => '>=',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005060 "            value    => 50,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005061 "            format   => $format1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005062 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005063 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005064 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005065 "=begin html"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005066 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005067 "<p><center><img src=\"http://jmcnamara.github.io/excel-writer-xlsx/images/examples/conditional_example.jpg\" alt=\"The output from the above example\"/></center></p>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005068 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005069 "=end html"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005070 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005071 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005072 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005073 "=head2 conditional_formatting( $row, $col, { parameter => 'value', ... } )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005074 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005075 "The C<conditional_formatting()> method is used to apply formatting  based on user defined criteria to an Excel::Writer::XLSX file."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005076 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005077 "It can be applied to a single cell or a range of cells. You can pass 3 parameters such as C<($row, $col, {...})> or 5 parameters such as C<($first_row, $first_col, $last_row, $last_col, {...})>. You can also use C<A1> style notation. For example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005078 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005079 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 0, 0,       {...} );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005080 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 0, 0, 4, 1, {...} );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005081 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005082 "    # Which are the same as:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005083 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005084 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1',       {...} );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005085 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:B5',    {...} );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005086 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005087 "See also the note about L</Cell notation> for more information."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005088 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005089 "Using C<A1> style notation is also possible to specify non-contiguous ranges, separated by a comma. For example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005090 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005091 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:D5,A8:D12', {...} );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005092 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005093 "The last parameter in C<conditional_formatting()> must be a hash ref containing the parameters that describe the type and style of the data validation. The main parameters are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005094 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005095 "    type"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005096 "    format"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005097 "    criteria"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005098 "    value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005099 "    minimum"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005100 "    maximum"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005101 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005102 "Other, less commonly used parameters are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005103 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005104 "    min_type"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005105 "    mid_type"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005106 "    max_type"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005107 "    min_value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005108 "    mid_value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005109 "    max_value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005110 "    min_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005111 "    mid_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005112 "    max_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005113 "    bar_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005114 "    bar_only"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005115 "    bar_solid"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005116 "    bar_negative_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005117 "    bar_border_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005118 "    bar_negative_border_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005119 "    bar_negative_color_same"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005120 "    bar_negative_border_color_same"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005121 "    bar_no_border"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005122 "    bar_direction"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005123 "    bar_axis_position"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005124 "    bar_axis_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005125 "    data_bar_2010"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005126 "    icon_style"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005127 "    icons"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005128 "    reverse_icons"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005129 "    icons_only"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005130 "    stop_if_true"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005131 "    multi_range"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005132 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005133 "Additional parameters which are used for specific conditional format types are shown in the relevant sections below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005134 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005135 "=head2 type"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005136 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005137 "This parameter is passed in a hash ref to C<conditional_formatting()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005138 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005139 "The C<type> parameter is used to set the type of conditional formatting that you wish to apply. It is always required and it has no default value. Allowable C<type> values and their associated parameters are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005140 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005141 "    Type            Parameters"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005142 "    ====            =========="
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005143 "    cell            criteria"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005144 "                    value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005145 "                    minimum"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005146 "                    maximum"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005147 "                    format"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005148 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005149 "    date            criteria"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005150 "                    value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005151 "                    minimum"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005152 "                    maximum"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005153 "                    format"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005154 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005155 "    time_period     criteria"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005156 "                    format"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005157 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005158 "    text            criteria"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005159 "                    value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005160 "                    format"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005161 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005162 "    average         criteria"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005163 "                    format"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005164 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005165 "    duplicate       format"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005166 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005167 "    unique          format"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005168 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005169 "    top             criteria"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005170 "                    value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005171 "                    format"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005172 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005173 "    bottom          criteria"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005174 "                    value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005175 "                    format"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005176 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005177 "    blanks          format"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005178 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005179 "    no_blanks       format"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005180 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005181 "    errors          format"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005182 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005183 "    no_errors       format"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005184 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005185 "    formula         criteria"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005186 "                    format"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005187 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005188 "    2_color_scale   min_type"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005189 "                    max_type"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005190 "                    min_value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005191 "                    max_value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005192 "                    min_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005193 "                    max_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005194 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005195 "    3_color_scale   min_type"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005196 "                    mid_type"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005197 "                    max_type"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005198 "                    min_value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005199 "                    mid_value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005200 "                    max_value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005201 "                    min_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005202 "                    mid_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005203 "                    max_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005204 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005205 "    data_bar        min_type"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005206 "                    max_type"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005207 "                    min_value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005208 "                    max_value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005209 "                    bar_only"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005210 "                    bar_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005211 "                    bar_solid*"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005212 "                    bar_negative_color*"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005213 "                    bar_border_color*"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005214 "                    bar_negative_border_color*"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005215 "                    bar_negative_color_same*"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005216 "                    bar_negative_border_color_same*"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005217 "                    bar_no_border*"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005218 "                    bar_direction*"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005219 "                    bar_axis_position*"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005220 "                    bar_axis_color*"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005221 "                    data_bar_2010*"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005222 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005223 "    icon_set        icon_style"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005224 "                    reverse_icons"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005225 "                    icons"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005226 "                    icons_only"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005227 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005228 "Data bar parameters marked with (*) are only available in Excel 2010 and later. Files that use these properties can still be opened in Excel 2007 but the data bars will be displayed without them."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005229 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005230 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005231 "=head2 type => 'cell'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005232 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005233 "This is the most common conditional formatting type. It is used when a format is applied to a cell based on a simple criterion. For example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005234 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005235 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005236 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005237 "            type     => 'cell',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005238 "            criteria => 'greater than',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005239 "            value    => 5,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005240 "            format   => $red_format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005241 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005242 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005243 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005244 "Or, using the C<between> criteria:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005245 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005246 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'C1:C4',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005247 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005248 "            type     => 'cell',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005249 "            criteria => 'between',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005250 "            minimum  => 20,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005251 "            maximum  => 30,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005252 "            format   => $green_format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005253 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005254 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005255 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005256 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005257 "=head2 criteria"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005258 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005259 "The C<criteria> parameter is used to set the criteria by which the cell data will be evaluated. It has no default value. The most common criteria as applied to C<< { type => 'cell' } >> are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005260 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005261 "    'between'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005262 "    'not between'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005263 "    'equal to'                  |  '=='  |  '='"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005264 "    'not equal to'              |  '!='  |  '<>'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005265 "    'greater than'              |  '>'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005266 "    'less than'                 |  '<'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005267 "    'greater than or equal to'  |  '>='"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005268 "    'less than or equal to'     |  '<='"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005269 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005270 "You can either use Excel's textual description strings, in the first column above, or the more common symbolic alternatives."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005271 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005272 "Additional criteria which are specific to other conditional format types are shown in the relevant sections below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005273 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005274 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005275 "=head2 value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005276 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005277 "The C<value> is generally used along with the C<criteria> parameter to set the rule by which the cell data  will be evaluated."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005278 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005279 "    type     => 'cell',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005280 "    criteria => '>',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005281 "    value    => 5"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005282 "    format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005283 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005284 "The C<value> property can also be an cell reference."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005285 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005286 "    type     => 'cell',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005287 "    criteria => '>',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005288 "    value    => '$C$1',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005289 "    format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005290 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005291 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005292 "=head2 format"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005293 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005294 "The C<format> parameter is used to specify the format that will be applied to the cell when the conditional formatting criterion is met. The format is created using the C<add_format()> method in the same way as cell formats:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005295 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005296 "    $format = $workbook->add_format( bold => 1, italic => 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005297 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005298 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005299 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005300 "            type     => 'cell',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005301 "            criteria => '>',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005302 "            value    => 5"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005303 "            format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005304 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005305 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005306 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005307 "The conditional format follows the same rules as in Excel: it is superimposed over the existing cell format and not all font and border properties can be modified. Font properties that can't be modified are font name, font size, superscript and subscript. The border property that cannot be modified is diagonal borders."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005308 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005309 "Excel specifies some default formats to be used with conditional formatting. You can replicate them using the following Excel::Writer::XLSX formats:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005310 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005311 "    # Light red fill with dark red text."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005312 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005313 "    my $format1 = $workbook->add_format("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005314 "        bg_color => '#FFC7CE',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005315 "        color    => '#9C0006',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005316 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005317 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005318 "    # Light yellow fill with dark yellow text."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005319 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005320 "    my $format2 = $workbook->add_format("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005321 "        bg_color => '#FFEB9C',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005322 "        color    => '#9C6500',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005323 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005324 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005325 "    # Green fill with dark green text."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005326 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005327 "    my $format3 = $workbook->add_format("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005328 "        bg_color => '#C6EFCE',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005329 "        color    => '#006100',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005330 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005331 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005332 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005333 "=head2 minimum"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005334 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005335 "The C<minimum> parameter is used to set the lower limiting value when the C<criteria> is either C<'between'> or C<'not between'>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005336 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005337 "    validate => 'integer',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005338 "    criteria => 'between',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005339 "    minimum  => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005340 "    maximum  => 100,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005341 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005342 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005343 "=head2 maximum"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005344 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005345 "The C<maximum> parameter is used to set the upper limiting value when the C<criteria> is either C<'between'> or C<'not between'>. See the previous example."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005346 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005347 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005348 "=head2 type => 'date'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005349 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005350 "The C<date> type is the same as the C<cell> type and uses the same criteria and values. However it allows the C<value>, C<minimum> and C<maximum> properties to be specified in the ISO8601 C<yyyy-mm-ddThh:mm:ss.sss> date format which is detailed in the C<write_date_time()> method."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005351 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005352 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:A4',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005353 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005354 "            type     => 'date',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005355 "            criteria => 'greater than',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005356 "            value    => '2011-01-01T',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005357 "            format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005358 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005359 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005360 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005361 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005362 "=head2 type => 'time_period'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005363 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005364 "The C<time_period> type is used to specify Excel's \"Dates Occurring\" style conditional format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005365 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005366 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:A4',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005367 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005368 "            type     => 'time_period',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005369 "            criteria => 'yesterday',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005370 "            format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005371 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005372 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005373 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005374 "The period is set in the C<criteria> and can have one of the following values:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005375 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005376 "        criteria => 'yesterday',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005377 "        criteria => 'today',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005378 "        criteria => 'last 7 days',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005379 "        criteria => 'last week',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005380 "        criteria => 'this week',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005381 "        criteria => 'next week',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005382 "        criteria => 'last month',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005383 "        criteria => 'this month',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005384 "        criteria => 'next month'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005385 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005386 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005387 "=head2 type => 'text'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005388 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005389 "The C<text> type is used to specify Excel's \"Specific Text\" style conditional format. It is used to do simple string matching using the C<criteria> and C<value> parameters:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005390 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005391 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:A4',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005392 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005393 "            type     => 'text',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005394 "            criteria => 'containing',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005395 "            value    => 'foo',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005396 "            format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005397 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005398 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005399 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005400 "The C<criteria> can have one of the following values:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005401 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005402 "    criteria => 'containing',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005403 "    criteria => 'not containing',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005404 "    criteria => 'begins with',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005405 "    criteria => 'ends with',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005406 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005407 "The C<value> parameter should be a string or single character."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005408 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005409 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005410 "=head2 type => 'average'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005411 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005412 "The C<average> type is used to specify Excel's \"Average\" style conditional format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005413 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005414 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:A4',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005415 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005416 "            type     => 'average',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005417 "            criteria => 'above',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005418 "            format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005419 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005420 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005421 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005422 "The type of average for the conditional format range is specified by the C<criteria>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005423 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005424 "    criteria => 'above',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005425 "    criteria => 'below',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005426 "    criteria => 'equal or above',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005427 "    criteria => 'equal or below',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005428 "    criteria => '1 std dev above',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005429 "    criteria => '1 std dev below',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005430 "    criteria => '2 std dev above',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005431 "    criteria => '2 std dev below',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005432 "    criteria => '3 std dev above',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005433 "    criteria => '3 std dev below',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005434 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005435 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005436 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005437 "=head2 type => 'duplicate'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005438 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005439 "The C<duplicate> type is used to highlight duplicate cells in a range:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005440 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005441 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:A4',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005442 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005443 "            type     => 'duplicate',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005444 "            format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005445 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005446 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005447 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005448 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005449 "=head2 type => 'unique'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005450 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005451 "The C<unique> type is used to highlight unique cells in a range:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005452 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005453 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:A4',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005454 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005455 "            type     => 'unique',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005456 "            format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005457 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005458 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005459 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005460 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005461 "=head2 type => 'top'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005462 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005463 "The C<top> type is used to specify the top C<n> values by number or percentage in a range:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005464 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005465 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:A4',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005466 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005467 "            type     => 'top',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005468 "            value    => 10,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005469 "            format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005470 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005471 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005472 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005473 "The C<criteria> can be used to indicate that a percentage condition is required:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005474 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005475 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:A4',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005476 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005477 "            type     => 'top',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005478 "            value    => 10,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005479 "            criteria => '%',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005480 "            format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005481 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005482 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005483 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005484 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005485 "=head2 type => 'bottom'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005486 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005487 "The C<bottom> type is used to specify the bottom C<n> values by number or percentage in a range."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005488 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005489 "It takes the same parameters as C<top>, see above."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005490 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005491 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005492 "=head2 type => 'blanks'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005493 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005494 "The C<blanks> type is used to highlight blank cells in a range:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005495 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005496 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:A4',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005497 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005498 "            type     => 'blanks',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005499 "            format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005500 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005501 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005502 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005503 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005504 "=head2 type => 'no_blanks'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005505 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005506 "The C<no_blanks> type is used to highlight non blank cells in a range:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005507 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005508 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:A4',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005509 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005510 "            type     => 'no_blanks',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005511 "            format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005512 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005513 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005514 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005515 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005516 "=head2 type => 'errors'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005517 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005518 "The C<errors> type is used to highlight error cells in a range:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005519 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005520 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:A4',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005521 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005522 "            type     => 'errors',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005523 "            format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005524 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005525 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005526 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005527 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005528 "=head2 type => 'no_errors'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005529 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005530 "The C<no_errors> type is used to highlight non error cells in a range:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005531 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005532 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:A4',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005533 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005534 "            type     => 'no_errors',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005535 "            format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005536 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005537 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005538 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005539 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005540 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005541 "=head2 type => 'formula'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005542 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005543 "The C<formula> type is used to specify a conditional format based on a user defined formula:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005544 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005545 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:A4',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005546 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005547 "            type     => 'formula',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005548 "            criteria => '=$A$1 > 5',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005549 "            format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005550 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005551 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005552 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005553 "The formula is specified in the C<criteria>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005554 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005555 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005556 "=head2 type => '2_color_scale'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005557 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005558 "The C<2_color_scale> type is used to specify Excel's \"2 Color Scale\" style conditional format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005559 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005560 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:A12',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005561 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005562 "            type  => '2_color_scale',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005563 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005564 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005565 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005566 "This conditional type can be modified with C<min_type>, C<max_type>, C<min_value>, C<max_value>, C<min_color> and C<max_color>, see below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005567 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005568 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005569 "=head2 type => '3_color_scale'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005570 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005571 "The C<3_color_scale> type is used to specify Excel's \"3 Color Scale\" style conditional format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005572 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005573 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:A12',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005574 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005575 "            type  => '3_color_scale',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005576 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005577 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005578 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005579 "This conditional type can be modified with C<min_type>, C<mid_type>, C<max_type>, C<min_value>, C<mid_value>, C<max_value>, C<min_color>, C<mid_color> and C<max_color>, see below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005580 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005581 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005582 "=head2 type => 'data_bar'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005583 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005584 "The C<data_bar> type is used to specify Excel's \"Data Bar\" style conditional format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005585 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005586 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:A12',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005587 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005588 "            type  => 'data_bar',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005589 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005590 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005591 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005592 "This data bar conditional type can be modified with the following parameters, which are explained in the sections below. These properties were available in the original xlsx file specification used in Excel 2007::"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005593 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005594 "    min_type"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005595 "    max_type"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005596 "    min_value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005597 "    max_value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005598 "    bar_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005599 "    bar_only"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005600 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005601 "In Excel 2010 additional data bar properties were added such as solid (non-gradient) bars and control over how negative values are displayed. These properties can be set using the following parameters:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005602 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005603 "    bar_solid"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005604 "    bar_negative_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005605 "    bar_border_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005606 "    bar_negative_border_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005607 "    bar_negative_color_same"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005608 "    bar_negative_border_color_same"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005609 "    bar_no_border"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005610 "    bar_direction"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005611 "    bar_axis_position"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005612 "    bar_axis_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005613 "    data_bar_2010"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005614 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005615 "Files that use these Excel 2010 properties can still be opened in Excel 2007 but the data bars will be displayed without them."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005616 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005617 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005618 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005619 "=head2 type => 'icon_set'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005620 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005621 "The C<icon_set> type is used to specify a conditional format with a set of icons such as traffic lights or arrows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005622 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005623 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:C1',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005624 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005625 "            type         => 'icon_set',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005626 "            icon_style   => '3_traffic_lights',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005627 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005628 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005629 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005630 "The icon set style is specified by the C<icon_style> parameter. Valid options are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005631 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005632 "    3_arrows"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005633 "    3_arrows_gray"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005634 "    3_flags"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005635 "    3_signs"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005636 "    3_symbols"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005637 "    3_symbols_circled"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005638 "    3_traffic_lights"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005639 "    3_traffic_lights_rimmed"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005640 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005641 "    4_arrows"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005642 "    4_arrows_gray"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005643 "    4_ratings"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005644 "    4_red_to_black"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005645 "    4_traffic_lights"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005646 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005647 "    5_arrows"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005648 "    5_arrows_gray"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005649 "    5_quarters"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005650 "    5_ratings"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005651 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005652 "The criteria, type and value of each icon can be specified using the C<icon> array of hash refs with optional C<criteria>, C<type> and C<value> parameters:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005653 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005654 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:D1',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005655 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005656 "            type         => 'icon_set',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005657 "            icon_style   => '4_red_to_black',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005658 "            icons        => [ {criteria => '>',  type => 'number',     value => 90},"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005659 "                              {criteria => '>=', type => 'percentile', value => 50},"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005660 "                              {criteria => '>',  type => 'percent',    value => 25},"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005661 "                            ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005662 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005663 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005664 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005665 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005666 "The C<icons criteria> parameter should be either C<< >= >> or C<< > >>. The default C<criteria> is C<< >= >>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005667 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005668 "The C<icons type> parameter should be one of the following values:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005669 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005670 "    number"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005671 "    percentile"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005672 "    percent"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005673 "    formula"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005674 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005675 "The default C<type> is C<percent>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005676 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005677 "The C<icons value> parameter can be a value or formula:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005678 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005679 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:D1',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005680 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005681 "            type         => 'icon_set',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005682 "            icon_style   => '4_red_to_black',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005683 "            icons        => [ {value => 90},"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005684 "                              {value => 50},"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005685 "                              {value => 25},"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005686 "                            ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005687 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005688 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005689 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005690 "Note: The C<icons> parameters should start with the highest value and with each subsequent one being lower. The default C<value> is C<(n * 100) / number_of_icons>. The lowest number icon in an icon set has properties defined by Excel. Therefore in a C<n> icon set, there is no C<n-1> hash of parameters."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005691 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005692 "The order of the icons can be reversed using the C<reverse_icons> parameter:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005693 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005694 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:C1',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005695 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005696 "            type          => 'icon_set',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005697 "            icon_style    => '3_arrows',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005698 "            reverse_icons => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005699 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005700 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005701 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005702 "The icons can be displayed without the cell value using the C<icons_only> parameter:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005703 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005704 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:C1',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005705 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005706 "            type         => 'icon_set',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005707 "            icon_style   => '3_flags',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005708 "            icons_only   => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005709 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005710 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005711 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005712 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005713 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005714 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005715 "=head2 min_type, mid_type, max_type"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005716 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005717 "The C<min_type> and C<max_type> properties are available when the conditional formatting type is C<2_color_scale>, C<3_color_scale> or C<data_bar>. The C<mid_type> is available for C<3_color_scale>. The properties are used as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005718 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005719 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:A12',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005720 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005721 "            type      => '2_color_scale',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005722 "            min_type  => 'percent',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005723 "            max_type  => 'percent',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005724 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005725 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005726 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005727 "The available min/mid/max types are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005728 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005729 "    min        (for min_type only)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005730 "    num"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005731 "    percent"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005732 "    percentile"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005733 "    formula"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005734 "    max        (for max_type only)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005735 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005736 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005737 "=head2 min_value, mid_value, max_value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005738 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005739 "The C<min_value> and C<max_value> properties are available when the conditional formatting type is C<2_color_scale>, C<3_color_scale> or C<data_bar>. The C<mid_value> is available for C<3_color_scale>. The properties are used as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005740 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005741 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:A12',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005742 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005743 "            type       => '2_color_scale',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005744 "            min_value  => 10,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005745 "            max_value  => 90,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005746 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005747 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005748 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005749 "=head2 min_color, mid_color,  max_color, bar_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005750 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005751 "The C<min_color> and C<max_color> properties are available when the conditional formatting type is C<2_color_scale>, C<3_color_scale> or C<data_bar>. The C<mid_color> is available for C<3_color_scale>. The properties are used as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005752 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005753 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:A12',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005754 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005755 "            type      => '2_color_scale',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005756 "            min_color => \"#C5D9F1\","
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005757 "            max_color => \"#538ED5\","
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005758 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005759 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005760 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005761 "The color can be specified as an Excel::Writer::XLSX color index or, more usefully, as a HTML style RGB hex number, as shown above."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005762 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005763 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005764 "=head2 bar_only"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005765 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005766 "The C<bar_only> parameter property displays a bar data but not the data in the cells:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005767 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005768 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'D3:D14',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005769 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005770 "            type     => 'data_bar',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005771 "            bar_only => 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005772 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005773 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005774 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005775 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005776 "=head2 bar_solid"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005777 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005778 "The C<bar_solid> parameter turns on a solid (non-gradient) fill for data bars:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005779 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005780 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005781 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'H3:H14',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005782 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005783 "            type      => 'data_bar',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005784 "            bar_solid => 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005785 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005786 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005787 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005788 "Note, this property is only visible in Excel 2010 and later."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005789 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005790 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005791 "=head2 bar_negative_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005792 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005793 "The C<bar_negative_color> parameter is used to set the color fill for the negative portion of a data bar."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005794 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005795 "The color can be specified as an Excel::Writer::XLSX color index or as a HTML style RGB hex number, as shown in the other examples."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005796 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005797 "Note, this property is only visible in Excel 2010 and later."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005798 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005799 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005800 "=head2 bar_border_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005801 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005802 "The C<bar_border_color> parameter is used to set the border color of a data bar."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005803 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005804 "The color can be specified as an Excel::Writer::XLSX color index or as a HTML style RGB hex number, as shown in the other examples."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005805 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005806 "Note, this property is only visible in Excel 2010 and later."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005807 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005808 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005809 "=head2 bar_negative_border_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005810 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005811 "The C<bar_negative_border_color> parameter is used to set the border color of the negative portion of a data bar."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005812 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005813 "The color can be specified as an Excel::Writer::XLSX color index or as a HTML style RGB hex number, as shown in the other examples."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005814 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005815 "Note, this property is only visible in Excel 2010 and later."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005816 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005817 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005818 "=head2 bar_negative_color_same"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005819 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005820 "The C<bar_negative_color_same> parameter sets the fill color for the negative portion of a data bar to be the same as the fill color for the positive portion of the data bar:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005821 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005822 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'N3:N14',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005823 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005824 "            type                           => 'data_bar',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005825 "            bar_negative_color_same        => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005826 "            bar_negative_border_color_same => 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005827 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005828 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005829 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005830 "Note, this property is only visible in Excel 2010 and later."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005831 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005832 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005833 "=head2 bar_negative_border_color_same"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005834 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005835 "The C<bar_negative_border_color_same> parameter sets the border color for the negative portion of a data bar to be the same as the border color for the positive portion of the data bar."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005836 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005837 "Note, this property is only visible in Excel 2010 and later."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005838 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005839 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005840 "=head2 bar_no_border"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005841 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005842 "The C<bar_no_border> parameter turns off the border of a data bar."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005843 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005844 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005845 "Note, this property is only visible in Excel 2010 and later, however the default in Excel 2007 is not to have a border."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005846 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005847 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005848 "=head2 bar_direction"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005849 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005850 "The C<bar_direction> parameter sets the direction for data bars. This property can be either C<left> for left-to-right or C<right> for right-to-left. If the property isn't set then Excel will adjust the position automatically based on the context:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005851 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005852 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'J3:J14',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005853 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005854 "            type          => 'data_bar',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005855 "            bar_direction => 'right'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005856 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005857 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005858 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005859 "Note, this property is only visible in Excel 2010 and later."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005860 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005861 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005862 "=head2 bar_axis_position"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005863 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005864 "The C<bar_axis_position> parameter sets the position within the cells for the axis that is shown in data bars when there are negative values to display. The property can be either C<middle> or C<none>. If the property isn't set then Excel will position the axis based on the range of positive and negative values."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005865 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005866 "Note, this property is only visible in Excel 2010 and later."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005867 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005868 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005869 "=head2 bar_axis_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005870 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005871 "The C<bar_axis_color> parameter sets the color for the axis that is shown in data bars when there are negative values to display."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005872 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005873 "The color can be specified as an Excel::Writer::XLSX color index or as a HTML style RGB hex number, as shown in the other examples."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005874 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005875 "Note, this property is only visible in Excel 2010 and later."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005876 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005877 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005878 "=head2 data_bar_2010"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005879 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005880 "The C<data_bar_2010> parameter sets Excel 2010 style data bars even when Excel 2010 specific properties aren't used. This can be used to create consistency across all the data bar formatting in a worksheet:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005881 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005882 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'L3:L14',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005883 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005884 "            type          => 'data_bar',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005885 "            data_bar_2010 => 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005886 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005887 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005888 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005889 "Note, this property is only visible in Excel 2010 and later."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005890 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005891 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005892 "=head2 stop_if_true"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005893 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005894 "The C<stop_if_true> parameter, if set to a true value, will enable the \"stop if true\" feature on the conditional formatting rule, so that subsequent rules are not examined for any cell on which the conditions for this rule are met."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005895 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005896 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005897 "=head2 Conditional Formatting Examples"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005898 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005899 "Example 1. Highlight cells greater than an integer value."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005900 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005901 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:F10',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005902 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005903 "            type     => 'cell',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005904 "            criteria => 'greater than',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005905 "            value    => 5,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005906 "            format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005907 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005908 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005909 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005910 "Example 2. Highlight cells greater than a value in a reference cell."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005911 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005912 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:F10',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005913 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005914 "            type     => 'cell',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005915 "            criteria => 'greater than',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005916 "            value    => '$H$1',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005917 "            format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005918 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005919 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005920 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005921 "Example 3. Highlight cells greater than a certain date:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005922 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005923 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:F10',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005924 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005925 "            type     => 'date',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005926 "            criteria => 'greater than',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005927 "            value    => '2011-01-01T',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005928 "            format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005929 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005930 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005931 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005932 "Example 4. Highlight cells with a date in the last seven days:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005933 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005934 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:F10',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005935 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005936 "            type     => 'time_period',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005937 "            criteria => 'last 7 days',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005938 "            format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005939 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005940 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005941 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005942 "Example 5. Highlight cells with strings starting with the letter C<b>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005943 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005944 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:F10',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005945 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005946 "            type     => 'text',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005947 "            criteria => 'begins with',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005948 "            value    => 'b',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005949 "            format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005950 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005951 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005952 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005953 "Example 6. Highlight cells that are 1 std deviation above the average for the range:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005954 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005955 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:F10',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005956 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005957 "            type     => 'average',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005958 "            format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005959 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005960 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005961 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005962 "Example 7. Highlight duplicate cells in a range:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005963 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005964 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:F10',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005965 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005966 "            type     => 'duplicate',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005967 "            format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005968 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005969 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005970 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005971 "Example 8. Highlight unique cells in a range."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005972 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005973 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:F10',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005974 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005975 "            type     => 'unique',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005976 "            format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005977 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005978 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005979 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005980 "Example 9. Highlight the top 10 cells."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005981 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005982 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:F10',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005983 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005984 "            type     => 'top',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005985 "            value    => 10,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005986 "            format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005987 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005988 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005989 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005990 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005991 "Example 10. Highlight blank cells."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005992 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005993 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:F10',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005994 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005995 "            type     => 'blanks',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005996 "            format   => $format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005997 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005998 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00005999 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006000 "Example 11. Set traffic light icons in 3 cells:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006001 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006002 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'A1:C1',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006003 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006004 "            type         => 'icon_set',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006005 "            icon_style   => '3_traffic_lights',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006006 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006007 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006008 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006009 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006010 "See also the C<conditional_format.pl> example program in C<EXAMPLES>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006011 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006012 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006013 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006014 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006015 "=head1 SPARKLINES IN EXCEL"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006016 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006017 "Sparklines are a feature of Excel 2010+ which allows you to add small charts to worksheet cells. These are useful for showing visual trends in data in a compact format."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006018 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006019 "In Excel::Writer::XLSX Sparklines can be added to cells using the C<add_sparkline()> worksheet method:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006020 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006021 "    $worksheet->add_sparkline("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006022 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006023 "            location => 'F2',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006024 "            range    => 'Sheet1!A2:E2',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006025 "            type     => 'column',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006026 "            style    => 12,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006027 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006028 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006029 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006030 "=begin html"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006031 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006032 "<p><center><img src=\"http://jmcnamara.github.io/excel-writer-xlsx/images/examples/sparklines1.jpg\" alt=\"Sparklines example.\"/></center></p>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006033 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006034 "=end html"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006035 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006036 "B<Note:> Sparklines are a feature of Excel 2010+ only. You can write them to an XLSX file that can be read by Excel 2007 but they won't be displayed."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006037 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006038 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006039 "=head2 add_sparkline( { parameter => 'value', ... } )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006040 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006041 "The C<add_sparkline()> worksheet method is used to add sparklines to a cell or a range of cells."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006042 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006043 "The parameters to C<add_sparkline()> must be passed in a hash ref. The main sparkline parameters are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006044 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006045 "    location        (required)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006046 "    range           (required)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006047 "    type"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006048 "    style"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006049 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006050 "    markers"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006051 "    negative_points"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006052 "    axis"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006053 "    reverse"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006054 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006055 "Other, less commonly used parameters are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006056 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006057 "    high_point"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006058 "    low_point"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006059 "    first_point"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006060 "    last_point"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006061 "    max"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006062 "    min"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006063 "    empty_cells"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006064 "    show_hidden"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006065 "    date_axis"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006066 "    weight"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006067 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006068 "    series_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006069 "    negative_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006070 "    markers_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006071 "    first_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006072 "    last_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006073 "    high_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006074 "    low_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006075 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006076 "These parameters are explained in the sections below:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006077 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006078 "=head2 location"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006079 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006080 "This is the cell where the sparkline will be displayed:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006081 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006082 "    location => 'F1'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006083 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006084 "The C<location> should be a single cell. (For multiple cells see L<Grouped Sparklines> below)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006085 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006086 "To specify the location in row-column notation use the C<xl_rowcol_to_cell()> function from the L<Excel::Writer::XLSX::Utility> module."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006087 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006088 "    use Excel::Writer::XLSX::Utility ':rowcol';"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006089 "    ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006090 "    location => xl_rowcol_to_cell( 0, 5 ), # F1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006091 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006092 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006093 "=head2 range"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006094 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006095 "This specifies the cell data range that the sparkline will plot:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006096 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006097 "    $worksheet->add_sparkline("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006098 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006099 "            location => 'F1',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006100 "            range    => 'A1:E1',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006101 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006102 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006103 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006104 "The C<range> should be a 2D array. (For 3D arrays of cells see L<Grouped Sparklines> below)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006105 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006106 "If C<range> is not on the same worksheet you can specify its location using the usual Excel notation:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006107 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006108 "            range => 'Sheet1!A1:E1',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006109 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006110 "If the worksheet contains spaces or special characters you should quote the worksheet name in the same way that Excel does:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006111 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006112 "            range => q('Monthly Data'!A1:E1),"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006113 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006114 "To specify the location in row-column notation use the C<xl_range()> or C<xl_range_formula()> functions from the L<Excel::Writer::XLSX::Utility> module."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006115 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006116 "    use Excel::Writer::XLSX::Utility ':rowcol';"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006117 "    ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006118 "    range => xl_range( 1, 1,  0, 4 ),                   # 'A1:E1'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006119 "    range => xl_range_formula( 'Sheet1', 0, 0,  0, 4 ), # 'Sheet1!A2:E2'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006120 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006121 "=head2 type"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006122 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006123 "Specifies the type of sparkline. There are 3 available sparkline types:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006124 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006125 "    line    (default)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006126 "    column"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006127 "    win_loss"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006128 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006129 "For example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006130 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006131 "    {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006132 "        location => 'F1',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006133 "        range    => 'A1:E1',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006134 "        type     => 'column',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006135 "    }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006136 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006137 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006138 "=head2 style"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006139 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006140 "Excel provides 36 built-in Sparkline styles in 6 groups of 6. The C<style> parameter can be used to replicate these and should be a corresponding number from 1 .. 36."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006141 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006142 "    {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006143 "        location => 'A14',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006144 "        range    => 'Sheet2!A2:J2',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006145 "        style    => 3,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006146 "    }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006147 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006148 "The style number starts in the top left of the style grid and runs left to right. The default style is 1. It is possible to override colour elements of the sparklines using the C<*_color> parameters below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006149 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006150 "=head2 markers"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006151 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006152 "Turn on the markers for C<line> style sparklines."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006153 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006154 "    {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006155 "        location => 'A6',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006156 "        range    => 'Sheet2!A1:J1',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006157 "        markers  => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006158 "    }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006159 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006160 "Markers aren't shown in Excel for C<column> and C<win_loss> sparklines."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006161 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006162 "=head2 negative_points"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006163 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006164 "Highlight negative values in a sparkline range. This is usually required with C<win_loss> sparklines."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006165 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006166 "    {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006167 "        location        => 'A21',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006168 "        range           => 'Sheet2!A3:J3',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006169 "        type            => 'win_loss',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006170 "        negative_points => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006171 "    }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006172 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006173 "=head2 axis"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006174 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006175 "Display a horizontal axis in the sparkline:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006176 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006177 "    {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006178 "        location => 'A10',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006179 "        range    => 'Sheet2!A1:J1',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006180 "        axis     => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006181 "    }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006182 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006183 "=head2 reverse"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006184 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006185 "Plot the data from right-to-left instead of the default left-to-right:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006186 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006187 "    {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006188 "        location => 'A24',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006189 "        range    => 'Sheet2!A4:J4',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006190 "        type     => 'column',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006191 "        reverse  => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006192 "    }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006193 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006194 "=head2 weight"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006195 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006196 "Adjust the default line weight (thickness) for C<line> style sparklines."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006197 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006198 "     weight => 0.25,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006199 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006200 "The weight value should be one of the following values allowed by Excel:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006201 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006202 "    0.25  0.5   0.75"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006203 "    1     1.25"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006204 "    2.25"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006205 "    3"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006206 "    4.25"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006207 "    6"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006208 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006209 "=head2 high_point, low_point, first_point, last_point"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006210 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006211 "Highlight points in a sparkline range."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006212 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006213 "        high_point  => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006214 "        low_point   => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006215 "        first_point => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006216 "        last_point  => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006217 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006218 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006219 "=head2 max, min"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006220 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006221 "Specify the maximum and minimum vertical axis values:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006222 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006223 "        max         => 0.5,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006224 "        min         => -0.5,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006225 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006226 "As a special case you can set the maximum and minimum to be for a group of sparklines rather than one:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006227 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006228 "        max         => 'group',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006229 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006230 "See L<Grouped Sparklines> below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006231 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006232 "=head2 empty_cells"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006233 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006234 "Define how empty cells are handled in a sparkline."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006235 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006236 "    empty_cells => 'zero',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006237 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006238 "The available options are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006239 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006240 "    gaps   : show empty cells as gaps (the default)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006241 "    zero   : plot empty cells as 0."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006242 "    connect: Connect points with a line (\"line\" type  sparklines only)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006243 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006244 "=head2 show_hidden"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006245 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006246 "Plot data in hidden rows and columns:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006247 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006248 "    show_hidden => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006249 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006250 "Note, this option is off by default."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006251 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006252 "=head2 date_axis"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006253 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006254 "Specify an alternative date axis for the sparkline. This is useful if the data being plotted isn't at fixed width intervals:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006255 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006256 "    {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006257 "        location  => 'F3',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006258 "        range     => 'A3:E3',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006259 "        date_axis => 'A4:E4',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006260 "    }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006261 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006262 "The number of cells in the date range should correspond to the number of cells in the data range."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006263 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006264 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006265 "=head2 series_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006266 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006267 "It is possible to override the colour of a sparkline style using the following parameters:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006268 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006269 "    series_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006270 "    negative_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006271 "    markers_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006272 "    first_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006273 "    last_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006274 "    high_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006275 "    low_color"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006276 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006277 "The color should be specified as a HTML style C<#rrggbb> hex value:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006278 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006279 "    {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006280 "        location     => 'A18',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006281 "        range        => 'Sheet2!A2:J2',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006282 "        type         => 'column',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006283 "        series_color => '#E965E0',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006284 "    }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006285 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006286 "=head2 Grouped Sparklines"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006287 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006288 "The C<add_sparkline()> worksheet method can be used multiple times to write as many sparklines as are required in a worksheet."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006289 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006290 "However, it is sometimes necessary to group contiguous sparklines so that changes that are applied to one are applied to all. In Excel this is achieved by selecting a 3D range of cells for the data C<range> and a 2D range of cells for the C<location>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006291 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006292 "In Excel::Writer::XLSX, you can simulate this by passing an array refs of values to C<location> and C<range>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006293 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006294 "    {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006295 "        location => [ 'A27',          'A28',          'A29'          ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006296 "        range    => [ 'Sheet2!A5:J5', 'Sheet2!A6:J6', 'Sheet2!A7:J7' ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006297 "        markers  => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006298 "    }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006299 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006300 "=head2 Sparkline examples"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006301 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006302 "See the C<sparklines1.pl> and C<sparklines2.pl> example programs in the C<examples> directory of the distro."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006303 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006304 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006305 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006306 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006307 "=head1 TABLES IN EXCEL"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006308 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006309 "Tables in Excel are a way of grouping a range of cells into a single entity that has common formatting or that can be referenced from formulas. Tables can have column headers, autofilters, total rows, column formulas and default formatting."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006310 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006311 "=begin html"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006312 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006313 "<p><center><img src=\"http://jmcnamara.github.io/excel-writer-xlsx/images/examples/tables.jpg\" width=\"640\" height=\"420\" alt=\"Output from tables.pl\" /></center></p>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006314 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006315 "=end html"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006316 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006317 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006318 "For more information see \"An Overview of Excel Tables\" L<http://office.microsoft.com/en-us/excel-help/overview-of-excel-tables-HA010048546.aspx>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006319 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006320 "Note, tables don't work in Excel::Writer::XLSX when C<set_optimization()> mode in on."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006321 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006322 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006323 "=head2 add_table( $row1, $col1, $row2, $col2, { parameter => 'value', ... })"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006324 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006325 "Tables are added to a worksheet using the C<add_table()> method:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006326 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006327 "    $worksheet->add_table( 'B3:F7', { %parameters } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006328 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006329 "The data range can be specified in 'A1' or 'row/col' notation (see also the note about L</Cell notation> for more information):"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006330 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006331 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006332 "    $worksheet->add_table( 'B3:F7' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006333 "    # Same as:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006334 "    $worksheet->add_table(  2, 1, 6, 5 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006335 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006336 "The last parameter in C<add_table()> should be a hash ref containing the parameters that describe the table options and data. The available parameters are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006337 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006338 "        data"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006339 "        autofilter"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006340 "        header_row"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006341 "        banded_columns"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006342 "        banded_rows"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006343 "        first_column"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006344 "        last_column"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006345 "        style"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006346 "        total_row"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006347 "        columns"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006348 "        name"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006349 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006350 "The table parameters are detailed below. There are no required parameters and the hash ref isn't required if no options are specified."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006351 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006352 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006353 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006354 "=head2 data"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006355 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006356 "The C<data> parameter can be used to specify the data in the cells of the table."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006357 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006358 "    my $data = ["
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006359 "        [ 'Apples',  10000, 5000, 8000, 6000 ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006360 "        [ 'Pears',   2000,  3000, 4000, 5000 ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006361 "        [ 'Bananas', 6000,  6000, 6500, 6000 ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006362 "        [ 'Oranges', 500,   300,  200,  700 ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006363 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006364 "    ];"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006365 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006366 "    $worksheet->add_table( 'B3:F7', { data => $data } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006367 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006368 "Table data can also be written separately, as an array or individual cells."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006369 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006370 "    # These two statements are the same as the single statement above."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006371 "    $worksheet->add_table( 'B3:F7' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006372 "    $worksheet->write_col( 'B4', $data );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006373 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006374 "Writing the cell data separately is occasionally required when you need to control the C<write_*()> method used to populate the cells or if you wish to tweak the cell formatting."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006375 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006376 "The C<data> structure should be an array ref of array refs holding row data as shown above."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006377 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006378 "=head2 header_row"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006379 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006380 "The C<header_row> parameter can be used to turn on or off the header row in the table. It is on by default."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006381 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006382 "    $worksheet->add_table( 'B4:F7', { header_row => 0 } ); # Turn header off."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006383 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006384 "The header row will contain default captions such as C<Column 1>, C<Column 2>,  etc. These captions can be overridden using the C<columns> parameter below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006385 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006386 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006387 "=head2 autofilter"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006388 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006389 "The C<autofilter> parameter can be used to turn on or off the autofilter in the header row. It is on by default."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006390 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006391 "    $worksheet->add_table( 'B3:F7', { autofilter => 0 } ); # Turn autofilter off."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006392 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006393 "The C<autofilter> is only shown if the C<header_row> is on. Filters within the table are not supported."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006394 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006395 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006396 "=head2 banded_rows"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006397 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006398 "The C<banded_rows> parameter can be used to used to create rows of alternating colour in the table. It is on by default."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006399 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006400 "    $worksheet->add_table( 'B3:F7', { banded_rows => 0 } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006401 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006402 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006403 "=head2 banded_columns"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006404 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006405 "The C<banded_columns> parameter can be used to used to create columns of alternating colour in the table. It is off by default."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006406 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006407 "    $worksheet->add_table( 'B3:F7', { banded_columns => 1 } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006408 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006409 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006410 "=head2 first_column"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006411 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006412 "The C<first_column> parameter can be used to highlight the first column of the table. The type of highlighting will depend on the C<style> of the table. It may be bold text or a different colour. It is off by default."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006413 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006414 "    $worksheet->add_table( 'B3:F7', { first_column => 1 } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006415 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006416 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006417 "=head2 last_column"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006418 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006419 "The C<last_column> parameter can be used to highlight the last column of the table. The type of highlighting will depend on the C<style> of the table. It may be bold text or a different colour. It is off by default."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006420 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006421 "    $worksheet->add_table( 'B3:F7', { last_column => 1 } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006422 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006423 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006424 "=head2 style"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006425 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006426 "The C<style> parameter can be used to set the style of the table. Standard Excel table format names should be used (with matching capitalisation):"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006427 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006428 "    $worksheet11->add_table("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006429 "        'B3:F7',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006430 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006431 "            data      => $data,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006432 "            style     => 'Table Style Light 11',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006433 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006434 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006435 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006436 "The default table style is 'Table Style Medium 9'."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006437 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006438 "You can also turn the table style off by setting it to 'None':"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006439 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006440 "    $worksheet11->add_table( 'B3:F7', { style => 'None' } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006441 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006442 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006443 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006444 "=head2 name"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006445 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006446 "By default tables are named C<Table1>, C<Table2>, etc. The C<name> parameter can be used to set the name of the table:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006447 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006448 "    $worksheet->add_table( 'B3:F7', { name => 'SalesData' } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006449 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006450 "If you override the table name you must ensure that it doesn't clash with an existing table name and that it follows Excel's requirements for table names L<http://office.microsoft.com/en-001/excel-help/define-and-use-names-in-formulas-HA010147120.aspx#BMsyntax_rules_for_names>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006451 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006452 "If you need to know the name of the table, for example to use it in a formula, you can get it as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006453 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006454 "    my $table      = $worksheet2->add_table( 'B3:F7' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006455 "    my $table_name = $table->{_name};"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006456 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006457 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006458 "=head2 total_row"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006459 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006460 "The C<total_row> parameter can be used to turn on the total row in the last row of a table. It is distinguished from the other rows by a different formatting and also with dropdown C<SUBTOTAL> functions."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006461 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006462 "    $worksheet->add_table( 'B3:F7', { total_row => 1 } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006463 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006464 "The default total row doesn't have any captions or functions. These must by specified via the C<columns> parameter below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006465 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006466 "=head2 columns"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006467 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006468 "The C<columns> parameter can be used to set properties for columns within the table."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006469 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006470 "The sub-properties that can be set are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006471 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006472 "    header"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006473 "    formula"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006474 "    total_string"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006475 "    total_function"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006476 "    total_value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006477 "    format"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006478 "    header_format"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006479 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006480 "The column data must be specified as an array ref of hash refs. For example to override the default 'Column n' style table headers:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006481 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006482 "    $worksheet->add_table("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006483 "        'B3:F7',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006484 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006485 "            data    => $data,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006486 "            columns => ["
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006487 "                { header => 'Product' },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006488 "                { header => 'Quarter 1' },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006489 "                { header => 'Quarter 2' },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006490 "                { header => 'Quarter 3' },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006491 "                { header => 'Quarter 4' },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006492 "            ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006493 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006494 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006495 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006496 "If you don't wish to specify properties for a specific column you pass an empty hash ref and the defaults will be applied:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006497 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006498 "            ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006499 "            columns => ["
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006500 "                { header => 'Product' },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006501 "                { header => 'Quarter 1' },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006502 "                { },                        # Defaults to 'Column 3'."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006503 "                { header => 'Quarter 3' },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006504 "                { header => 'Quarter 4' },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006505 "            ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006506 "            ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006507 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006508 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006509 "Column formulas can by applied using the C<formula> column property:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006510 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006511 "    $worksheet8->add_table("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006512 "        'B3:G7',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006513 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006514 "            data    => $data,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006515 "            columns => ["
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006516 "                { header => 'Product' },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006517 "                { header => 'Quarter 1' },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006518 "                { header => 'Quarter 2' },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006519 "                { header => 'Quarter 3' },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006520 "                { header => 'Quarter 4' },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006521 "                {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006522 "                    header  => 'Year',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006523 "                    formula => '=SUM(Table8[@[Quarter 1]:[Quarter 4]])'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006524 "                },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006525 "            ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006526 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006527 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006528 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006529 "The Excel 2007 C<[#This Row]> and Excel 2010 C<@> structural references are supported within the formula."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006530 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006531 "As stated above the C<total_row> table parameter turns on the \"Total\" row in the table but it doesn't populate it with any defaults. Total captions and functions must be specified via the C<columns> property and the C<total_string>, C<total_function> and C<total_value> sub properties:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006532 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006533 "    $worksheet10->add_table("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006534 "        'B3:F8',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006535 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006536 "            data      => $data,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006537 "            total_row => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006538 "            columns   => ["
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006539 "                { header => 'Product',   total_string   => 'Totals' },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006540 "                { header => 'Quarter 1', total_function => 'sum' },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006541 "                { header => 'Quarter 2', total_function => 'sum' },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006542 "                { header => 'Quarter 3', total_function => 'sum' },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006543 "                { header => 'Quarter 4', total_function => 'sum' },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006544 "            ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006545 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006546 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006547 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006548 "The supported totals row C<SUBTOTAL> functions are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006549 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006550 "        average"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006551 "        count_nums"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006552 "        count"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006553 "        max"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006554 "        min"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006555 "        std_dev"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006556 "        sum"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006557 "        var"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006558 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006559 "User defined functions or formulas aren't supported."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006560 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006561 "It is also possible to set a calculated value for the C<total_function> using the C<total_value> sub property. This is only necessary when creating workbooks for applications that cannot calculate the value of formulas automatically. This is similar to setting the C<value> optional property in C<write_formula()>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006562 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006563 "    $worksheet10->add_table("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006564 "        'B3:F8',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006565 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006566 "            data      => $data,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006567 "            total_row => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006568 "            columns   => ["
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006569 "                { total_string   => 'Totals' },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006570 "                { total_function => 'sum', total_value => 100 },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006571 "                { total_function => 'sum', total_value => 200 },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006572 "                { total_function => 'sum', total_value => 100 },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006573 "                { total_function => 'sum', total_value => 400 },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006574 "            ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006575 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006576 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006577 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006578 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006579 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006580 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006581 "Formatting can also be applied to columns, to the column data using C<format> and to the header using C<header_format>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006582 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006583 "    my $currency_format = $workbook->add_format( num_format => '$#,##0' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006584 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006585 "    $worksheet->add_table("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006586 "        'B3:D8',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006587 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006588 "            data      => $data,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006589 "            total_row => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006590 "            columns   => ["
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006591 "                { header => 'Product', total_string => 'Totals' },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006592 "                {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006593 "                    header         => 'Quarter 1',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006594 "                    total_function => 'sum',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006595 "                    format         => $currency_format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006596 "                },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006597 "                {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006598 "                    header         => 'Quarter 2',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006599 "                    header_format  => $bold,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006600 "                    total_function => 'sum',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006601 "                    format         => $currency_format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006602 "                },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006603 "            ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006604 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006605 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006606 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006607 "Standard Excel::Writer::XLSX format objects can be used. However, they should be limited to numerical formats for the columns and simple formatting like text wrap for the headers. Overriding other table formatting may produce inconsistent results."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006608 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006609 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006610 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006611 "=head1 FORMULAS AND FUNCTIONS IN EXCEL"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006612 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006613 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006614 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006615 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006616 "=head2 Introduction"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006617 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006618 "The following is a brief introduction to formulas and functions in Excel and Excel::Writer::XLSX."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006619 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006620 "A formula is a string that begins with an equals sign:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006621 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006622 "    '=A1+B1'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006623 "    '=AVERAGE(1, 2, 3)'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006624 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006625 "The formula can contain numbers, strings, boolean values, cell references, cell ranges and functions. Named ranges are not supported. Formulas should be written as they appear in Excel, that is cells and functions must be in uppercase."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006626 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006627 "Cells in Excel are referenced using the A1 notation system where the column is designated by a letter and the row by a number. Columns range from A to XFD i.e. 0 to 16384, rows range from 1 to 1048576. The C<Excel::Writer::XLSX::Utility> module that is included in the distro contains helper functions for dealing with A1 notation, for example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006628 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006629 "    use Excel::Writer::XLSX::Utility;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006630 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006631 "    ( $row, $col ) = xl_cell_to_rowcol( 'C2' );    # (1, 2)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006632 "    $str = xl_rowcol_to_cell( 1, 2 );              # C2"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006633 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006634 "The Excel C<$> notation in cell references is also supported. This allows you to specify whether a row or column is relative or absolute. This only has an effect if the cell is copied. The following examples show relative and absolute values."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006635 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006636 "    '=A1'   # Column and row are relative"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006637 "    '=$A1'  # Column is absolute and row is relative"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006638 "    '=A$1'  # Column is relative and row is absolute"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006639 "    '=$A$1' # Column and row are absolute"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006640 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006641 "Formulas can also refer to cells in other worksheets of the current workbook. For example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006642 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006643 "    '=Sheet2!A1'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006644 "    '=Sheet2!A1:A5'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006645 "    '=Sheet2:Sheet3!A1'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006646 "    '=Sheet2:Sheet3!A1:A5'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006647 "    q{='Test Data'!A1}"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006648 "    q{='Test Data1:Test Data2'!A1}"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006649 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006650 "The sheet reference and the cell reference are separated by C<!> the exclamation mark symbol. If worksheet names contain spaces, commas or parentheses then Excel requires that the name is enclosed in single quotes as shown in the last two examples above. In order to avoid using a lot of escape characters you can use the quote operator C<q{}> to protect the quotes. See C<perlop> in the main Perl documentation. Only valid sheet names that have been added using the C<add_worksheet()> method can be used in formulas. You cannot reference external workbooks."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006651 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006652 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006653 "The following table lists the operators that are available in Excel's formulas. The majority of the operators are the same as Perl's, differences are indicated:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006654 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006655 "    Arithmetic operators:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006656 "    ====================="
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006657 "    Operator  Meaning                   Example"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006658 "       +      Addition                  1+2"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006659 "       -      Subtraction               2-1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006660 "       *      Multiplication            2*3"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006661 "       /      Division                  1/4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006662 "       ^      Exponentiation            2^3      # Equivalent to **"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006663 "       -      Unary minus               -(1+2)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006664 "       %      Percent (Not modulus)     13%"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006665 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006666 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006667 "    Comparison operators:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006668 "    ====================="
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006669 "    Operator  Meaning                   Example"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006670 "        =     Equal to                  A1 =  B1 # Equivalent to =="
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006671 "        <>    Not equal to              A1 <> B1 # Equivalent to !="
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006672 "        >     Greater than              A1 >  B1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006673 "        <     Less than                 A1 <  B1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006674 "        >=    Greater than or equal to  A1 >= B1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006675 "        <=    Less than or equal to     A1 <= B1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006676 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006677 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006678 "    String operator:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006679 "    ================"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006680 "    Operator  Meaning                   Example"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006681 "        &     Concatenation             \"Hello \" & \"World!\" # [1]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006682 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006683 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006684 "    Reference operators:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006685 "    ===================="
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006686 "    Operator  Meaning                   Example"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006687 "        :     Range operator            A1:A4               # [2]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006688 "        ,     Union operator            SUM(1, 2+2, B3)     # [3]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006689 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006690 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006691 "    Notes:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006692 "    [1]: Equivalent to \"Hello \" . \"World!\" in Perl."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006693 "    [2]: This range is equivalent to cells A1, A2, A3 and A4."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006694 "    [3]: The comma behaves like the list separator in Perl."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006695 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006696 "The range and comma operators can have different symbols in non-English versions of Excel, see below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006697 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006698 "For a general introduction to Excel's formulas and an explanation of the syntax of the function refer to the Excel help files or the following: L<http://office.microsoft.com/en-us/assistance/CH062528031033.aspx>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006699 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006700 "In most cases a formula in Excel can be used directly in the C<write_formula> method. However, there are a few potential issues and differences that the user should be aware of. These are explained in the following sections."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006701 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006702 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006703 "=head2 Non US Excel functions and syntax"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006704 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006705 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006706 "Excel stores formulas in the format of the US English version, regardless of the language or locale of the end-user's version of Excel. Therefore all formula function names written using Excel::Writer::XLSX must be in English:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006707 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006708 "    worksheet->write_formula('A1', '=SUM(1, 2, 3)');   # OK"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006709 "    worksheet->write_formula('A2', '=SOMME(1, 2, 3)'); # French. Error on load."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006710 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006711 "Also, formulas must be written with the US style separator/range operator which is a comma (not semi-colon). Therefore a formula with multiple values should be written as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006712 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006713 "    worksheet->write_formula('A1', '=SUM(1, 2, 3)'); # OK"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006714 "    worksheet->write_formula('A2', '=SUM(1; 2; 3)'); # Semi-colon. Error on load."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006715 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006716 "If you have a non-English version of Excel you can use the following multi-lingual Formula Translator (L<http://en.excel-translator.de/language/>) to help you convert the formula. It can also replace semi-colons with commas."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006717 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006718 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006719 "=head2 Formulas added in Excel 2010 and later"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006720 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006721 "Excel 2010 and later added functions which weren't defined in the original file specification. These functions are referred to by Microsoft as I<future> functions. Examples of these functions are C<ACOT>, C<CHISQ.DIST.RT> , C<CONFIDENCE.NORM>, C<STDEV.P>, C<STDEV.S> and C<WORKDAY.INTL>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006722 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006723 "When written using C<write_formula()> these functions need to be fully qualified with a C<_xlfn.> (or other) prefix as they are shown the list below. For example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006724 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006725 "    worksheet->write_formula('A1', '=_xlfn.STDEV.S(B1:B10)')"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006726 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006727 "They will appear without the prefix in Excel."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006728 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006729 "The following list is taken from the MS XLSX extensions documentation on future functions: L<http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/dd907480%28v=office.12%29.aspx>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006730 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006731 "    _xlfn.ACOT"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006732 "    _xlfn.ACOTH"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006733 "    _xlfn.AGGREGATE"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006734 "    _xlfn.ARABIC"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006735 "    _xlfn.ARRAYTOTEXT"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006736 "    _xlfn.BASE"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006737 "    _xlfn.BETA.DIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006738 "    _xlfn.BETA.INV"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006739 "    _xlfn.BINOM.DIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006740 "    _xlfn.BINOM.DIST.RANGE"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006741 "    _xlfn.BINOM.INV"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006742 "    _xlfn.BITAND"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006743 "    _xlfn.BITLSHIFT"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006744 "    _xlfn.BITOR"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006745 "    _xlfn.BITRSHIFT"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006746 "    _xlfn.BITXOR"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006747 "    _xlfn.CEILING.MATH"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006748 "    _xlfn.CEILING.PRECISE"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006749 "    _xlfn.CHISQ.DIST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006750 "    _xlfn.CHISQ.DIST.RT"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006751 "    _xlfn.CHISQ.INV"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006752 "    _xlfn.CHISQ.INV.RT"
