NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006860 "    _xlfn.Z.TEST"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006861 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006862 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006863 "=head2 Using Tables in Formulas"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006864 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006865 "Worksheet tables can be added with Excel::Writer::XLSX using the C<add_table()> method:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006866 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006867 "    worksheet->add_table('B3:F7', {options});"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006868 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006869 "By default tables are named C<Table1>, C<Table2>, etc., in the order that they are added. However it can also be set by the user using the C<name> parameter:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006870 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006871 "    worksheet->add_table('B3:F7', {'name': 'SalesData'});"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006872 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006873 "If you need to know the name of the table, for example to use it in a formula,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006874 "you can get it as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006875 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006876 "    table = worksheet->add_table('B3:F7');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006877 "    table_name = table->{_name};"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006878 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006879 "When used in a formula a table name such as C<TableX> should be referred to as C<TableX[]> (like a Perl array):"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006880 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006881 "    worksheet->write_formula('A5', '=VLOOKUP(\"Sales\", Table1[], 2, FALSE');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006882 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006883 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006884 "=head2 Dealing with #NAME? errors"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006885 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006886 "If there is an error in the syntax of a formula it is usually displayed in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006887 "Excel as C<#NAME?>. If you encounter an error like this you can debug it as"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006888 "follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006889 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006890 "=over"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006891 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006892 "=item 1. Ensure the formula is valid in Excel by copying and pasting it into a cell. Note, this should be done in Excel and not other applications such as OpenOffice or LibreOffice since they may have slightly different syntax."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006893 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006894 "=item 2. Ensure the formula is using comma separators instead of semi-colons, see L<Non US Excel functions and syntax> above."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006895 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006896 "=item 3. Ensure the formula is in English, see L<Non US Excel functions and syntax> above."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006897 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006898 "=item 4. Ensure that the formula doesn't contain an Excel 2010+ future function as listed in L<Formulas added in Excel 2010 and later> above. If it does then ensure that the correct prefix is used."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006899 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006900 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006901 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006902 "Finally if you have completed all the previous steps and still get a C<#NAME?> error you can examine a valid Excel file to see what the correct syntax should be. To do this you should create a valid formula in Excel and save the file. You can then examine the XML in the unzipped file."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006903 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006904 "The following shows how to do that using Linux C<unzip> and libxml's xmllint"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006905 "L<http://xmlsoft.org/xmllint.html> to format the XML for clarity:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006906 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006907 "    $ unzip myfile.xlsx -d myfile"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006908 "    $ xmllint --format myfile/xl/worksheets/sheet1.xml | grep '<f>'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006909 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006910 "            <f>SUM(1, 2, 3)</f>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006911 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006912 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006913 "=head2 Formula Results"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006914 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006915 "Excel::Writer::XLSX doesn't calculate the result of a formula and instead stores the value 0 as the formula result. It then sets a global flag in the XLSX file to say that all formulas and functions should be recalculated when the file is opened."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006916 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006917 "This is the method recommended in the Excel documentation and in general it works fine with spreadsheet applications. However, applications that don't have a facility to calculate formulas will only display the 0 results. Examples of such applications are Excel Viewer, PDF Converters, and some mobile device applications."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006918 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006919 "If required, it is also possible to specify the calculated result of the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006920 "formula using the optional last C<value> parameter in C<write_formula>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006921 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006922 "    worksheet->write_formula('A1', '=2+2', num_format, 4);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006923 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006924 "The C<value> parameter can be a number, a string, a boolean sting (C<'TRUE'> or C<'FALSE'>) or one of the following Excel error codes:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006925 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006926 "    #DIV/0!"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006927 "    #N/A"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006928 "    #NAME?"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006929 "    #NULL!"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006930 "    #NUM!"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006931 "    #REF!"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006932 "    #VALUE!"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006933 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006934 "It is also possible to specify the calculated result of an array formula created with C<write_array_formula>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006935 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006936 "    # Specify the result for a single cell range."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006937 "    worksheet->write_array_formula('A1:A1', '{=SUM(B1:C1*B2:C2)}', format, 2005);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006938 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006939 "However, using this parameter only writes a single value to the upper left cell in the result array. For a multi-cell array formula where the results are required, the other result values can be specified by using C<write_number()> to write to the appropriate cell:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006940 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006941 "    # Specify the results for a multi cell range."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006942 "    worksheet->write_array_formula('A1:A3', '{=TREND(C1:C3,B1:B3)}', format, 15);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006943 "    worksheet->write_number('A2', 12, format);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006944 "    worksheet->write_number('A3', 14, format);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006945 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006946 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006947 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006948 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006949 "=head1 WORKING WITH VBA MACROS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006950 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006951 "An Excel C<xlsm> file is exactly the same as a C<xlsx> file except that is includes an additional C<vbaProject.bin> file which contains functions and/or macros. Excel uses a different extension to differentiate between the two file formats since files containing macros are usually subject to additional security checks."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006952 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006953 "The C<vbaProject.bin> file is a binary OLE COM container. This was the format used in older C<xls> versions of Excel prior to Excel 2007. Unlike all of the other components of an xlsx/xlsm file the data isn't stored in XML format. Instead the functions and macros as stored as pre-parsed binary format. As such it wouldn't be feasible to define macros and create a C<vbaProject.bin> file from scratch (at least not in the remaining lifespan and interest levels of the author)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006954 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006955 "Instead a workaround is used to extract C<vbaProject.bin> files from existing xlsm files and then add these to Excel::Writer::XLSX files."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006956 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006957 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006958 "=head2 The extract_vba utility"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006959 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006960 "The C<extract_vba> utility is used to extract the C<vbaProject.bin> binary from an Excel 2007+ xlsm file. The utility is included in the Excel::Writer::XLSX bin directory and is also installed as a standalone executable file:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006961 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006962 "    $ extract_vba macro_file.xlsm"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006963 "    Extracted: vbaProject.bin"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006964 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006965 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006966 "=head2 Adding the VBA macros to a Excel::Writer::XLSX file"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006967 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006968 "Once the C<vbaProject.bin> file has been extracted it can be added to the Excel::Writer::XLSX workbook using the C<add_vba_project()> method:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006969 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006970 "    $workbook->add_vba_project( './vbaProject.bin' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006971 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006972 "If the VBA file contains functions you can then refer to them in calculations using C<write_formula>:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006973 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006974 "    $worksheet->write_formula( 'A1', '=MyMortgageCalc(200000, 25)' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006975 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006976 "Excel files that contain functions and macros should use an C<xlsm> extension or else Excel will complain and possibly not open the file:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006977 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006978 "    my $workbook  = Excel::Writer::XLSX->new( 'file.xlsm' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006979 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006980 "It is also possible to assign a macro to a button that is inserted into a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006981 "worksheet using the C<insert_button()> method:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006982 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006983 "    my $workbook  = Excel::Writer::XLSX->new( 'file.xlsm' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006984 "    ..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006985 "    $workbook->add_vba_project( './vbaProject.bin' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006986 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006987 "    $worksheet->insert_button( 'C2', { macro => 'my_macro' } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006988 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006989 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006990 "It may be necessary to specify a more explicit macro name prefixed by the workbook VBA name as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006991 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006992 "    $worksheet->insert_button( 'C2', { macro => 'ThisWorkbook.my_macro' } );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006993 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006994 "See the C<macros.pl> from the examples directory for a working example."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006995 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006996 "Note: Button is the only VBA Control supported by Excel::Writer::XLSX. Due to the large effort in implementation (1+ man months) it is unlikely that any other form elements will be added in the future."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006997 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006998 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00006999 "=head2 Setting the VBA codenames"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007000 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007001 "VBA macros generally refer to workbook and worksheet objects. If the VBA codenames aren't specified then Excel::Writer::XLSX will use the Excel defaults of C<ThisWorkbook> and C<Sheet1>, C<Sheet2> etc."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007002 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007003 "If the macro uses other codenames you can set them using the workbook and worksheet C<set_vba_name()> methods as follows:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007004 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007005 "      $workbook->set_vba_name( 'MyWorkbook' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007006 "      $worksheet->set_vba_name( 'MySheet' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007007 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007008 "You can find the names that are used in the VBA editor or by unzipping the C<xlsm> file and grepping the files. The following shows how to do that using libxml's xmllint L<http://xmlsoft.org/xmllint.html> to format the XML for clarity:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007009 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007010 "    $ unzip myfile.xlsm -d myfile"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007011 "    $ xmllint --format `find myfile -name \"*.xml\" | xargs` | grep \"Pr.*codeName\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007012 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007013 "      <workbookPr codeName=\"MyWorkbook\" defaultThemeVersion=\"124226\"/>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007014 "      <sheetPr codeName=\"MySheet\"/>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007015 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007016 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007017 "Note: This step is particularly important for macros created with non-English versions of Excel."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007018 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007019 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007020 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007021 "=head2 What to do if it doesn't work"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007022 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007023 "This feature should be considered experimental and there is no guarantee that it will work in all cases. Some effort may be required and some knowledge of VBA will certainly help. If things don't work out here are some things to try:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007024 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007025 "=over"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007026 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007027 "=item *"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007028 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007029 "Start with a simple macro file, ensure that it works and then add complexity."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007030 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007031 "=item *"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007032 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007033 "Try to extract the macros from an Excel 2007 file. The method should work with macros from later versions (it was also tested with Excel 2010 macros). However there may be features in the macro files of more recent version of Excel that aren't backward compatible."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007034 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007035 "=item *"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007036 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007037 "Check the code names that macros use to refer to the workbook and worksheets (see the previous section above). In general VBA uses a code name of C<ThisWorkbook> to refer to the current workbook and the sheet name (such as C<Sheet1>) to refer to the worksheets. These are the defaults used by Excel::Writer::XLSX. If the macro uses other names then you can specify these using the workbook and worksheet C<set_vba_name()> methods:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007038 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007039 "      $workbook>set_vba_name( 'MyWorkbook' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007040 "      $worksheet->set_vba_name( 'MySheet' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007041 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007042 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007043 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007044 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007045 "=head1 EXAMPLES"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007046 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007047 "See L<Excel::Writer::XLSX::Examples> for a full list of examples."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007048 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007049 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007050 "=head2 Example 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007051 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007052 "The following example shows some of the basic features of Excel::Writer::XLSX."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007053 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007054 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007055 "    #!/usr/bin/perl -w"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007056 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007057 "    use strict;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007058 "    use Excel::Writer::XLSX;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007059 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007060 "    # Create a new workbook called simple.xlsx and add a worksheet"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007061 "    my $workbook  = Excel::Writer::XLSX->new( 'simple.xlsx' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007062 "    my $worksheet = $workbook->add_worksheet();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007063 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007064 "    # The general syntax is write($row, $column, $token). Note that row and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007065 "    # column are zero indexed"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007066 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007067 "    # Write some text"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007068 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 0, 'Hi Excel!' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007069 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007070 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007071 "    # Write some numbers"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007072 "    $worksheet->write( 2, 0, 3 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007073 "    $worksheet->write( 3, 0, 3.00000 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007074 "    $worksheet->write( 4, 0, 3.00001 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007075 "    $worksheet->write( 5, 0, 3.14159 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007076 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007077 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007078 "    # Write some formulas"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007079 "    $worksheet->write( 7, 0, '=A3 + A6' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007080 "    $worksheet->write( 8, 0, '=IF(A5>3,\"Yes\", \"No\")' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007081 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007082 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007083 "    # Write a hyperlink"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007084 "    my $hyperlink_format = $workbook->add_format("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007085 "        color     => 'blue',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007086 "        underline => 1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007087 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007088 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007089 "    $worksheet->write( 10, 0, 'http://www.perl.com/', $hyperlink_format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007090 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007091 "    $workbook->close();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007092 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007093 "=begin html"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007094 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007095 "<p><center><img src=\"http://jmcnamara.github.io/excel-writer-xlsx/images/examples/a_simple.jpg\" width=\"640\" height=\"420\" alt=\"Output from a_simple.pl\" /></center></p>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007096 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007097 "=end html"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007098 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007099 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007100 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007101 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007102 "=head2 Example 2"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007103 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007104 "The following is a general example which demonstrates some features of working with multiple worksheets."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007105 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007106 "    #!/usr/bin/perl -w"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007107 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007108 "    use strict;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007109 "    use Excel::Writer::XLSX;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007110 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007111 "    # Create a new Excel workbook"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007112 "    my $workbook = Excel::Writer::XLSX->new( 'regions.xlsx' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007113 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007114 "    # Add some worksheets"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007115 "    my $north = $workbook->add_worksheet( 'North' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007116 "    my $south = $workbook->add_worksheet( 'South' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007117 "    my $east  = $workbook->add_worksheet( 'East' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007118 "    my $west  = $workbook->add_worksheet( 'West' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007119 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007120 "    # Add a Format"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007121 "    my $format = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007122 "    $format->set_bold();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007123 "    $format->set_color( 'blue' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007124 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007125 "    # Add a caption to each worksheet"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007126 "    for my $worksheet ( $workbook->sheets() ) {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007127 "        $worksheet->write( 0, 0, 'Sales', $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007128 "    }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007129 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007130 "    # Write some data"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007131 "    $north->write( 0, 1, 200000 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007132 "    $south->write( 0, 1, 100000 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007133 "    $east->write( 0, 1, 150000 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007134 "    $west->write( 0, 1, 100000 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007135 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007136 "    # Set the active worksheet"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007137 "    $south->activate();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007138 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007139 "    # Set the width of the first column"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007140 "    $south->set_column( 0, 0, 20 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007141 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007142 "    # Set the active cell"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007143 "    $south->set_selection( 0, 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007144 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007145 "    $workbook->close();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007146 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007147 "=begin html"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007148 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007149 "<p><center><img src=\"http://jmcnamara.github.io/excel-writer-xlsx/images/examples/regions.jpg\" width=\"640\" height=\"420\" alt=\"Output from regions.pl\" /></center></p>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007150 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007151 "=end html"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007152 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007153 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007154 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007155 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007156 "=head2 Example 3"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007157 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007158 "Example of how to add conditional formatting to an Excel::Writer::XLSX file. The example below highlights cells that have a value greater than or equal to 50 in red and cells below that value in green."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007159 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007160 "    #!/usr/bin/perl"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007161 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007162 "    use strict;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007163 "    use warnings;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007164 "    use Excel::Writer::XLSX;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007165 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007166 "    my $workbook  = Excel::Writer::XLSX->new( 'conditional_format.xlsx' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007167 "    my $worksheet = $workbook->add_worksheet();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007168 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007169 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007170 "    # This example below highlights cells that have a value greater than or"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007171 "    # equal to 50 in red and cells below that value in green."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007172 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007173 "    # Light red fill with dark red text."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007174 "    my $format1 = $workbook->add_format("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007175 "        bg_color => '#FFC7CE',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007176 "        color    => '#9C0006',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007177 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007178 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007179 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007180 "    # Green fill with dark green text."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007181 "    my $format2 = $workbook->add_format("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007182 "        bg_color => '#C6EFCE',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007183 "        color    => '#006100',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007184 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007185 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007186 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007187 "    # Some sample data to run the conditional formatting against."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007188 "    my $data = ["
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007189 "        [ 34, 72,  38, 30, 75, 48, 75, 66, 84, 86 ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007190 "        [ 6,  24,  1,  84, 54, 62, 60, 3,  26, 59 ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007191 "        [ 28, 79,  97, 13, 85, 93, 93, 22, 5,  14 ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007192 "        [ 27, 71,  40, 17, 18, 79, 90, 93, 29, 47 ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007193 "        [ 88, 25,  33, 23, 67, 1,  59, 79, 47, 36 ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007194 "        [ 24, 100, 20, 88, 29, 33, 38, 54, 54, 88 ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007195 "        [ 6,  57,  88, 28, 10, 26, 37, 7,  41, 48 ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007196 "        [ 52, 78,  1,  96, 26, 45, 47, 33, 96, 36 ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007197 "        [ 60, 54,  81, 66, 81, 90, 80, 93, 12, 55 ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007198 "        [ 70, 5,   46, 14, 71, 19, 66, 36, 41, 21 ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007199 "    ];"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007200 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007201 "    my $caption = 'Cells with values >= 50 are in light red. '"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007202 "      . 'Values < 50 are in light green';"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007203 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007204 "    # Write the data."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007205 "    $worksheet->write( 'A1', $caption );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007206 "    $worksheet->write_col( 'B3', $data );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007207 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007208 "    # Write a conditional format over a range."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007209 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'B3:K12',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007210 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007211 "            type     => 'cell',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007212 "            criteria => '>=',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007213 "            value    => 50,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007214 "            format   => $format1,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007215 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007216 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007217 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007218 "    # Write another conditional format over the same range."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007219 "    $worksheet->conditional_formatting( 'B3:K12',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007220 "        {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007221 "            type     => 'cell',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007222 "            criteria => '<',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007223 "            value    => 50,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007224 "            format   => $format2,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007225 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007226 "    );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007227 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007228 "    $workbook->close();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007229 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007230 "=begin html"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007231 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007232 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007233 "<p><center><img src=\"http://jmcnamara.github.io/excel-writer-xlsx/images/examples/conditional_format.jpg\" width=\"640\" height=\"420\" alt=\"Output from conditional_format.pl\" /></center></p>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007234 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007235 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007236 "=end html"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007237 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007238 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007239 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007240 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007241 "=head2 Example 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007242 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007243 "The following is a simple example of using functions."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007244 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007245 "    #!/usr/bin/perl -w"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007246 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007247 "    use strict;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007248 "    use Excel::Writer::XLSX;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007249 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007250 "    # Create a new workbook and add a worksheet"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007251 "    my $workbook  = Excel::Writer::XLSX->new( 'stats.xlsx' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007252 "    my $worksheet = $workbook->add_worksheet( 'Test data' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007253 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007254 "    # Set the column width for columns 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007255 "    $worksheet->set_column( 0, 0, 20 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007256 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007257 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007258 "    # Create a format for the headings"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007259 "    my $format = $workbook->add_format();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007260 "    $format->set_bold();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007261 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007262 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007263 "    # Write the sample data"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007264 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 0, 'Sample', $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007265 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 1, 1 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007266 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 2, 2 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007267 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 3, 3 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007268 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 4, 4 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007269 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 5, 5 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007270 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 6, 6 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007271 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 7, 7 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007272 "    $worksheet->write( 0, 8, 8 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007273 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007274 "    $worksheet->write( 1, 0, 'Length', $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007275 "    $worksheet->write( 1, 1, 25.4 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007276 "    $worksheet->write( 1, 2, 25.4 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007277 "    $worksheet->write( 1, 3, 24.8 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007278 "    $worksheet->write( 1, 4, 25.0 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007279 "    $worksheet->write( 1, 5, 25.3 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007280 "    $worksheet->write( 1, 6, 24.9 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007281 "    $worksheet->write( 1, 7, 25.2 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007282 "    $worksheet->write( 1, 8, 24.8 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007283 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007284 "    # Write some statistical functions"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007285 "    $worksheet->write( 4, 0, 'Count', $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007286 "    $worksheet->write( 4, 1, '=COUNT(B1:I1)' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007287 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007288 "    $worksheet->write( 5, 0, 'Sum', $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007289 "    $worksheet->write( 5, 1, '=SUM(B2:I2)' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007290 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007291 "    $worksheet->write( 6, 0, 'Average', $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007292 "    $worksheet->write( 6, 1, '=AVERAGE(B2:I2)' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007293 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007294 "    $worksheet->write( 7, 0, 'Min', $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007295 "    $worksheet->write( 7, 1, '=MIN(B2:I2)' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007296 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007297 "    $worksheet->write( 8, 0, 'Max', $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007298 "    $worksheet->write( 8, 1, '=MAX(B2:I2)' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007299 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007300 "    $worksheet->write( 9, 0, 'Standard Deviation', $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007301 "    $worksheet->write( 9, 1, '=STDEV(B2:I2)' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007302 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007303 "    $worksheet->write( 10, 0, 'Kurtosis', $format );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007304 "    $worksheet->write( 10, 1, '=KURT(B2:I2)' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007305 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007306 "    $workbook->close();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007307 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007308 "=begin html"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007309 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007310 "<p><center><img src=\"http://jmcnamara.github.io/excel-writer-xlsx/images/examples/stats.jpg\" width=\"640\" height=\"420\" alt=\"Output from stats.pl\" /></center></p>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007311 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007312 "=end html"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007313 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007314 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007315 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007316 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007317 "=head2 Example 5"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007318 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007319 "The following example converts a tab separated file called C<tab.txt> into an Excel file called C<tab.xlsx>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007320 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007321 "    #!/usr/bin/perl -w"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007322 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007323 "    use strict;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007324 "    use Excel::Writer::XLSX;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007325 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007326 "    open( TABFILE, 'tab.txt' ) or die \"tab.txt: $!\";"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007327 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007328 "    my $workbook  = Excel::Writer::XLSX->new( 'tab.xlsx' );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007329 "    my $worksheet = $workbook->add_worksheet();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007330 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007331 "    # Row and column are zero indexed"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007332 "    my $row = 0;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007333 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007334 "    while ( <TABFILE> ) {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007335 "        chomp;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007336 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007337 "        # Split on single tab"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007338 "        my @fields = split( '\\t', $_ );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007339 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007340 "        my $col = 0;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007341 "        for my $token ( @fields ) {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007342 "            $worksheet->write( $row, $col, $token );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007343 "            $col++;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007344 "        }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007345 "        $row++;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007346 "    }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007347 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007348 "    $workbook->close();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007349 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007350 "NOTE: This is a simple conversion program for illustrative purposes only. For converting a CSV or Tab separated or any other type of delimited text file to Excel I recommend the more rigorous csv2xls program that is part of H.Merijn Brand's L<Text::CSV_XS> module distro."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007351 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007352 "See the examples/csv2xls link here: L<http://search.cpan.org/~hmbrand/Text-CSV_XS/MANIFEST>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007353 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007354 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007355 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007356 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007357 "=head2 Additional Examples"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007358 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007359 "The following is a description of the example files that are provided"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007360 "in the standard Excel::Writer::XLSX distribution. They demonstrate the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007361 "different features and options of the module. See L<Excel::Writer::XLSX::Examples> for more details."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007362 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007363 "    Getting started"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007364 "    ==============="
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007365 "    a_simple.pl             A simple demo of some of the features."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007366 "    bug_report.pl           A template for submitting bug reports."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007367 "    demo.pl                 A demo of some of the available features."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007368 "    formats.pl              All the available formatting on several worksheets."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007369 "    regions.pl              A simple example of multiple worksheets."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007370 "    stats.pl                Basic formulas and functions."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007371 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007372 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007373 "    Intermediate"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007374 "    ============"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00007375 "    autofit.pl              Examples of simulated worksheet autofit."
